 Comment| 3:33 am ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE, LIABILITY AND THE LAWARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE, LIABILITY AND THE LAW Read MoreRead More Intellectual Property Law Uncategorized August 14, 2026August 14, 2026| Peter AkhihieroPeter Akhihiero| 0 Comment| 3:27 am ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE AND PRIVACYARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE AND PRIVACY Read MoreRead More General Uncategorized August 14, 2026August 14, 2026| Peter AkhihieroPeter Akhihiero| 0 Comment| 3:21 am ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE AND LEGAL LIABILITY IN NIGERIAARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE AND LEGAL LIABILITY IN NIGERIA Read MoreRead More General Uncategorized August 14, 2026August 14, 2026| Peter AkhihieroPeter Akhihiero| 0 Comment| 3:16 am DISRUPTIVE ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCEDISRUPTIVE ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE Read MoreRead More Practice and Procedure Uncategorized August 14, 2026August 14, 2026| Peter AkhihieroPeter Akhihiero| 0 Comment| 3:10 am THE ROLE OF ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE IN LEGAL PRACTICETHE ROLE OF ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE IN LEGAL PRACTICE Read MoreRead More General Uncategorized August 14, 2026August 14, 2026| Peter AkhihieroPeter Akhihiero| 0 Comment| 2:29 am Artificial Intelligence and Law_ An OverviewArtificial Intelligence and Law_ An Overview Read MoreRead More Intellectual Property Law Uncategorized August 14, 2026August 14, 2026| Peter AkhihieroPeter Akhihiero| 0 Comment| 2:19 am AI IN CRIMINAL JUSTICEAI IN CRIMINAL JUSTICE Read MoreRead More Posts pagination 1 2 … 6 Next SearchSearchRecent PostsARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE IN COURT PROCEEDINGS MRS ITOHAN MERCY ERIOGHAYE V. MR UYI CHESTERSON ERIOGHAYE MR. SAMUEL DICKENS ORIE V. MR. DARREN QUANGBI & 2 ORS High Court of the FCT Civil Procedure Rules, 2025 REGULATING AI IN THE UNITED STATES ET AL Recent CommentsEthan Nason on AppealHero Chidozie on Laws of the Federation of NigeriaArchives October 2026 September 2026 August 2026 July 2026 June 2026 May 2026 April 2026 March 2026 February 2026 January 2026 December 2025 October 2025 September 2025 July 2025 June 2025 May 2025 April 2025 March 2025 February 2025 January 2025 December 2024 November 2024 October 2024 September 2024 August 2024 July 2024 June 2024 May 2024 April 2024 March 2024 January 2024 December 2023 November 2023 October 2023 August 2023 June 2023 May 2023 April 2023 March 2023 February 2023 January 2023 December 2022 November 2022 October 2022 July 2022 June 2022 May 2022 April 2022 March 2022 February 2022 January 2022 December 2021 November 2021 October 2021 September 2021 July 2021 June 2021 Categories Arbitration Articles Articles Blog Caselaw Digest Chieftaincy Matters Commercial Law Commercial Law Company Law Constitutional Law Constitutional Law Constitutions of Nations Criminal Law Criminal Law and Procedure Customary Law Customary Law and Procedure Election Cases Environmental Law Family Law Forms and Precedents General General Human Rights Law Human Rights Law Insurance Intellectual Property Law International Law Jurisprudence Labour Law Land Cases Land Law Laws of the Federation of Nigeria Legislations Matrimonial Cases Oil and Gas Oil and Gas Legislations Practice and Procedure Probate Matters Rules Rulings Statutes Torts Torts Law Uncategorized Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors LINKSHome Our Team Articles Arbitration Commercial Law Company Law Constitutional Law Criminal Law and Procedure Customary Law and Procedure Family Law Environmental Law General Human Rights Law Insurance Intellectual Property Law International Law Jurisprudence Labour Law Maritime Law Oil and Gas Practice and Procedure Revenue Law Torts HOW TO TENDER AND PLAY VIDEO EVIDENCE IN COURT Caselaw Digest Legislations Constitutions of Nations Laws of the Federation of Nigeria Oil and Gas Legislations Rules Statutes Judgments Chieftaincy Matters Commercial Law Constitutional Law Criminal Law Customary Law Election Cases Environmental Law General Human Rights Law Insurance Law Labour Law Land Cases Matrimonial Cases Probate Matters Rulings Torts Law Forms and Precedents Legal Extras Blog Articles Top 7 Mistakes Every Personal Injury Lawyer Should Avoid in Their Career Contact RECENT POSTS ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE IN COURT PROCEEDINGS MRS ITOHAN MERCY ERIOGHAYE V. MR UYI CHESTERSON ERIOGHAYE MR. SAMUEL DICKENS ORIE V. MR. DARREN QUANGBI & 2 ORS High Court of the FCT Civil Procedure Rules, 2025 REGULATING AI IN THE UNITED STATES ET AL ARCHIVES October 2026 (1) September 2026 (2) August 2026 (77) July 2026 (28) June 2026 (22) May 2026 (3) April 2026 (4) March 2026 (5) February 2026 (7) January 2026 (3) December 2025 (1) October 2025 (4) September 2025 (1) July 2025 (5) June 2025 (12) May 2025 (11) April 2025 (3) March 2025 (2) February 2025 (4) January 2025 (1) December 2024 (2) November 2024 (202) October 2024 (84) September 2024 (169) August 2024 (2) July 2024 (3) June 2024 (452) May 2024 (17) April 2024 (2) March 2024 (3) January 2024 (3) December 2023 (1) November 2023 (2) October 2023 (3) August 2023 (1) June 2023 (1) May 2023 (3) April 2023 (2) March 2023 (1) February 2023 (1) January 2023 (3) December 2022 (1) November 2022 (1) October 2022 (3) July 2022 (4) June 2022 (1) May 2022 (3) April 2022 (2) March 2022 (2) February 2022 (1) January 2022 (1) December 2021 (1) November 2021 (2) October 2021 (1) September 2021 (1) July 2021 (1) June 2021 (3) CALENDAR October 2026 M T W T F S S 1234 567891011 12131415161718 19202122232425 262728293031 « Sep Scroll to Top","title":"Category: Uncategorized","url":"https://nigerianlawguru.com/category/uncategorized/","id":309},{"content_hash":"d994c53ab854e89fa9c123e6ec0076b2c3f89f43392cc5cc5f744dca2dcde660","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:58.827260+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/customer-credit-check","language":"","text":"What is Customer Credit Check ByLola, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuApril 5, 2023•5mins ReadWhat Is A Customer Credit Check?Why A Customer Credit Check Is Important For BusinessesHow Are Customer Credit Checks Done?Share this postAlthough it may be perceived as relatively new, customer credit checks are essential measures for businesses in order to make informed decisions regarding customers. The cluster of words in the term gives a vague insight into what the process entails already. it’s about understanding a person’s credit score and financial reliability before extending any credit or service. A customer credit check is an important evaluation process. Reading further in this article will leave you better informed about customer credit checks and why it is truly important. But of course, a better explanation and definition of customer credit checks will be a better consequent section for this article. including how to check your credit score and interpret your credit report effectively. What Is A Customer Credit Check? This refers to a process that entails evaluating the creditworthiness of a person or business by reviewing the person's credit score. This process is undertaken to ensure that a customer will be able to repay any debt or credit they may be seeking from the company. The process involves a request for permission to review the person's credit report or that of a business from credit reporting agencies. The credit report provided will then provide information about the customer's or business's history and current credit balances. All of which shape the final credit score. With the information obtained, an informed decision is possible. The company decides whether to approve a loan, a credit card, or any other form of credit. A customer credit check is a constituent of the KYC process, which is of the customer onboarding process as well. There are two types of credit checks: 1. Soft CreditCheck This is a credit inquiry that does not affect the borrower's credit score. It is used by lenders for preapproval or prequalification purposes. It can also be used by landlords to verify a borrower's creditworthiness. Soft credit checks typically involve a review of a borrower's credit report without any deep analysis of information. 2. Hard Credit Check: A hard credit check, often referred to as a hard inquiry, is a credit inquiry that has the potential to lower a borrower's credit score. When a lender makes a credit determination, such as approving an application for a loan or credit card, it conducts a hard credit check. Before accepting services that demand recurring payments, other organizations like landlords, utility providers, or cell phone carriers might also run hard credit checks on potential customers. A borrower's credit report contains information on hard credit checks, and having several in a short period of time can harm their credit score. which is why many people now explore how to check their credit score for free before applying for credit. Why A Customer Credit Check Is Important For Businesses Customer Credit Checks are not only utilized by credit card companies or loan companies, or any other financial-related institution. It can also be utilized by other types of corporate companies and businesses that sell or provide services for payment. In order to prevent cash flow issues, customers can be assessed in order to determine whether they will default in payments or not. Asides from that, customer credit checks can also inform sales strategies. Some of the importance of a customer credit check for efficacy in business include; 1. Customer Credit Checks Help Mitigate Risks: It not only mitigates risks but identifies them; this is how risks are then mitigated because they have been initially mitigated. This means that risks are reduced. When credit checks are conducted, it reduces the risks of bad debts and non-payment. Credit checks enable a business to assess a customer's ability to pay back their debts and to pay for services as well. This way, businesses that offer credit can make informed decisions on whether to extend credit or not. 2. Customer Credit Checks Helps Businesses Make Better Decisions: Because credit reports are undertaken, businesses are able to gain valuable insights into a potential customer's behaviour. These include payment history and outstanding debts. Access to this sort of valuable information will help businesses make informed decisions about extending credit and setting credit limits. 3. Improves Customer Relationships: Customer credit checks will help businesses build trust with customers. In a case where credit reports come back with positive numerical, a company is able to see that the potential customer takes their financial obligations seriously. Companies can demonstrate a commitment to responsible lending practices; this will put a business in a positive light and therefore attract more customers. 4. Protects Cash Flow: Accumulating debts can have major negative impacts on a company's cash flow. By conducting credit checks, businesses can avoid extending credit to high-risk customers and ensure that customers do not default on payment. 5. Resources Can Be Effectively Managed: With credit checks; businesses can allocate resources more effectively. When high-risk customers are identified, they may require additional attention and monitoring. It can also help businesses avoid wasting time and resources on customers who may likely flaunt payments. 6. Targeting Creditworthy Customers: Credit Customer credit checks enable businesses to identify customers with solid credit histories and low defaulting on payments. This means they are more likely to be financially stable and have more resources to make larger purchases or engage in long-term relationships with businesses. Information obtained from customer credit checks can be useful to sales and marketing teams to target these beneficial potential customers and tailor their messaging and promotions to appeal to their needs and preferences. 7. Compliance: Certain industries or governments require businesses to perform credit checks on customers before extending credit to them. Failure to comply with this regulation attracts fines and legal issues. 8. Identifying Fraud: Customer credit checks help identify potential fraudsters or customers that may attempt to obtain credit with false information. This helps businesses avoid financial losses and maintain a solid reputation. How Are Customer Credit Checks Done? Customer Credit checks can be done by businesses, banks, and other financial institutions or entities to assess the creditworthiness of individuals or companies before extending credit to them or doing business with them. Customer Credit Checks involve multiple steps, which include; a. Obtaining the customer's personal information: Which includes the customer's full name, address, date of birth, driver's license, passport, and national id cards. b. Checking credit history: In Kenya, credit history is checked through the Credit Reference Bureau (CRB) system. The CRB system is a centralized database that collects and maintains credit information on individuals and businesses from various lenders, such as banks, microfinance institutions, and mobile lending platforms. c. Reviewing financial information: The customer's income, employment history, and other financial information may be requested to assess their ability to repay credit. d. Analyzing credit scores: Credit scores are generated based on the customer's credit history, and lenders typically use this information to determine the likelihood of default. e. Making a decision: Based on the information obtained, businesses can now make decisions that are beneficial for the company, taking into account many factors like cash flow. Bottom Line Customer credit checks are a substantial part of the KYC process and also seek to protect companies from customers who will not have favourable commercial relationships with them. It will therefore help companies flourish and grow while avoiding problems with cash flow. With the Youverify automated OS KYC processes can be done much more faster and efficiently. Book a demo session today to see how Youverify can help automate your customer credit check for a more efficient and cost-effective process. Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"What is Customer Credit Check","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/customer-credit-check","id":317},{"content_hash":"93b4621af398afbadedf5aa53adc3abf826e8caaca0b608e51fe160c62b28752","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:57.643592+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/26/dotun-oladipo-sudden-death/","language":"","text":"Dotun Oladipo: Why Does Sudden Death Take the Good and Spare the Wicked? By Yushau A. Shuaib - August 26, 2026 Dotun Oladipo Dotun Oladipo: Why Does Sudden Death Take the Good and Spare the Wicked? By Yushau A. Shuaib On the afternoon of Tuesday, August 25, 2026, I exchanged messages with Dotun Oladipo — respected journalist, former President of the Guild of Corporate Online Publishers (GOCOP), and publisher of The Eagle Online. I sent him a report on the unfolding political rivalry in Katsina State — between the camps of Governor Dikko Radda and Tinubu’s adviser, Ibrahim Masari — which allegedly led to the arrest of security aides attached to the governor — for publication on his platform. In his characteristically simple and professional manner, Dotun asked me to forward it to his Gmail address instead of the Yahoo account I had previously used. I obliged. Within minutes, the article was published, and a few hours later, he promoted it as the lead story in his WhatsApp news broadcast. That was Dotun: no bureaucracy, no excuses, no endless promises. If a material was newsworthy and suitable for his platform, he acted promptly. The next day, as I prepared to attend a programme, my son Gidado called. “Dotun Oladipo is trending everywhere,” he said. I smiled instinctively. When good people trend, one hopes it is for good reasons — an appointment, an award, a promotion, or another professional milestone. Then he completed the sentence. “They say he is dead.” “What? What!” I screamed. It struck like a thunderbolt. I immediately returned to our last conversation. I checked the messages. I looked again at the article he had published. Everything had happened less than twenty‑four hours earlier. How could a man who had just attended to a professional request suddenly become the subject of an obituary? Reports said Dotun died at 56 after suddenly taking ill at his office in Lagos. Colleagues said he had appeared well earlier in the day and had even gone to the gym before proceeding with his normal activities. He reportedly became unwell at work and sought medical attention, but he did not survive. Some reports suggested a heart attack, though the precise medical cause should be left to those competent to establish it. But in that moment of grief, my mind rebelled against the cold logic of mortality. Why does sudden death sometimes appear to pick the good ones — even those, like Dotun, who had just observed the physical exercise recommended for medical fitness? Why would it take a patriotic journalist, a gentleman, and a friend, while bandit kingpins, terrorist commanders, and others who inflict misery on innocent citizens continue to roam? Why do people who work for peace and national stability depart without warning, while those who threaten the peace seem to survive one violent episode after another? Of course, these are not questions mortals can answer. Death does not operate according to our understanding of fairness or fitness. My relationship with Dotun spanned many years. I came to know him more closely during Nigeria’s most challenging security period, when the Forum of Spokespersons of Security and Response Agencies (FOSSRA) was coordinated under Major General Chris Olukolade and supported by the Office of the National Security Adviser under Colonel Sambo Dasuki (Rtd). It was also the era when PRNigeria strengthened its role in security reporting and crisis communication. Read Also: FRSC, BATMedics Screen 500 Drivers to Mark World Sight Day Troops Foil Lakurawa Assault on Kebbi Military Base, Recover 17 Motorcycles, Weapons Troops Nab 9 Suspected Terrorist Collaborators in Yobe, Rescue 6 Hostages in Plateau Those were difficult years. Boko Haram dominated headlines, security agencies were under immense pressure, and public confidence was constantly shaken by conflicting reports and propaganda. The relationship between security institutions and the media was therefore critical. Journalists had to balance the public’s right to know with the responsibility not to compromise operations, while security agencies had to recognise that journalists were not adversaries simply because they asked hard questions. Dotun understood that delicate balance. He believed in journalism, but he also understood national responsibility. Our interactions continued long after that period. Until the article he published barely hours before his death — which he even promoted as the lead story in his final WhatsApp news broadcast — Dotun remained remarkably consistent. Whenever I sent him something newsworthy, he responded promptly. Sometimes it was purely professional; at other times, it carried the quiet solidarity colleagues extend to one another without making a ceremony of friendship. Dotun’s professional journey spanned nearly three decades across major Nigerian newsrooms — Nigerian Tribune, Nigerian Compass, The Sun, and PUNCH, where he served as Politics Editor — before establishing The Eagle Online. His contribution to digital journalism was significant. There was a time when online journalism in Nigeria was treated as the unruly younger cousin of traditional media — associated with sensationalism, poor verification, and weak gatekeeping. People like Dotun helped change that perception. Through The Eagle Online and his leadership in GOCOP, he belonged to a generation of publishers who proved that online journalism could be fast without being reckless, competitive without abandoning ethics, and independent while still respecting professional standards. He understood something that remains relevant today: the platform may change, but the fundamentals of journalism should not. Holding leaders accountable does not mean abandoning responsibility to the nation. Dotun represented the bridge between the traditional newsroom and the digital environment that now defines journalism. Beyond the profession, I remember the human being — a patriotic Nigerian, a dependable colleague, a gentleman who valued friendship and professional solidarity, and a publisher who could receive an article and, without drama, publish it within minutes if he considered it worthy. As I mourn Dotun Oladipo, I also reflect on the peculiar vulnerability of journalists. We chase deadlines, manage breaking news, respond to calls at odd hours, travel unexpectedly, confront professional pressures, and carry burdens audiences rarely see. We report everyone else’s emergencies while sometimes ignoring the warnings from our own bodies. The circumstances surrounding Dotun’s passing should remind media professionals — and indeed all of us — that health deserves attention even amid the pressure of deadlines and obligations. At 56, we shared the same age, and by human reckoning he left far too soon. Yet life is ultimately measured not by how long we stay, but by what we leave in the minds and lives of others. My brother Dotun, I still struggle to reconcile our last exchange with the obituary that followed. Yesterday, you were the publisher receiving my article; today, you are the subject of one. The deadline has finally come for a story none of us ever wished to publish. Good night, Dotun Oladipo — and with tears, I send this one last message to your favourite email, [email protected]. Yushau A. Shuaib Publisher, PRNigeria & Economic Confidential [email protected] VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSFOSSRAGOCOPGovernor Dikko RaddaIbrahim Masari Previous articleAdeniyi Reaffirms Customs’ Commitment to Humanitarian Relief, Receives International AwardNext articleFG Unveils 100 Electric Buses to Ease Civil Servants’ Transport Burden Yushau A. Shuaib Xing","title":"Dotun Oladipo: Why Does Sudden Death Take the Good and Spare the Wicked?","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/26/dotun-oladipo-sudden-death/","id":324},{"content_hash":"db4306bd81e525fe25d287530714f2537ef6dd1d80022f709fecbdb63286e864","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:55.172872+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-ekyc","language":"","text":"What Is eKYC? ByEmmanuel AgwuApril 5, 2023•5mins ReadWhat Does eKYC Mean?How is eKYC Process Carried Out?Why eKYC is The Future Of KYC?BottomlineShare this postSince The emergence of Know Your Customer as a precautionary measure with financial institutions, fintech, and businesses, advanced versions of KYC like perpetual KYC and eKYC, eKYC is a relatively new concept. It can be a great way to execute the KYC process better. This article aims to highlight eKYC as a progressive of the KYC process, discussing all that needs to be known about eKYc. What Does eKYC Mean? eKYC is an acronym for Electronic Know Your Customer and refers to the remote, paperless process that minimizes the costs and traditional bureaucracy that are employed in KYC processes. It enables businesses and organizations to verify the identity of their customers in a more efficient and secure manner without the need for physical documents or face-to-face interactions. eKYC can be used to authenticate customers and meet regulatory requirements in a variety of businesses, including banking, insurance, telecom, and e-commerce. It entails retrieving people's personal information from the government database and confirming their identities using Aadhaar-based authentication methods, such as biometric scanners and OTP (one-time password) verification. The information is subsequently utilized to open accounts, offer services, or carry out other necessary transactions. Related: What is a Qualified Electronic Signature How is eKYC Process Carried Out? In Kenya, the eKYC process is primarily used in the financial sector to verify the identity of customers and comply with regulatory requirements. Failure to meet up with these regulations may attract legal issues. The eKYC process in Kenya involves the use of various digital tools, such as biometric identification, mobile phones, and online platforms, to verify the identity of individuals. The process is generally used by financial institutions, such as banks, insurance companies, and microfinance institutions, to comply with regulatory requirements while providing a seamless customer experience. To perform eKYC in Kenya, customers typically provide their personal information, such as their name, address, and date of birth, along with their government-issued ID number. This information is then verified against the government's database through the use of biometric identification, such as fingerprints or facial recognition. The eKYC process in Kenya has several benefits, which include increased security, reduced fraud, and improved customer experience. By using digital tools to verify customer identity, financial institutions can ensure that their customers are who they claim to be, reducing the risk of fraud and increasing security. Additionally, eKYC can be performed quickly and easily, providing a seamless customer experience. This includes: 1. Customer onboarding The potential customer is required to provide their personal information, such as their name, address, and date of birth, along with their government-issued ID number. This can be done through an online form or mobile app. Many businesses today employ perpetual Know Your Customer processes to stay up to date with emerging customer risks. 2. Biometric verification In biometric verification, the customer's biometric data, such as fingerprints or facial recognition, is collected and verified against the government's database. This way, authentication of an individual's identity can be carried out through a digital process. 3. ID verification In ID verification, the customer's ID information is verified against the government's database, and any discrepancies are flagged for further review. 4. Risk assessment The customer's risk profile is assessed based on their personal information and transaction history. This is used to determine the level of due diligence required for the customer. 5. Verification completion Once the customer's identity is verified and their risk profile is assessed, the eKYC process is complete. The customer can then access the financial institution's services, such as opening a bank account or applying for a loan. The eKYC process in Kenya is facilitated by various digital tools, such as mobile apps, online platforms, and biometric verification devices. These tools are used to securely collect and verify customer information, ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements while providing a seamless customer experience. It's worth noting that the eKYC process may vary slightly depending on the financial institution and the specific requirements of the regulatory authorities. However, the overall process typically follows the steps outlined above. Recommended - Customer Credit Check - All You Need to Know Why eKYC is The Future Of KYC? Electronic Know Your Customer (eKYC) is considered the future of KYC (Know Your Customer) because it offers several advantages over traditional KYC processes. a. Speed and convenience: eKYC allows for quick verification of customer identity through electronic channels, such as online or mobile platforms. This eliminates the need for customers to physically visit a bank or financial institution to complete the KYC process, saving time and effort. b. Cost-effectiveness: eKYC significantly reduces the cost of the KYC process by eliminating the need for manual data entry and verification. This is especially important for small businesses and startups that may not have the resources to invest in expensive KYC solutions. c. Increased accuracy: eKYC reduces the risk of errors in the KYC process by using automated data validation and verification checks. This helps ensure that the information provided by the customer is accurate and up-to-date. d. Enhanced security: eKYC uses advanced security measures, such as biometric authentication and encryption, to protect sensitive customer data from fraud and identity theft. e. Compliance: eKYC helps financial institutions comply with regulatory requirements, such as Anti-Money Laundering (AML) and Counter-Terrorism Financing (CTF) regulations. f. Digital transformation: With the increasing digitization of financial services, eKYC enables financial institutions to offer a fully digital customer onboarding experience. This can help attract tech-savvy customers who prefer to conduct their financial transactions online. g. Improved customer experience: eKYC offers a seamless customer experience, allowing customers to complete the KYC process from the comfort of their own homes or offices. This can help improve customer satisfaction and loyalty. h. Scalability: eKYC can be easily scaled to accommodate large volumes of customers, making it ideal for businesses that experience rapid growth. I. Flexibility: eKYC can be customized to meet the specific needs of different financial institutions, depending on their customer base and regulatory requirements. j. Reduced fraud: eKYC uses advanced fraud detection techniques, such as facial recognition and document authentication, to reduce the risk of fraudulent activity. This way, resources are managed more effectively, and customers and businesses are at lesser risk. k. Accessible Digital Tools Available: There are many accessible digital tools available for paperless KYC that can be easily utilized. Youverify offers automatable eKYC solutions that help businesses perform adequate customer risk assessment and satisfy compliance. Bottomline eKYC offers numerous benefits over traditional KYC processes, making it a popular choice among financial institutions looking to streamline customer onboarding procedures while ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements. With the increasing digitization of financial services, eKYC is expected to become the norm in the near future. See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for eKYC screening of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today.Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"What Is eKYC?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-ekyc","id":333},{"content_hash":"676029780225e0e6c07e08d4e68f7d4a049d59980e0c7e9d5517876021ba8268","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:54.179016+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/26/unveils-electric-buses-ease/","language":"","text":"FG Unveils 100 Electric Buses to Ease Civil Servantsâ Transport Burden By Mukhtar Y - August 26, 2026 FG Unveils 100 Electric Buses to Ease Civil Servantsâ Transport Burden The Federal Government has inaugurated 100 Renewed Hope Mass Transit electric buses as part of efforts to provide safer, more affordable and reliable transportation for federal civil servants across the country. The Head of the Civil Service of the Federation, Didi Walson-Jack, disclosed this on Wednesday in Abuja during the inauguration and symbolic handover of the buses. Walson-Jack described the initiative as a practical demonstration of the Federal Governmentâs commitment to improving the welfare and wellbeing of civil servants. She disclosed that 37 of the 100 buses had been delivered as the first phase of the project. âFor me, it is a dream come true. This inauguration is providentially my parting gift to my dearly beloved federal civil servants,â she said. The Head of Service, whose tenure ends on Thursday, said the inauguration provided a fitting conclusion to her tenure, particularly as the initiative would continue to benefit civil servants after her departure. She expressed appreciation to President Bola Ahmed Tinubu for his support for the project and his broader efforts to transform the Federal Civil Service. According to her, transportation is more than the movement of workers from one location to another, as daily commuting directly affects employeesâ finances, safety, punctuality and productivity. âAn intervention that makes transportation safer, more reliable and more affordable is therefore not a peripheral welfare gesture; it is an investment in a more productive civil service,â she said. Walson-Jack said the electric buses would help reduce workersâ transportation costs and mitigate some of the risks associated with daily commuting. She added that the initiative demonstrated the translation of the administrationâs Renewed Hope Agenda into practical interventions with direct benefits for workers. âThe acquisition of the buses is made possible through the Renewed Hope Infrastructure Development Fund,â she said. Read Also: FRSC, BATMedics Screen 500 Drivers to Mark World Sight Day Troops Foil Lakurawa Assault on Kebbi Military Base, Recover 17 Motorcycles, Weapons Troops Nab 9 Suspected Terrorist Collaborators in Yobe, Rescue 6 Hostages in Plateau She commended the Minister of State for Industry, John Enoh, for his support for the project and for undertaking the symbolic handover of the buses. Walson-Jack also commended the Director-General of the National Automotive Design and Development Council, Joseph Osanipin, and his team for their collaboration in ensuring that the buses were developed in accordance with the objectives of Nigeriaâs national automotive policy. She stressed that staff welfare remained a critical component of public service reform, noting that a motivated and supported workforce would be better positioned to discharge its responsibilities with professionalism, commitment and excellence. The Head of Service urged civil servants to protect and maintain the buses, describing them as valuable government assets provided for their benefit. âMy dearly beloved civil servants, treat these buses as your own personal assets. They are assets that have been provided for you all,â she said. Enoh: Buses will ease workersâ transportation challenges Earlier, Enoh described the inauguration as a significant milestone in the Federal Governmentâs efforts to improve the welfare of civil servants. He said deploying the electric buses would help address transportation challenges confronting workers while advancing the administrationâs broader sustainability and development objectives. The minister said the initiative was part of the governmentâs efforts to translate its policies into tangible interventions capable of improving the daily lives of Nigerians. He commended Walson-Jack for her contributions to the Federal Civil Service and wished her well as she concludes her tenure as Head of Service. Enoh said the 37 buses already delivered represented the first phase of the 100-bus project, with the remaining vehicles expected to be deployed in line with the implementation plan. The initiative is expected to provide federal civil servants with a more affordable and reliable commuting option while supporting the governmentâs broader push for sustainable transportation and improved public-sector productivity. VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSCivil Servantselectric vehicleTinubuTransportation Previous articleDotun Oladipo: Why Does Sudden Death Take the Good and Spare the Wicked?Next article2027: Beyond Certificates and Other Rehashed Nothingness, Nigerians Deserve an Issue-based CampaignÂ Mukhtar Y","title":"FG Unveils 100 Electric Buses to Ease Civil Servantsâ Transport Burden","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/26/unveils-electric-buses-ease/","id":340},{"content_hash":"eaaa45d2b91a0250fb1307199b7ea115fbda5824c515cf4369b6080d0d5184dc","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:51.442460+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/perpetual-kyc-the-future-of-compliance","language":"","text":"What is Perpetual KYC - The Future of Compliance? ByLola, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuApril 5, 2023•5mins ReadWhat is Perpetual KYC?What Processes Does Perpetual KYC or even-Driven KYC EntailWhy Is Perpetual KYC The Future Of Compliance?Bottom LineShare this postPerpetual compliance (pKYC) is a new Know Your Customer (KYC) measure approach. It emphasizes the need for proactiveness in KYC instead of being reactive, as well as the continuous monitoring of customers. It also involves periodic KYC reviews for those triggered by abnormal customer behaviour patterns. Perpetual KYC has been regarded as the next big thing for compliance; is it really the next big thing? What is perpetual KYC? This article seeks to examine what perpetual KYC is in detail. What is Perpetual KYC? What is Perpetual KYC? It is also known as continuous, dynamic, and event-driven KYC. Perpetual KYC is abbreviated as pKYC. pKYC is a brand new approach to due fill that emphasizes the rapidly advancing financial markets in financial institutions and business entities that now exist. So, instead of working reactively, the new approach is based on continuous monitoring for events that may alert an institution about the need to review and update customer information. Perpetual KYC (Know Your Customer) refers to a continuous or ongoing process of verifying and monitoring the identity and activities of a customer. Unlike traditional KYC, which is performed initially only once during customer onboarding, perpetual KYC involves regular updates to customer information and monitoring of their behaviour, transactions, and risk profile. It is critical to adopt pKYC in industries with high regulatory requirements, such as finance and banking, where detecting and preventing money laundering, fraud, and other financial crimes is important. Businesses can continuously monitor customer activity to identify suspicious behaviour and take appropriate action to mitigate risk. Perpetual KYC usually involves the use of technology and automation to streamline the process of collecting, verifying, and updating customer information. This can include the use of data analytics, artificial intelligence, and machine learning to identify patterns and anomalies in customer behaviour, as well as the integration of data from multiple sources to create a complete picture of each customer's risk profile. What Processes Does Perpetual KYC or even-Driven KYC Entail Perpetual KYC is an important regulatory requirement for many industries, particularly those in the financial sector, to prevent money laundering, fraud, and other financial crimes. The processes involved in perpetual KYC typically include the following: 1. Customer Identification: This involves collecting and verifying the customer's identity at the beginning of the relationship using various identity documents such as passports, photocard driving licenses, or national identity cards. 2. Perpetual KYC Customer Due Diligence: Perpetual KYC due diligence is the process of assessing the customer's risk profile and verifying the source of their funds or wealth. It involves reviewing the customer's background, financial history, and activities to identify any future risks. 3. Continuous Monitoring: This involves continuously monitoring the customer's activities and transactions for suspicious or unusual behaviour. It helps to detect any changes in the customer's risk profile or activity that may warrant further investigation. This further illustrates the proactive approach of pKYC or event-driven KYC, as though the p in the abbreviation stands for proactive 4. Risk Assessment: This involves periodically reassessing the customer's risk profile based on changes in their behaviour or circumstances, such as a change in their employment status, a significant increase in their transactions, or a change in their location. 5. Enhanced Due Diligence: This is a more intense or rigorous form of perpetual KYC due diligence that may be required for customers who are considered high-risk, such as politically exposed persons (PEPs) or customers from high-risk jurisdictions. This is referred to as enhanced due diligence. It involves gathering additional information and conducting more frequent monitoring to mitigate the higher risk. 6. Record Keeping: This involves maintaining comprehensive and up-to-date customer information and interactions records, including identification documents, account opening forms, transaction history, and any correspondence with the customer. These records are important for audit and regulatory purposes. 7. Customer Screening: This is the process of screening customers against various watchlists, such as sanctions lists or politically exposed persons (PEP) lists. It helps to identify any customers who may pose a higher risk of financial crime. 8. Training and Awareness: Perpetual KYC requires a well-trained and knowledgeable workforce that can effectively understand and apply the company's policies and procedures. This involves providing staff with regular training and awareness programs to ensure they stay up-to-date with regulatory requirements and emerging risks. 9. Reporting: Perpetual KYC requires companies to report suspicious transactions or activity to the relevant authorities. This involves implementing robust reporting procedures and maintaining effective communication channels with law enforcement and regulatory bodies. 10. Technology and Automation: Perpetual KYC can be a labour-intensive process, especially for larger organizations. As a result, many companies are leveraging technology and automation to streamline the process and improve efficiency. This includes the use of artificial intelligence and machine learning algorithms to analyze customer data and identify potential risks. pKYC is a diverse process encompassing numerous stakeholders and procedures, such as compliance teams, risk managers, IT departments, and personnel interacting with customers. Companies can better safeguard themselves and their customers against financial crime and reputational harm by developing efficient perpetual KYC processes. Recommended - What Is eKYC? Why Is Perpetual KYC The Future Of Compliance? The pKYC approach to KYC is gaining momentum. It is becoming quite popular in the compliance industry due to its ability to provide continuous monitoring and risk management and meet evolving regulatory requirements. Experts have solid reasons why pKYC should replace a single initial KYC with traditional KYC. There are several reasons why perpetual KYC is considered the future of compliance, which includes: 1. Enhanced risk management: Perpetual KYC provides ongoing monitoring of customer behaviour and transactions, which enables financial institutions to identify and mitigate potential risks in real-time. This approach allows organizations to identify high-risk customers early and take appropriate measures to mitigate risks. 2. Compliance with evolving regulations: Regulatory requirements are constantly changing, and organizations need to keep up with the latest compliance requirements to avoid fines and penalties. Perpetual KYC ensures continuous compliance with evolving regulations, which saves time and resources in the long run. 3. Improved customer experience: Perpetual KYC eliminates the need for customers to provide their identification and other documentation repeatedly. This improves the overall customer experience, as customers only need to provide their information once, and updates are made automatically. 4. Cost-effective: Perpetual KYC is more cost-effective than traditional KYC processes, which are often manual and require significant resources. Perpetual KYC automates the process, reducing the need for manual intervention and increasing efficiency. Bottom Line Perpetual KYC is the future of compliance because it provides ongoing monitoring and risk management, ensures compliance with evolving regulations, improves the customer experience, and is cost-effective. By implementing perpetual KYC, organizations can streamline compliance processes and stay ahead of regulatory requirements. See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for pKYC and Customer Risk Assessment for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today.Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"What is Perpetual KYC - The Future of Compliance?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/perpetual-kyc-the-future-of-compliance","id":353},{"content_hash":"92dbee306acd3adb6abcb147f7781932bccba92b7fafaf7b7654768b0f2dc3e5","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:49.691555+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/26/beyond-certificates-rehashed/","language":"","text":"2027: Beyond Certificates and Other Rehashed Nothingness, Nigerians Deserve an Issue-based CampaignÂ By Kabir Akintayo - August 26, 2026 Atiku Abubakar, President Bola Ahmed Tinubu and Peter Obi 2027: Beyond Certificates and Other Rehashed Nothingness, Nigerians Deserve an Issue-based CampaignÂ By Kabir Akintayo Nigeria is once again approaching that familiar political season when presidential campaigns risk becoming less about the future and more about the past. Instead of asking who can fix the economy, secure the country, create jobs or keep the lights on, political actors may once again spend precious months arguing over certificates, academic records, old court cases and controversies that have already dominated previous elections, that would be a disservice to Nigerians. The 2027 presidential election should be different. It should be an election of ideas, records, policies and measurable commitments. Candidates should be compelled to tell Nigerians not only what they have done, but what they intend to do differently. The campaign season formally got underway in August, with major political parties and presidential candidates beginning to activate their campaign structures. The All Progressives Congress (APC), which is seeking President Bola Ahmed Tinubuâs re-election, unveiled a 2027 Presidential Campaign Council with 223 members. The list generated controversy, particularly over the inclusion of serving public officials, including officials occupying sensitive government positions. Even though the list, according to reports was withdrawn for further review, but that controversy, important as it may be for questions about conflict of interest and the use of public office, should not define the presidential contest. More importantly, the opposition must resist the temptation to allow the campaign to become another certificate war. For example, Peter Obi, presidential candidate of the National Democratic Congress (NDC), while declaring his campaign kick-off on Friday, said that Nigerians should take advantage of the campaign period to examine each candidateâs academic records and proposals and ask âtough questions.â The certificate controversy was already one of the dominant issues of the 2023 election. Yet President Tinubu emerged from that election and subsequently assumed office. Whatever oneâs opinion about the controversy, reopening the same argument four years later without connecting it to a broader assessment of governance risks turning the 2027 election into a referendum on old controversies rather than a contest over Nigeriaâs future. INEC has also published the personal particulars and academic credentials submitted by presidential candidates in accordance with the electoral framework. The real question now should therefore be: What will each candidate do with the enormous powers of the Nigerian presidency? That is where the opposition should concentrate its fire. Subsidy removal: Follow the money Few issues offer a better opportunity for an issue-based campaign than the removal of petrol subsidy. The government has consistently defended the policy as necessary to save resources and prevent the country from sinking deeper into fiscal crisis. The latest government figures provide opposition parties with an opportunity to ask legitimate and uncomfortable questions. Finance Minister and Coordinating Minister of the Economy, Taiwo Oyedele, recently disclosed that reforms involving subsidy removal and foreign-exchange liberalisation generated an estimated â¦15.8 trillion in resources for the Federation between June 2023 and December 2025. The Federal Government received about â¦5.43 trillion, states â¦6.52 trillion and local governments â¦3.88 trillion. Oyedele also said the Federal Government generated about â¦20.4 trillion in incremental resources when other revenues and borrowing were included, while additional expenditure reached approximately â¦30.64 trillion. This is precisely where an intelligent opposition campaign should begin. Where did the money go? What measurable improvements have Nigerians received in return for the enormous sacrifice imposed by subsidy removal? How much was spent on infrastructure, social intervention, transportation, healthcare, education and poverty reduction? What independent mechanism can verify the governmentâs figures? Rather than simply declaring subsidy removal a failure, the opposition should demand an independent audit of the savings, allocations and expenditure. It should present its own alternative: If subsidy is not returning, what replaces it? If it should return, how will the government finance it without recreating the fiscal problems that led to its removal? Electricity should become an election test Electricity is another area where the 2027 campaign can become a genuine accountability contest. The administration inherited a deeply troubled power sector and has implemented reforms, including the Electricity Act and measures aimed at strengthening the national grid. The Federal Government itself acknowledged in June that the sector had suffered from generation shortages, unreliable gas supply, fragile transmission infrastructure and significant legacy debts. Read Also: COAS Tasks Senior Commanders on Strategic Leadership Amidst Evolving Security Threats EFCCâs Asset Recovery Hits N673bn in Three Years, Says Olukoyede 2027: Beyond Certificates and Other Rehashed Nothingness, Nigerians Deserve an Issue-based CampaignÂ But Nigerians are ultimately interested in one thing: Does electricity work? The President made electricity a major part of his 2023 political pitch, including a pledge that Nigerians should hold him accountable if he failed to deliver on his promise. The Presidency has since argued that the promise was misrepresented and has pointed to reforms in the sector. That disagreement should not be settled by propaganda, but figures. How many hours of electricity does the average Nigerian receive today compared with 2023? What is the actual generation capacity? How much electricity is lost in transmission and distribution? What is the metering gap? What has been done with the billions allocated to the sector? What is the roadmap to reliable electricity? The opposition should put those questions before Nigerians and offer a credible alternative. Cost of living is bigger than certificates For millions of Nigerians, the most important political issue is not whether a politician attended a particular school. It is whether they can afford food. The International Monetary Fund said in its 2026 assessment that conditions remained difficult for many Nigerians, with poverty estimated at 63 per cent and about 27 million people estimated to have experienced food insecurity in late 2025. It also warned that higher fuel and food prices could worsen the situation. This gives presidential candidates a wide policy field. They should be debating how to reduce food prices without destroying farmersâ incentives; how to improve agricultural productivity; how to reduce transportation costs; how to make the naira more stable; how to create productive employment; and how to protect poor Nigerians while pursuing economic reforms. The question should not simply be, âWhat certificate do you have?â It should be: âWhat is your economic plan, how much will it cost, and how will you pay for it?â Security cannot be pushed to the background . Security should arguably be the biggest issue of the 2027 election. Recent mass kidnappings and continuing attacks have demonstrated that insecurity remains a serious national challenge. The latest incidents in Niger State, including a major mass abduction reported on Monday, have again raised questions about the ability of the state to protect vulnerable communities. Every presidential candidate should therefore be required to present a detailed security doctrine. How will the next government defeat banditry? What happens to ransom payments? How will intelligence gathering be improved? What is the strategy for securing rural communities, schools, highways and farms? How will security agencies be better equipped without creating avenues for corruption? And perhaps most importantly, what institutional reforms will ensure that security policy survives changes in government? These questions deserve more campaign airtime than scandals from decades ago. Debt, taxation and public accountability Nigeriaâs rising fiscal pressures also demand serious debate. The next president will inherit an economy where debt servicing, revenue mobilisation and infrastructure spending must be carefully balanced. The IMF has warned about limited fiscal space, high interest-to-revenue pressures and the need to strengthen budget processes, fiscal reporting, transparency and accountability. Candidates should therefore publish their position on taxation, borrowing and public expenditure. Will they borrow more? At what interest rate? For what projects? What happens when revenue falls short? Will they publish a comprehensive register of government borrowing and major projects? How will they reduce leakages? The recently introduced tax reforms also deserve serious debate. Candidates should tell Nigerians whether they support them, which aspects they would retain, which they would amend and how their preferred tax system would protect businesses and low-income households. A credible opposition candidate should be able to say: This is what the government did; this is where it failed; this is what we would change; this is what it would cost; and this is how Nigerians would benefit. That is what presidential politics should look like. For example, opposition candidates should be able to tell Nigerians about a policy like NELFUND. Is it a good policy, will you scrap it, review it or expand it? How about the Petroleum Industry Act which has deregulated the downstream sector of the petroleum industry? The candidate who has promised to return fuel subsidy immediately after swearing-in must tell Nigerians how he intends to do that without amending the PIA. He has to tell voters whether he plans to convince the National Assembly to amend the law or he wants to run a lawless administration. And when the campaign ends, voters should be able to choose not simply the candidate with the most damaging allegation against an opponent, but the candidate with the most convincing plan for making Nigeria work. Kabir Akintayo is a journalist and a public affairs analyst. He writes from Abuja, and can be reached at [email protected] VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSAPCNDCPeter ObiTinubu Previous articleFG Unveils 100 Electric Buses to Ease Civil Servantsâ Transport BurdenNext articleEFCCâs Asset Recovery Hits N673bn in Three Years, Says Olukoyede Kabir Akintayo Xing","title":"2027: Beyond Certificates and Other Rehashed Nothingness, Nigerians Deserve an Issue-based CampaignÂ","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/26/beyond-certificates-rehashed/","id":360},{"content_hash":"e8e1d23af3f9f280f04500001d29b37a103a082fc259cacf83ea057391cdb3c8","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:47.367593+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/id-verification-in-kenya","language":"","text":"ID Verification in Kenya: How to Check Your ID Status Online in KenyaByTemitope LawalApril 4, 2023•5mins ReadKey Takeaways1. You can check your ID status online in Kenya through the eCitizen portal or by sending your waiting card serial number via SMS to the official Huduma short code.2. Kenya's identity system is transitioning to the Maisha Card and Maisha Namba, which replaced the paused Huduma Namba/NIIMS rollout after August 20243. If your ID is lost, stolen, or damaged, you'll need to apply for an ID replacement separately; this is a different process from checking the status of a new ID application.How to Check Your ID Status Online in Kenya (2026 Update)Quick Answer: Check Your ID Status in KenyaWhere to Find Your Waiting Card Serial NumberHow to Check ID Status in Kenya by SMSMaisha Namba and the Maisha Card: What Changed in Kenya's ID System \"Since 2024How to Check ID Status Online in KenyaHow to Check If Your ID Is Ready for CollectionHow Long Does It Take to Get a National ID in Kenya?Checking Your ID Status at the Chief's or DO's OfficeWhat If Your ID Was Lost, Stolen, or Damaged?How to Do a Kenyan ID Number CheckWhat is ID Verification in Kenya?What is IPRS ID Verification Kenya?What is the use of IPRS ID Verification Kenya?ID Verification Methods for Businesses in Kenya?How to Choose a KYC and ID Verification Provider in KenyaVerify IDs in Kenya with YouverifyHow to Choose a KYC Solution Provider for ID Verification in KenyaHow Businesses Verify Kenyan IDs at Scale (vs. Manual Checking) Outline:Achieve a Seamless ID Verification In Kenya with YouverifyShare this post How to Check Your ID Status Online in Kenya (2026 Update) To check your ID status online in Kenya, send the 9-digit serial number on your waiting card by SMS to 20031, or enter the same serial number on the National ID status portal at identity.go.ke/search. Both methods tell you whether your ID card is still processing or ready for collection. SMS is the most reliable option, because the online portal is sometimes unavailable. Quick Answer: Check Your ID Status in Kenya By SMS (most reliable):Open a new text message.Type only the 9-digit serial number printed on your waiting card.Send it to 20031.You receive a reply from 20031 with your ID status.The SMS costs about KES 5, and the reply can occasionally take up to a few days during busy periods. If it delays, resend the same serial number.Online:Go to identity.go.ke/search.Open the National ID section.Select Status.Enter your 9-digit waiting card serial number.Submit to view your status.If the online portal does not load or returns nothing, use the SMS method instead. Portal availability has been inconsistent, and SMS to 20031 is the method that works most consistently. Where to Find Your Waiting Card Serial NumberYou cannot check your ID status without your waiting card serial number, so this is the first thing to locate.The waiting card is the paper slip issued to you at the Huduma Centre or National Registration Bureau (NRB) office immediately after your fingerprints and photo were captured. Printed on it is a unique 9-digit serial number tied to your application. That number is what both the SMS service and the online portal use to look up your status. Keep the waiting card safe and dry: it is your only proof of a pending application, and you must surrender it when you collect your finished ID.If you have lost your waiting card, you will need to visit the Huduma Centre or NRB office where you applied to retrieve your serial number, as it is not recoverable by name online. How to Check ID Status in Kenya by SMSThe SMS method works on any phone, needs no internet, and is the method most Kenyans rely on because it does not depend on the portal being online.Create a new text message.Type only your 9-digit waiting card serial number, with no extra words.Send the message to 20031.Wait for the reply from 20031, which states whether your ID is still being processed or is ready for collection.Keep at least KES 5 of airtime on your line. If you do not get a reply immediately, the system is sometimes slow, so wait and resend the same serial number after a while. Maisha Namba and the Maisha Card: What Changed in Kenya's ID System \"Since 2024 Kenya’s digital identity program is transitioning from the older second-generation national ID system toward the Maisha ecosystem, which includes Maisha Namba, a lifelong unique personal identifier, and the chip-enabled Maisha Card. The program faced court challenges and delays, and reporting in 2024 indicates the rollout moved forward again after legal hurdles were cleared. How to Check ID Status Online in KenyaIf you prefer to check on a browser, use the National ID status portal. Visit identity.go.ke/search.Click into the National ID section.Select Status.Enter the 9-digit serial number from your waiting card.Submit to see whether your ID is ready. One honest caveat: the online national ID search portal is not always available, and at times it has been down for extended periods. If the page fails to load or returns no result, this is a known issue with the portal rather than a problem with your application. Fall back to the SMS method to 20031, which is more consistently available. How to Check If Your ID Is Ready for CollectionBoth the SMS and online methods return one of two outcomes. Either your ID is still being processed, or it is ready for collection.Once the status shows ready, collect your ID from the same office where you applied, whether that is a Huduma Centre, the National Registration Bureau, or your local Chief's or District Officer's (DO's) office. Bring your waiting card, because you must hand it over to receive your ID. First-time IDs are free to collect.If weeks have passed and your status has not changed, it is worth visiting the office in person. Occasionally a card is ready but the status has not updated, and staff can confirm on the spot. How Long Does It Take to Get a National ID in Kenya?For most first-time applicants, a Kenyan national ID takes roughly two to four weeks to process after biometric capture, though official guidance sometimes quotes about 10 working days. The actual wait depends on application volumes and how quickly cards are printed and distributed to collection points.Because timelines vary, the practical approach is to start checking your status by SMS to 20031 about two weeks after your biometrics were taken, then check every few days until it shows ready. Checking Your ID Status at the Chief's or DO's OfficeBefore SMS and online checks existed, the only way to check an ID status was to visit the local Chief's or District Officer's office in person. This method still works and remains useful in rural areas with limited network coverage.Bring your waiting card to the office, and staff can look up whether your ID has arrived for collection. For most people, though, the SMS method to 20031 is faster and avoids the trip. What If Your ID Was Lost, Stolen, or Damaged?The steps above are for checking a new ID application. If your existing ID was lost, stolen, or damaged, that is a different process called ID replacement, handled through the National Registration Bureau and the eCitizen portal, and it involves a police abstract and a separate fee.For the full replacement process, see our dedicated guide on how to replace a lost or damaged ID card in Kenya. How to Do a Kenyan ID Number CheckA Kenyan ID number check confirms that an ID number is valid and matches the person presenting it, by checking it against the government's population register. This is different from checking your ID application status, which tracks a pending card.For individuals, your own ID details sit in your eCitizen profile once your ID is issued. For businesses, verifying a customer's ID number is done against the Integrated Population Registration System (IPRS), explained below, usually through a licensed verification provider rather than manually. Identity verification in Kenya has become increasingly important as more businesses shift towards online transactions and remote operations in Kenya, making it essential to have a reliable way to verify identities. For instance, many organizations now recommend a Kenyan ID number check before proceeding with any transaction. Also, digital commerce in Kenya is expected to grow annually between 13% and 16.4%, anticipating a market worth over $1 billion by 2029, driving demand for robust digital ID. Quick Answer: How to Check Your ID Status Online in KenyaIf you simply want to check my ID status online in Kenya right now, here's the fastest way:Visit the official eCitizen ID status portal.Go to the \"National ID\" section.Enter your waiting card serial number.Click \"Submit\" to view your status.Alternatively, send your serial number via SMS to the official Huduma short code. Kenya, like many other countries, has established regulations and protocols to ensure that individuals and businesses can authenticate their identities for legal and compliance reasons. In this blog post, we will explore the ID verification landscape in Kenya, including IPRD ID verification in Kenya, and some FAQs, such as how to check ID verification status in Kenya. Additionally, some services even offer a search ID number name in Kenya feature to assist users who may not have their ID number readily available. What is ID Verification in Kenya? Id verification in Kenya refers to the process of confirming the identity of an individual by verifying their personal information against the Kenyan government's official records and databases. The process is usually carried out by government agencies, financial institutions, and other organizations that require proof of identity from their clients or customers. In Kenya, the government has put in place various measures and systems for ID verification, including the National Integrated Identity Management System (NIIMS), commonly known as Huduma Namba, which is a unique identification number that is assigned to all Kenyan citizens and residents. This system collects biometric and personal information from individuals, such as fingerprints and facial recognition, ensuring a thorough Kenyan ID number search when needed. Financial institutions in Kenya also use identity verification systems to comply with the Know Your Customer (KYC) regulations. KYC requires that banks and other financial institutions verify the identity of their customers before providing them with services or products. In summary, id verification in Kenya involves verifying an individual's personal information against official records to ensure that they are who they claim to be. The process is used by various organizations for different purposes, including government programs, financial transactions, and access to various services. What is IPRS ID Verification Kenya? The Integrated Population Registration System (IPRS) in Kenya is a computerized database that collects and stores demographic data on Kenyan citizens and residents. It is used to maintain a comprehensive record of the population of Kenya, which includes personal identification details, biometric data, and other relevant information. This system serves as a robust IPRS ID verification Kenya tool. The IPRS is managed by the National Registration Bureau (NRB), which is a government agency under the Ministry of Interior and Coordination of National Government. The system is designed to provide a unique identification number to every citizen and resident in Kenya and is used for various government programs and services, such as voter registration, census taking, social services provision, and identity verification. In essence, the IPRS also functions as an effective IPRS checker for verifying identities across different sectors. What is the use of IPRS ID Verification Kenya? The Integrated Population Registration System (IPRS) in Kenya is used for identity verification purposes by various organizations, including government agencies, financial institutions, and other private entities. The system collects and stores personal identification details and biometric data of Kenyan citizens and residents, which can be used to verify an individual's identity. The use of IPRS id verification in Kenya is aimed at reducing the risk of identity fraud and improving security. The system enables organizations to confirm that the personal details provided by an individual match those in the government's database, making it easier to verify the identity of individuals. For example, financial institutions in Kenya use the IPRS for KYC compliance, which requires them to verify the identity of their customers before providing them with services or products. This process essentially performs an ID number check online that is both quick and efficient. For example, financial institutions in Kenya use the IPRS for Know Your Customer (KYC) compliance, which requires them to verify the identity of their customers before providing them with services or products. The IPRS ID verification in Kenya is used to confirm the identity of individuals and to ensure that they are who they claim to be. This helps to prevent identity theft and other forms of financial fraud. The IPRS is also used for voter registration in Kenya. During the voter registration process, the IPRS is used to verify the identity of individuals and ensure that only eligible voters are registered. This helps to prevent electoral fraud and to ensure the integrity of the electoral process. In summary, the use of the Integrated Population Registration System (IPRS) in Kenya for identity verification purposes is aimed at reducing the risk of identity fraud and improving security. The system is widely used by various organizations to verify the identity of individuals, including financial institutions, government agencies, and electoral bodies. Related: Benefits of Business Identity Verification for Kenyan Startups ID Verification Methods for Businesses in Kenya? An accurate and secure ID verification in Kenya is important for businesses, government agencies, banks, fintech, and the payment industry. There are several methods commonly used to verify customers' identities, which include: 1. Document VerificationDocument verification is the process of checking or verifying the authenticity of identity documents such as national IDs, driver's licenses or passports. Today, technologies such as MRZ (machine-readable zone), is used to extract and match data on documents against the government database to confirm validity. In Kenya, it is one of the most commonly used methods of ID verification in basic KYC processes. 2. Government Data SourcesCustomer ID Verification in Kenya can also be carried out by directly verifying the details on the government database, a process known as government KYC check. Examples of documents commonly verified from the government databases in Kenya may include: i. National ID: The national ID card in Kenya is issued by the National Registration Bureau (NRB) to citizens 18 years and above. This ensures that every citizen has a unique identifier. The ID card includes details such as name, date of birth, and ID number, all of which can be verified through the official NRB website.ii. Passport:The Kenyan passport is issued by the Department of immigration services, and it serves as bth an international travel document and a valid means of identification. It contains personal details such as nationality, date of birth that match the government's record.iii. Driver's License: The Kenyan driver's license is issued by the National Transport and Safety Authority (NTSA). It contains the driver's name, license number and DOB. The Kenyan driver's license can be verified against the NTSA's record to confirm its authenticity.Aside from the above-mentioned documents for ID verification in Kenya, other documents can be used such as Kenyan refugee ID, KRA pin certificate, alien card, etc. 3. Commercial and Credit Data Sources i. Credit Data Sources:Credit bureaus provide credit reports that not only help businesses to verify their customers' identities, but also give them insight into an individual's financial behaviour and creditworthiness.ii. Commercial Data Sources:Commercial databases allow businesses to confirm their customers' information, such as employment records and financial history. This offers an additional layer of verification. If your ID verification is complete, you will be able to access various government services and programs. If your ID verification is incomplete, you may need to visit a Huduma Namba registration center to complete the process. In summary, checking your ID verification status in Kenya is easy and can be done online through the Huduma Namba website too, https://www.hudumanamba.go.ke/. You only need to provide your ID number, and you will receive a message indicating whether your ID verification is complete or not. For those wondering how to apply for a Kenyan ID online, the official site also provides guidance on the application process. Moreover, once you submit your details, you can perform a www.identity.go.ke check ID status to confirm that your ID verification is complete. If your verification is incomplete, you may need to visit a Huduma Namba registration center to finalize the process. How to Choose a KYC and ID Verification Provider in Kenya If you are selecting a provider to handle ID verification for your business in Kenya, weigh these factors.Local coverage: Confirm the provider actually verifies Kenyan documents against Kenyan sources such as IPRS and NTSA, and covers every other market on your roadmap so you can scale without switching vendors.Document verification quality. The provider should reliably verify the documents your customers actually use, national IDs and passports first, with high accuracy and low system downtime.Fraud prevention. Look for biometric verification, facial recognition, liveness detection, spoof detection, and automated deduplication, so the provider catches impersonation rather than only reading documents.User experience. Verification should be fast and should hold up on low bandwidth, which matters in areas with unstable connectivity, to avoid onboarding drop-offs.Integration. The solution should fit your existing systems through a clean API, work across devices, and not require months of implementation. Verify IDs in Kenya with Youverify Checking your own ID status is a personal task, but for businesses, verifying customer identities in Kenya is a compliance obligation with real financial and legal consequences if it is done poorly.Youverify provides identity verification built for Kenya and the wider EMEA region, including document verification, government KYC checks against sources such as IPRS, AML screening, enhanced due diligence, and fraud prevention with biometric and liveness checks. This lets compliance teams onboard genuine customers quickly while filtering out fraudulent ones, and it keeps your business aligned with Kenyan KYC and AML requirements.Book a free demo with our compliance experts to see how Youverify verifies Kenyan IDs at scale. Related: ID Verification in Canada How to Choose a KYC Solution Provider for ID Verification in Kenya Choosing the right Know Your Customer (KYC) solution provider is very important as it helps businesses ensure they comply with regulatory requirements, improve security, and enhance customer experience. This KYC solution guide This detailed guide will help you know the factors to consider when choosing the best KYC solution provider for your business: Step 1: Determine Your Business's KYC RequirementsFirst off, understand your business’s KYC requirements as stated by regulatory authorities. With this, you can identify the type of KYC software provider you need. Step 2: Analyze Market Coverage by LocationNow that you understand your business's KYC needs, compile a list of top KYC solution providers and analyse them based on their geographical coverage. If you operate your business across multiple jurisdictions, you will need a KYC partner, such as Youverify, with a presence and solution coverage in all the countries in your business roadmap. This ensures seamless scalability when you expand into new markets.Step 3: Ensure ID Document Verification CapabilitiesAn important quality of the best KYC solution provider is its ability to accurately verify identification documents such as ID cards, passports. Although there are several identity verification methods for businesses today, to verify customer IDs, document verification is often the most reliable and less likely to have system failures, and scalable. Ensure that your ID verification solution provider can verify the most commonly used documents among your customer base.Step 4: Evaluate the Fraud Prevention Capabilities.Fraud prevention is important in preventing financial loss and protecting your business’s reputation. Your ID verification solution provider should have robust fraud prevention solutions such as biometric verification, facial recognition, liveness detection, spoof detection, and automated deduplication checks.Step 5: Consider User Experience ImpactThe ID verification solution provider's suite of solutions should support your customer onboarding process in terms of speed, accessibility, and customizability. For instance, in countries with unstable internet connections, it is important to work with a provider optimised for low-bandwidth environments to reduce the risk of user drop-offs.Step 6: Confirm Technology CompatibilityEnsuring technology compatibility with your KYC provider is very important. Your KYC provider’s solution must integrate seamlessly with your existing systems to prevent prolonged implementation delays. Also, if you operate your business in multiple jurisdictions, it is important that the solution be optimized across different devices and access data points, the solution should be optimised for different devices and access points. The more options a provider offers, the better and stronger it will be for your long-term strategy.Step 7: Test Prospective ProvidersLastly, test the KYC providers that meet your criteria from steps 1 to 6. This testing phase will give you a clear picture of their strength, limitations, and how well they align with your business goals. How Businesses Verify Kenyan IDs at Scale (vs. Manual Checking) Outline: Manual ID checking through eCitizen, SMS, or ad hoc back-and-forth may work for a few records, but it breaks down fast when you’re onboarding hundreds or thousands of customers, employees, or vendors. For banks, fintechs, lenders, employers, and other regulated businesses, manual checks are slow, inconsistent, harder to audit, and more vulnerable to human error and fraud. Automated verification changes the workflow entirely. Instead of staff comparing documents one by one, a business sends ID data through a verification platform that can cross-check records against trusted sources such as IPRS, eCitizen-linked services, and BRS, then return a structured result in seconds. That means a compliance team can verify identity details, detect mismatches, reduce duplicate records, and keep a cleaner audit trail across every onboarding journey. It also makes it easier to scale without adding the same amount of manual review headcount. For businesses handling higher volumes, automation is especially useful because it standardizes decision-making. The same rules can be applied consistently to every applicant, whether they are a customer, employee, agent, or supplier. With the right verification layer, teams can also route only risky or incomplete cases for manual review. That helps reduce friction for legitimate users while keeping controls strong enough for KYC, KYB, and fraud prevention workflows. Want to verify Kenyan IDs faster and with less manual effort? Explore Youverify’s Customer Onboarding product to automate identity checks at scale. Achieve a Seamless ID Verification In Kenya with Youverify ID verification in Kenya is a critical aspect of doing business and protecting oneself from potential fraud. The various methods available for ID verification provide businesses and individuals with options to choose from based on their needs and budget.However, it is important to comply with regulations to avoid potential legal and financial repercussions. Automating the ID verification process can save time, money, and resources while increasing accuracy and security.Youverify provides identity verification services across countries in the EMEA region to help businesses meet their KYC and AML compliance needs. The services include document verification, KYC checks, AML checks, enhanced due diligence checks, government KYC checks, enhanced document verification, fraud prevention solutions and a lot more.Ready to simplify ID verification in Kenya? Discover how Youverify helps you stay compliant and prevent fraud. To get started, book a demo today to see how Youverify can help you achieve KYC compliance in Kenya. FAQsFrequently Asked Questions1. How do I check my ID status online in Kenya?Enter the 9-digit serial number from your waiting card on the National ID status portal at identity.go.ke/search, under the National ID section, then select Status and submit. If the portal is unavailable, send the same serial number by SMS to 20031 and you will receive a reply with your status. SMS is the more reliable method.2. How do I check my ID status by SMS in Kenya?Open a new text message, type only your 9-digit waiting card serial number, and send it to 20031. You will receive a reply confirming whether your ID is still processing or ready for collection. The SMS costs about KES 5, and the reply can occasionally take up to a few days during busy periods.3. How long does it take to get a national ID in Kenya?Most first-time IDs take about two to four weeks after biometric capture, though official guidance sometimes quotes around 10 working days. Start checking your status by SMS to 20031 about two weeks after your biometrics were taken, then check every few days until it shows ready for collection.4. What is a waiting card serial number?It is the unique 9-digit number printed on the waiting card you received after your fingerprints and photo were captured. Both the SMS service and the online portal use this number to look up your ID status. Keep the waiting card safe, because you must surrender it when you collect your finished ID.5. Can I check my ID status without the waiting card serial number?No. Both the SMS and online methods require the 9-digit serial number. If you have lost your waiting card, visit the Huduma Centre or National Registration Bureau office where you applied to retrieve your serial number, as it cannot be recovered by name online.6. What is IPRS in Kenya?The Integrated Population Registration System (IPRS) is Kenya's central register of citizen and resident records, managed by the National Registration Bureau. It is used to verify that a person's details match official records, supporting KYC compliance, voter registration, and access to government services. For official enquiries, the National Registration Bureau can be reached at iprs.interior@gmail.com.7. How do businesses verify a Kenyan ID number?Businesses verify a customer's ID number against the Integrated Population Registration System (IPRS) and other government sources such as NTSA for driving licences, usually through a licensed identity verification provider. This confirms the ID number is valid and belongs to the person presenting it, which manual checks cannot reliably do at scale.Related ArticlesView All PostsKenyaTemitope Lawal•June 13, 2024Benefits of Business Identity Verification for Kenyan StartupsRead MoreKenyaTemitope Lawal•May 12, 2023Easy Way: How to Replace Lost ID Card in KenyaRead MoreKenyaVictoria okere•January 15, 2026The New Kenyan ID Card: Maisha Namba ExplainedRead More","title":"ID Verification in Kenya: How to Check Your ID Status Online in Kenya","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/id-verification-in-kenya","id":368},{"content_hash":"4e99cd1ac59c8d2a2200ccd2e702250e5775cc47a2d7f1e5c18ab1eb87c4aa0b","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:43.299956+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-identity-proofing","language":"","text":"What Is Identity Proofing?ByEmmanuel AgwuMarch 31, 2023•5mins ReadWhat Does Identity Proofing Mean?Why does Identity Proofing Matter?How is Identity Proofing Carried Out As A Crucial Part Of The KYC Process?What is Digital Identity Proofing?The Importance of Digital Identity ProofingIdentity Verification Methods That Are Commonly Adopted In KYC ProcessesBottom LineShare this post The identity of an individual facilitates recognition. Recognition of an individual comes in handy in many ways, that is, in different contexts and provisions. When it comes to financial transactions and business, identification and recognition will always be pertinent. Definitely, it is rational for every business entity, individual, company, and financial institution to confirm an individual's identity through proper recognition processes. This is done in order to avoid the successful perpetuation of malicious activities. What Does Identity Proofing Mean? To the layman or mind, Identity Proofing may mean or involve verbal confirmation of the genuine of one's identity to accelerate recognition. However, Identity Proofing refers to the process of verifying a user’s identity, that is, authenticating that they are who they say they are. If a certain customer claims to be; John Doe, there must be evidence to authenticate that he is John Doe. It may sound like mere authentication based on a username and password. However, identity proofing comes before all of that. It comes into play even before a user or a customer gets to have a username or a password. Identity proofing refers to verifying an individual's identity to ensure that they are who they claim to be. It is a critical step in many industries, such as finance, healthcare, and government, where personal and confidential information is exchanged. Identity proofing involves collecting and verifying various forms of identification and personal information, such as government-issued IDs, biometric data, and personal background information. This process is used to confirm an individual's identity and ensure they are authorized to access certain resources or services. The aim of the process of identity proofing is to establish trust in the identity of an individual to prevent fraud and identity theft. It is an important part of identity and access management (IAM). It is often used with other security measures, such as multi-factor authentication, to protect sensitive information and systems. Why does Identity Proofing Matter? According to credible sources, identity fraud or theft costs the United Kingdom about four billion a year. There are an estimated 28 million data breaches across the UK every year. Using an email address and a phone number alone is not enough for a secure identity and access management system. It tells very little about an individual. This is why it is important to go beyond those two pieces of information and go through a full KYC process as a business. Proper Identity proofing reduces data breaches by eighty percent since eighty percent of data breaches and identity fraud are associated with weak, reused, and stolen credentials. For one, identity proofing is an essential component of KYC regulations. KYC refers to the process adopted by financial institutions and businesses or corporate entities to accurately verify the identity of a potential customer or incoming customer, and their risk levels are also assessed. KYC regulations aim to prevent money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illicit activities that can be perpetrated or facilitated through financial processes. Identity proofing is important to KYC because it enables financial institutions to verify their customers' identities accurately. This process involves collecting and verifying various forms of identification and personal information, such as government-issued IDs, biometric data, and personal background information. It ensures that the person applying for financial services is who they claim to be and that the personal information they provide is accurate. Fraud and money laundering risks can be mitigated through the KYC process by conducting appropriate identity proofing. It also helps financial institutions comply with regulatory requirements and avoid potential fines and reputational damage associated with non-compliance. It is important to know that Identity proofing is crucial to the KYC process. It enables financial institutions to accurately verify identify their customers and comply with the regulations provided and, to help prevent financial crimes and protect the financial system's integrity. Read Also: Electronic ID Verification How is Identity Proofing Carried Out As A Crucial Part Of The KYC Process? Identity proofing is done on KYC by collecting and verifying different authentic forms of identification and personal information from the customer. Here are the usual ways or steps which are involved with KYC. a. Customer’s Information Is Collected Customers are asked to provide personal information like; name, date of birth, address, a form of identification issued by the government, such as a passport, photo card, driving license, or national id card. These will serve as a form of proof for a person's identity and existence. b. Identity Documents Are Verified Identification documents provided by the customer will be verified for authenticity and validity. Verification may involve checking the documents' holograms, watermarks, and other security features. c. Personal Information Provided Are Verified Personal information provided by customers is verified through multiple sources, like credit bureaus, public records, and other official databases. d. Biometric Verification KYC processes can also include biometric verification, such as facial recognition and fingerprint scanning, to confirm the identity of the customer. It will also help determine if the customer has been on any watchlists or has a history of suspicious or criminal activity. e. Risk Assessment The total information collected is analyzed to assess the customer's risk level. This also determines if the customer is on any watchlists or has a history of suspicious activity. f. Ongoing Or Continuous Monitoring KYC requirements will involve monitoring the customer’s activity in order to detect any suspicious behaviour or changes in risk level. Certainly, identity proofing for KYC involves a comprehensive verification process that ensures the customer's identity is accurately verified and their risk level is assessed. The procedures may vary depending on the financial institution's policies and regulatory requirements. What is Digital Identity Proofing? Digital identity proofing process is an important process that verifies a person's identity in the digital realm. It involves collecting and validating various pieces of information, including name, date of birth, address, and government-issued identification documents. This technology plays a vital role in enhancing online security by ensuring that individuals are who they claim to be. Implementing a robust digital identity proofing measures, businesses and organizations can mitigate risks, protect sensitive data, and foster trust in the digital ecosystem. The Importance of Digital Identity Proofing As the digital landscape continues to evolve, the need for secure and reliable digital identity proofing solutions becomes increasingly essential. By implementing robust digital identity proofing measures, businesses and organizations can:Mitigate Fraud: Protect against identity theft and fraudulent activities.Enhance Customer Experience: Streamline onboarding processes and improve customer satisfaction.Comply with Regulations: Adhere to industry standards and government regulations.Build Trust: Establish credibility and build strong customer relationships. Key Technologies in Digital Identity Proofing Process Several technologies are employed in digital identity proofing to ensure accuracy and security. They include biometric authentication, knowledge-based authentication, and document verification. Identity Verification Methods That Are Commonly Adopted In KYC Processes It is not confusing to ascertain materials and ways to facilitate identity proofing. However, as time advances, methods advance as well. This section will explain and discuss identity verification methods that are commonly adopted. Identity verification aims to ascertain or, more technically, authenticate a potential customer's acknowledged identity in the KYC process. This is a crucial step to preventing the preparation of financial fraud and other malicious or illegal activities that may be facilitated with financial irregularities, like money laundering to drug trafficking. 1. Social media verification It may seem unconventional, however, with the widespread use of social media and the equivalent growth of users on various social platforms. Social media verification may be a method to consider. However, it may be easily manipulated in some ways. Social media platforms can be used to verify an individual's identity. This involves confirming their account details and comparing them to their government-issued ID. 2. Biometric Verification Biometric verification: Biometric verification involves using unique physical characteristics, such as fingerprints or facial recognition, to establish a customer's identity. This method is becoming increasingly popular, particularly in the financial industry. 3. Knowledge-based authentication Knowledge-based authentication involves answering a series of questions about the personal information only the true individual would know, such as the name of a first pet or the street where they grew up. However, this is not used in an initial KYC process. And may be considered an outdated method, but it was commonly used in the 2000s. 4. Government-issued Identification Using government-issued identification is one of the most common ways to prove a customer’s identity is by presenting a government-issued ID, such as a passport, driving photo license, or national ID card. It still remains one of the standard methods to adopt. 5. Utility Bill Verification Some organizations may require individuals to provide a utility bill as proof of their address. This can be used together with other forms of identification to verify an individual's identity. 6. In-person Verification Organizations may sometimes require individuals to present themselves in person to confirm their identity. This can involve meeting with a representative of the organization or going to a designated location for identification purposes. 7. Notarized documents Notarized documents can be used to prove an individual's identity in certain circumstances, such as when applying for a loan or opening a bank account. 8. In-person verification Organizations may sometimes require individuals to present themselves in person to confirm their identity. This can involve meeting with a representative of the organization or going to a designated location for identification purposes. 9. Background Check Background checks can also be used to verify an individual's identity. This involves conducting a search of public records and databases to confirm personal information. Recommended: Understanding the difference between authentication, identification and verification Bottom Line Various organizations adopt different methods to verify the identities that potential customers are presenting. It is a crucial part of the KYC process and will serve as a major shield against illicit financial activities. Businesses can quickly perform KYC, biometrics verification, fraud detection, and anti-money laundering checks with Youverify. Join 100+ leading companies that use Youverify for identity spoofing for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today. Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"What Is Identity Proofing?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-identity-proofing","id":383},{"content_hash":"bb7eb853c2045445688255107f9fd7b5a74ceb244ced5fd617d597e57dbedb94","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:40.238379+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-multi-factor-authentication-mfa","language":"","text":"Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) – Everything You Need to KnowByPriscilla Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 31, 2023•5mins ReadTypes of Multi-Factor AuthenticationWhat are the Benefits of Multi-Factor Authentication?Best Practices for Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)Bottom LineShare this postIn today’s digital world, passwords are no longer enough to secure our online accounts. Cyber threats such as phishing, hacking, and identity theft are becoming increasingly sophisticated, and it is essential to use Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) to protect our online identity and data. Multi-Factor Authentication adds an extra layer of security to the login process, making it more difficult for attackers to gain access to your account even if they have your password. In this article, we will discuss everything you need to know about Multi-Factor Authentication, including its types, benefits, and best practices. What is Multi-Factor Authentication? Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) is a security mechanism that requires users to provide two or more forms of authentication to access a system or application. Multi-Factor Authentication is based on the idea that using multiple factors to authenticate a user’s identity makes it more difficult for unauthorized users to gain access. The goal of Multi-Factor Authentication is to enhance security by requiring additional information beyond just a password or PIN, which can be easily guessed, stolen, or hacked. How does Multi-Factor Authentication Work? Multi-Factor Authentication requires users to provide two or more factors of authentication. So when a user enters their username and password to access a system or application, the system will prompt the user to provide an additional form of authentication. This is where Multiple-Factor Authentication comes in. Users may provide: Something they know: The user will be required to provide a password or PINSomething they have: A verification code may be sent to their mobile devices (something they have)Something they are: This requires users to provide a unique physical characteristic (something they are) such as a fingerprint, iris scan, or facial recognition. To authenticate with MFA, a user must provide at least two of these factors. For example, a user may enter a password and then provide a one-time code generated by a security token. Or, a user may scan their fingerprint and then enter a PIN. Once the additional authentication factor is verified, the user is granted access to the system or application. This way, even if a hacker manages to obtain a user’s password, they would still need access to the user’s physical device or biometric data to complete the authentication process. See also: What is Token Based Authentication? Types of Multi-Factor Authentication There are several types of Multi-Factor Authentication, and the most common ones are: 1. SMS-based MFA In this type of MFA, the user receives a one-time passcode (OTP) via SMS on their registered mobile number. The user has to enter this OTP along with their password to access their account. SMS-based MFA is the easiest and most widely used MFA, but it has some security issues as the OTP can be intercepted by attackers or can be accessed by someone who has physical access to the user’s phone. 2. Time-based One-Time Password (TOTP) MFA TOTP is a time-based authentication mechanism that generates a new OTP every 30 seconds. The OTP is generated by an app on the user’s phone or a dedicated hardware token. The user has to enter this OTP along with their password to access their account. TOTP is more secure than SMS-based MFA as the OTP is generated locally and is not sent over the internet. 3. Biometric MFA Biometric MFA uses the user’s unique physical characteristics, such as fingerprints or facial recognition, to authenticate them. Biometric MFA is more secure than traditional password-based authentication as it is difficult to replicate someone’s physical characteristics. 4. Push Notification MFA Push notification MFA sends a notification to the user’s phone asking them to confirm the login request. The user has to approve or deny the request. This type of MFA is very secure as it requires physical access to the user’s phone and also requires the user’s confirmation to grant access. See also: What are The Types of Authentication? What are the Benefits of Multi-Factor Authentication? MFA provides several benefits that help to improve the security of online accounts. Some of the key benefits of MFA are: a. Stronger security: MFA adds an extra layer of security to the login process, making it difficult for attackers to gain access to your account even if they have your password. Improved user experience: MFA is quick and easy to use. It eliminates the need for users to remember multiple passwords or to enter one-time passcodes every time they log in. b. Reduced risk of data breaches: MFA reduces the risk of data breaches as it is difficult for attackers to gain access to sensitive data even if they manage to compromise a user’s password. c. Compliance: Many regulations such as GDPR, HIPAA, and PCI DSS require companies to use MFA to protect sensitive data. Using MFA can help companies to comply with these regulations. Best Practices for Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) To ensure the best security for your online accounts, it is essential to follow some best practices when using MFA. Some of the best practices for MFA are: a. Use MFA wherever possible Use MFA for all your online accounts that support it, including email, social media, banking, and other financial accounts. b. Use different factors for authentication Use multiple factors for authentication, such as something you know (password), something you have (smartphone or security key), and something you are (biometric data like fingerprint or facial recognition). c. Use strong and unique passwords Use strong and unique passwords for all your accounts, and avoid using the same password for multiple accounts. d. Secure your devices Secure your devices, including your smartphone and computer, with a strong password, PIN, or biometric authentication. Keep your software and apps up-to-date to avoid any vulnerabilities. e. Be aware of phishing attacks Be aware of phishing attacks and never share your MFA codes or passwords with anyone, even if they claim to be from a legitimate source. f. Use trusted MFA methods Choose trusted MFA methods, such as Time-based One-Time Password (TOTP) or Universal 2nd Factor (U2F), and avoid using SMS or email for MFA authentication. g. Test your MFA setup: Test your MFA setup regularly to ensure it is working correctly and detect any issues before they become a problem. Bottom Line As technology continues to evolve, so do the threats to our online security. By staying informed and adopting robust security measures like MFA, we can better protect ourselves from cyber-attacks and enjoy the benefits of the digital world with peace of mind. While MFA is not foolproof, it is still one of the best ways to keep your accounts secure. In this article, we have covered everything you need to know about MFA, including its benefits, different authentication factors, and best practices for implementation. See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for KYC and AML screening of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today. Related ArticlesAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Priscilla, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 28, 2023What is a Sanctions List?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Emmanuel Agwu•February 14, 2024RegTech's Influence On Regulatory Policy and ReformRead More","title":"Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) – Everything You Need to Know","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-multi-factor-authentication-mfa","id":393},{"content_hash":"58e3abd5420ac6340e8d167aa18b2dd62d46fed5b7dcaf7860b7e9ad7b4729fd","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:38.181849+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/31/launches-hire-nigeria/","language":"","text":"FG Launches âHire from Nigeriaâ Campaign, Targets One Million Jobs By Prnigeria - August 31, 2026 (LâR) Director-General, Nigerian Television Authority (NTA), Comrade Salihu Abdulhamid Dembos; Founder and President, Tech4Dev, Joel Ogunsola; Honourable Minister of Education, Dr. Maruf Olatunji Alausa; Honourable Minister of Information and National Orientation, Mohammed Idris, fnipr; Honourable Minister of Industry, Trade and Investment, Dr. Jumoke Oduwole; Special Adviser to President Bola Ahmed Tinubu on Media and Public Communications, Sunday Dare; Director-General, Federal Radio Corporation of Nigeria (FRCN), Dr. Mohammed Bulama; and Senior Special Assistant, Strategic Communication & NATEP, Office of the Honourable Minister of Industry, Trade and Investment, Olajumoke Dan-Okayi, during the Press Conference for the launch of the âHire for Nigeriaâ Campaign on August 31, 2026, in Abuja. Photo: Khalid Ahmed/FMINO. FG Launches âHire from Nigeriaâ Campaign, Targets One Million Jobs The Federal Government has launched the âHire from Nigeriaâ Campaign, a major initiative aimed at positioning Nigerian professionals and digitally delivered services in the global market and creating one million export-linked jobs over the next five years. The Minister of Information and National Orientation, Mohammed Idris, disclosed this on Monday in Abuja at the official public presentation of the campaign at Radio House. Idris said the initiative marked a shift in Nigeriaâs traditional approach to exports, with greater emphasis now being placed on the countryâs human capital, skills, creativity and professional services. He said the global economy had evolved beyond the movement of physical goods, allowing knowledge, skills and services to cross borders digitally. According to him, Nigeria is well positioned to compete in the emerging global talent economy because of its large youthful population, growing pool of skilled professionals, entrepreneurial culture, English-language proficiency and expanding capabilities in technology, creative services and business support. âHire from Nigeriaâ is a public-private initiative involving the Federal Ministry of Industry, Trade and Investment, Federal Ministry of Education, National Talent Export Programme (NATEP), Bank of Industryâs iDICE programme and other domestic and international private-sector partners. The Information Minister said communication would be central to the success of the initiative, stressing that Nigeria must not only develop talent but also ensure that the global market is aware of the quality and capacity of its professionals. He pledged the support of the Federal Ministry of Information and National Orientation in promoting the campaign through its communication platforms, agencies and partnerships. âWe have a responsibility to ensure that the story of Nigerian talent is told clearly, credibly and consistently,â Idris said. Read Also: FRSC, BATMedics Screen 500 Drivers to Mark World Sight Day Troops Foil Lakurawa Assault on Kebbi Military Base, Recover 17 Motorcycles, Weapons Troops Nab 9 Suspected Terrorist Collaborators in Yobe, Rescue 6 Hostages in Plateau He added that the government would use the campaign to communicate directly with employers, investors and businesses worldwide, emphasising that Nigeria has skilled people ready to compete and collaborate in the international marketplace. The Minister also encouraged young Nigerians to take advantage of opportunities offered by the digital economy, noting that they no longer need to relocate abroad to participate in the global workforce. âWith the right skills, infrastructure, professional standards, and access to markets, Nigerians can live and work and earn here at home, while providing valuable services to clients and employers worldwide,â he said. Idris said such an approach would generate foreign exchange inflows, increase tax revenues and expand Nigeriaâs skills and knowledge base. He linked the initiative to President Bola Ahmed Tinubuâs Renewed Hope Agenda, particularly its focus on job creation, economic diversification, skills development, global integration and youth empowerment. According to him, the Federal Government has set an ambition of creating one million export-linked jobs within five years as part of its strategy to make Nigeria a major hub for talent and services exports. He stressed, however, that achieving the target would require sustained collaboration between government and the private sector, training institutions and employers, technology companies and young professionals, as well as stronger connections between Nigerian talent and international markets. The Minister said activities would build towards the global launch of the campaign during the 81st Session of the United Nations General Assembly, providing an opportunity for Nigeria to showcase its human capital to a wider international audience. He urged stakeholders to ensure that âHire from Nigeriaâ becomes more than a promotional campaign, but evolves into a sustainable platform connecting Nigerian professionals with global opportunities. âThe message of today is therefore simple: Nigeria is ready to compete on the global stage. Nigerian talent is ready to work. And the world should look to Nigeria,â Idris said. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGS(NATEP)Bola Ahmed TinubuHire from Nigeria Previous articleREPORTER’S DIARY: Adeniyi and Nigeria Customs in Global EyesNext articleMilitary Rescues 10 Abducted NYSC Members in Kogi Forest Prnigeria Xing","title":"FG Launches âHire from Nigeriaâ Campaign, Targets One Million Jobs","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/31/launches-hire-nigeria/","id":403},{"content_hash":"0f37d4cf4a8b5005e34adcd4bda25221bad28499fc8bfee146c218b9f3dbbe51","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:36.311249+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/anti-money-laundering-act-kenya","language":"","text":"Anti-Money Laundering Act Kenya: Penalty for Money Laundering in Kenya ByTemitope LawalMarch 30, 2023•5mins ReadWhat is Considered to be Money Laundering in Kenya?What is Anti-Money Laundering Act?What is Anti-Money Laundering Act in Kenya?What is the Proceeds of Crime and Anti-Money Laundering Act Kenya 2017 all about?What is the Penalty for Money Laundering in Kenya?Who Regulates Anti-Money Laundering Act in KenyaBottom LineShare this postThe Anti-Money Laundering Act in Kenya is a crucial piece of legislation aimed at combatting financial crimes such as money laundering and terrorism financing. According to the 2020 Money Laundering and Terrorist Financing Risk Assessment report, Kenya has been identified as a high-risk country for money laundering due to its strategic location as a gateway to East and Central Africa. The report further reveals that between 2017 and 2019, a total of KES 24.6 billion (approximately USD 229 million) was frozen from suspected money laundering activities. This article discusses the Anti-Money Laundering Act in Kenya and the role it plays in preventing criminal activities and safeguarding the integrity of Kenya's financial system. What is Considered to be Money Laundering in Kenya? According to the Kenyan government, a person who is aware or should have been aware that property is the result of criminal activity and: 1. Enters into any kind of agreement, arrangement, or transaction with someone related to that property, whether or not that agreement, arrangement, or transaction is legally binding; or 2. Performs any other action related to that property, either alone or with others, which has the effect of: hiding or altering the nature, origin, location, disposition, or movement of that property, or the ownership of it, or any interest anyone may have in it;helping anyone who has committed or is committing a crime, whether in Kenya or elsewhere, to evade prosecution; or removing or reducing any property obtained directly or indirectly as a result of the commission of a crime. What is Anti-Money Laundering Act? The Anti-Money Laundering Act (AMLA) is a set of laws and anti-money regulations aimed at preventing the use of illegally obtained funds for financial transactions. The act is designed to combat money laundering, which is the process of hiding the true source of funds obtained through illegal activities such as drug trafficking, corruption, and fraud. What is Anti-Money Laundering Act in Kenya? The Anti-Money Laundering Act Kenya requires financial institutions, including banks, insurance companies, and money remittance companies, to implement anti-money laundering policies and procedures. This is to ensure KYT compliance with the anti-money laundering act in Kenya. Also, these institutions must also conduct customer due diligence and report suspicious transactions to the Financial Reporting Centre (FRC), which is Kenya's financial intelligence unit responsible for receiving, analyzing, and disseminating financial information related to suspected money laundering and terrorism financing activities. Under the Anti-Money Laundering Act Kenya, it is a criminal offence to engage in money laundering activities, and those found guilty may face imprisonment or fines. The act also provides for the freezing of assets that are suspected to be linked to money laundering or terrorism financing activities. Hence all financial institutions are expected to know and monitor their customers' transaction history. Related: Anti-Money Laundering (AML) Act Kenya What is the Proceeds of Crime and Anti-Money Laundering Act Kenya 2017 all about? The Proceeds of Crime and Anti-Money Laundering Act Kenya (POCAMLA) is a law in Kenya that was passed in 2017 to strengthen the country's anti-money laundering and counter-terrorism financing framework. The act is a revision of the previous Anti-Money Laundering Act Kenya of 2009, hence it is called the Proceeds of Crime and Anti-Money Laundering (amendment) act 2017 Kenya. The POCAMLA is designed to prevent the use of illegally obtained funds for financial transactions within Kenya and to disrupt the financing of criminal activities such as drug trafficking, corruption, and terrorism. It requires businesses and institutions, including financial institutions, lawyers, real estate agents, and casinos, to implement anti-money laundering and counter-terrorism financing policies and procedures. The proceeds of crime and anti-money laundering act Kenya also establishes the Asset Recovery Agency (ARA), which is responsible for identifying, tracing, freezing, seizing, and recovering proceeds of crime. The ARA is also tasked with enforcing the provisions of the act and working closely with other government agencies to prosecute money laundering and terrorism financing cases. Under the POCAMLA, it is a criminal offence to engage in money laundering activities or to provide financial services to individuals or entities involved in such activities. The act provides severe penalties for those found guilty, including fines and imprisonment. The Proceeds of Crime and anti-money laundering act 2009 Kenya, classifies the following as criminal offences punishable under the anti-money laundering act Kenya: Money LaunderingFailure to report suspicion regarding proceeds of crime.Acquisition, possession or use of proceeds of crime.Financial promotion of an offence. Conveyance of monetary instruments to or from KenyaMisuse of informationTipping off.Failure to comply with the provisions of this ActFailure to comply with an order of the court. Hindering a person in the performance of functions under this ActMisrepresentation. Protection of information and informersSecrecy obligations overrode.Client advocate relationship. Overall, the Proceeds of Crime and Anti-Money Laundering Act in Kenya is a critical tool in the country's efforts to combat financial crimes and protect its financial system. What is the Penalty for Money Laundering in Kenya? The penalty for money laundering in Kenya is either a maximum of 14 years imprisonment or a fine that is not greater than KShs. 5,000,000/- or the value of the property involved in the offence, whichever amount is higher. It is also possible to receive both a fine and imprisonment. Who Regulates Anti-Money Laundering Act in Kenya 1. Financial Reporting Centre:In industries that are regulated, such as banking and insurance, the Financial Reporting Centre collaborates closely with the regulator to ensure that the regulated institutions fulfil their anti-money laundering obligations correctly. Conversely, in sectors without a regulator, such as the real estate industry or high-value dealers, the Financial Reporting Centre interacts directly with the reporting institutions as the anti-money laundering act regulator in Kenya. 2. The Anti-Money Laundering Advisory Board.As per Section 49 of the Act, the board was formed with the responsibility of providing policy guidance to the government, identifying proceeds of crime or unlawful activities through best practices, offering advice to the FRC on its functions and powers, and serving as a consultant to various entities. 3. The Assets Recovery Agency.Established under Section 53 of the Act, the ARA investigate and enforce sanctions against individuals who violate the Act. The Agency is well-known for its authority in obtaining court orders for confiscation, forfeiture, restraint, and preservation of assets that are believed to have been obtained through illegal means. Bottom Line Complying with the Anti-Money Laundering Act in Kenya is crucial to ensure that financial transactions are conducted legally and ethically. However, staying compliant can be challenging and time-consuming. Therefore, businesses and individuals can benefit greatly from a real-time automated compliance tool- Workflow Builder that can automate all compliance needs. So, if you want to stay ahead of the game and comply with the Anti-Money Laundering Act in Kenya seamlessly, invest in workflow builder today. See how 100+ leading companies use Workflow Builder for KYC and AML screening of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today. Related ArticlesView All PostsKenyaTemitope Lawal•June 13, 2024Benefits of Business Identity Verification for Kenyan StartupsRead MoreKenyaTemitope Lawal•May 12, 2023Easy Way: How to Replace Lost ID Card in KenyaRead MoreKenyaVictoria okere•January 15, 2026The New Kenyan ID Card: Maisha Namba ExplainedRead More","title":"Anti-Money Laundering Act Kenya: Penalty for Money Laundering in Kenya","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/anti-money-laundering-act-kenya","id":408},{"content_hash":"9540533022178c338998b08515d3ee4008039d859b2f4079a0b8d1ecc49c3a33","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:35.283406+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/31/military-rescues-abducted/","language":"","text":"Military Rescues 10 Abducted NYSC Members in Kogi Forest By Prnigeria - August 31, 2026 Military troops Military Rescues 10 Abducted NYSC Members in Kogi Forest The military high command has rescued 10 of the 15 National Youth Service Corps (NYSC) members abducted by suspected kidnappers in Kogi State. Director of Defence Information, Major General Michael Onoja, disclosed this on Monday during the monthly press briefing of the Defence Headquarters in Abuja. Read Also: The Humble Giant: A Tribute to Late Idris Ibrahim IBRO By Abdullahi Sani Maiunguwa Tinubu declares three-day mourning for 32 NAF personnel killed in air crash FRSC Divests Safeline Microfinance Bank, Hands Over to ROBOPAY Onoja said the 10 corps members were rescued from Egume Forest in Ofu Local Government Area of the state, adding that the operation was still ongoing to rescue the remaining victims and apprehend the perpetrators. The victims were among 15 NYSC members kidnapped last week while travelling from Abuja to Bayelsa State. The kidnappers had reportedly demanded a ransom of N20 million from the families of some of the victims. The Defence Headquarters urged members of the public to remain calm and vigilant, while encouraging them to promptly report suspicious movements or information that could assist security forces in rescuing the remaining victims and arresting the suspects. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSDefence HeadquartersMajor General Michael OnojaNYSC Previous articleFG Launches ‘Hire from Nigeria’ Campaign, Targets One Million JobsNext articleTroops Rescue 21 Kidnap Victims in Kogi Forests Prnigeria Xing","title":"Military Rescues 10 Abducted NYSC Members in Kogi Forest","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/31/military-rescues-abducted/","id":415},{"content_hash":"61ae004b2605b21ba1c111a522f675ea2a793dd0d4fdbd6bcb85604576f472ef","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:32.624304+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-documents-are-required-for-kyc","language":"","text":"What Documents Are Required For KYC?ByPriscilla, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 30, 2023•5mins ReadDocuments Required for KYC3. Proof of Income4. Business Proof5. Other DocumentsFAQBottom LineShare this postKYC, which stands for Know Your Customer, is a term used to describe the process of identifying and verifying the identity of customers or clients. This process is important for businesses in various industries, such as banking, finance, and real estate, as it helps to prevent fraud, money laundering, and other illegal activities. KYC compliance is mandated by various government agencies and regulators around the world. The documents required for KYC vary depending on the industry and the country in which the business is operating. However, some general documents are commonly required for KYC purposes. In this article, we will discuss the documents required for KYC and their importance. Documents Required for KYC The most important documents required for KYC include: 1. Proof of Identity The most important document for KYC is proof of identity. This is a document that proves the identity of the customer. The most common documents used for proof of identity are government-issued identification documents like a passport, driver’s license, or national ID card. a. Passport A passport is a travel document issued by the government that proves the identity and nationality of the holder. It is an internationally recognized document and is often used as proof of identity for KYC purposes. To verify the authenticity of the passport, the business must check the security features of the passport, such as holograms, watermarks, and biometric information. b. Driver’s License A driver’s license is a government-issued document that allows the holder to operate a motor vehicle. It contains the name, address, and date of birth of the holder, and is often used as proof of identity for KYC purposes. To verify the authenticity of the driver’s license, the business must check the security features of the license, such as holograms, watermarks, and microprinting. c. National ID Card A national ID card is a government-issued document that contains the name, address, and date of birth of the holder. It is often used as proof of identity for KYC purposes in countries where it is mandatory to have a national ID card. To verify the authenticity of the national ID card, the business must check the security features of the card, such as holograms, watermarks, and microprinting. d. Voter ID Card A voter ID card is a government-issued document that contains the customer's name, photograph, address, and date of birth. It is commonly used as an identity proof document in India. e. PAN Card A PAN card is a government-issued document that is used for tax purposes in India. It contains the customer's name, photograph, and date of birth. 2. Proof of Address In addition to proof of identity, businesses also need to collect and verify proof of address. This is a document that proves the address of the customer. The most common documents used for proof of address are utility bills, bank statements, and government-issued documents like a driver’s license or national ID card. a. Utility bills Utility bills like electricity bills, water bills, or gas bills are often used as proof of address for KYC purposes. The bill must be recent (usually within the last three months) and must contain the name and address of the customer. To verify the authenticity of the bill, the business must check that it is an original bill and not a photocopy or printout. b. Bank statements Bank statements are also commonly used as proof of address for KYC purposes. The statement must be recent (usually within the last three months) and must contain the name and address of the customer. To verify the authenticity of the statement, the business must check that it is an original statement and not a photocopy or printout. c. Driver’s license or national ID card In some countries, a driver’s license or national ID card can be used as both proofs of identity and proof of address. The address on the document must be the same as the address of the customer. To verify the authenticity of the document, the business must check the security features of the document, such as holograms, watermarks, and microprinting. d. Bank Statement A bank statement is another commonly accepted address proof document. It contains the customer's name and address, as well as details of their bank account. e. Rental Agreement A rental agreement is a document that is used to verify the customer's residential address if they are living in a rented property. It contains the customer's name and address, as well as details of the rental agreement. f. Passport A passport can also be used as an address proof document if the customer's address is mentioned in the passport. g. Voter ID Card A voter ID card can also be used as an address proof document if the customer's address is mentioned in the card. Recommended - How To Get A Proof of Address 3. Proof of Income Some businesses may also require proof of income as part of the KYC process. This is a document that proves the income of the customer. The most common documents used for proof of income are: a. Salary Slip A salary slip is a document that is provided by the employer to the employee. It contains details of the employee's salary, including the amount, deductions, and taxes. b. Bank Statement A bank statement can also be used as an income proof document. It contains details of the customer's income, including deposits, withdrawals, and other transactions. c. Income Tax Return An income tax return is a document that is filed by the customer with the government to report their income and taxes. It contains details of the customer's income, 4. Business Proof Business proof is required for KYC when the customer is a business entity or a self-employed individual. The purpose of business proof is to establish the existence of the business and to confirm the identity of the owner. Examples of business proof documents include: a. Business registration certificate b. Partnership deed c. Memorandum of Association d. Articles of Association Business registration certificate is the most commonly accepted business proof document as it establishes the existence of the business and contains the name and address of the owner. Partnership deeds, Memorandum of Association, and Articles of Association are also acceptable as they contain details about the business and its ownership. 5. Other Documents Apart from the above-mentioned documents, some institutions may require additional documents for KYC purposes. These documents may include: a. References from other financial institutions b. Photographs c. Signature specimens References from other financial institutions are required to establish the customer's financial history and to confirm their identity. Photographs and signature specimens are required to ensure that the customer's photograph and signature match the ones on the identity proof document.INTREASTING READ: What is KYC? Understanding KYC & How it Works (Updated) FAQ 1. What documents are required for KYC in bankWhen opening an account, banks must comply with regulatory standards, making it important to know what documents are required for KYC in bank processes. Typically, banks request:1. Proof of Identity: Passport, national ID card, driver’s license, or voter’s card.2. Proof of Address: Utility bill, tenancy agreement, or recent bank statement.3. Passport-sized Photographs: For proper identification records.Understanding what documents are required for KYC in bank ensures smooth onboarding while helping institutions prevent fraud and money laundering. Whether for savings or business accounts, the answer to what documents are required for KYC in bank is centered on valid identity and address verification. 2. What documents are required for KYC verificationMany customers ask what documents are required for KYC verification, especially when completing digital onboarding or in-branch checks. Generally, the following are needed:1. A government-issued ID such as a passport, national ID, or driver’s license.2. Proof of residential address, such as a utility bill or bank statement (not older than 3 months).3. Additional documentation, like employment letters or tax records, may be required depending on the institution or reason for KYC verification.By clarifying what documents are required for KYC verification, financial institutions ensure transparency, compliance, and fraud prevention. Always check with your bank to confirm exactly what documents are required for KYC verification, since requirements may differ. 3. What documents are required for KYC current accountOpening a current account usually demands more details than a savings account, making it necessary to know what documents are required for KYC current account checks. These often include:1. For Individuals: National ID, proof of address, and passport photographs.2. For Businesses: Certificate of incorporation, tax identification number (TIN), board resolution, and valid IDs of signatories.Banks enforce these rules to strengthen compliance and protect against illicit activities. By understanding what documents are required for KYC current account, applicants avoid delays during account opening. Whether you’re an entrepreneur or individual, ensure you provide all documents required for KYC current account requirements upfront. 4. What are KYC requirementsMany people ask, what are KYC requirements, since they form the foundation of customer due diligence in banking. KYC requirements generally include:1. Identity Verification – A government-issued ID such as passport, driver’s license, or national ID.2. Proof of Address – Utility bills, tenancy agreements, or recent bank statements.3. Risk Assessment – Source of funds and occupation details.4. Ongoing Monitoring – Regular checks for suspicious activity.Understanding what are KYC requirements ensures customers meet compliance obligations while institutions mitigate risks. In practice, KYC requirements is about proving identity, confirming addresses, and monitoring transactions for security.Bottom Line When it comes to identifying and verifying your clients, certain documents will make for a smooth process. Documents used in formal transactions such as Banks statements or Government issued/approved documents are best used in this process. Youverify is your go-to RegTech Company for all your financial transactions. Request a Demo. Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"What Documents Are Required For KYC?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-documents-are-required-for-kyc","id":423},{"content_hash":"07305eedfa3dc84ed3752774299afe52978ecdd12a85badd91b29c6f052abdb0","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:31.382328+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/31/troops-kidnap-victims-kogi/","language":"","text":"Troops Rescue 21 Kidnap Victims in Kogi Forests By Prnigeria - August 31, 2026 The Rescued Kidnap Victims Troops Rescue 21 Kidnap Victims in Kogi Forests Troops of the Nigerian Army’s 12 Brigade, in collaboration with the Nigeria Police Force, Department of State Services (DSS), local hunters and vigilante groups, have rescued 21 kidnapped victims in Dekina Local Government Area of Kogi State. The victims were rescued on Monday, August 31, 2026, during a coordinated search operation conducted in the Egume and Ochaja forest areas following reports of the mass abduction of road users along the Alloma–Ejule–Itobe road axis. According to a statement by the Acting Assistant Director, Army Public Relations, 12 Brigade, Lieutenant Hassan Abdullahi, the operation was launched after troops received credible intelligence on the location of the victims. Read Also: NITDA DG Kashifu Inuwa Empowers Hundreds in Jigawa as TMS Movement Aligns with Tinubu’s Renewed Hope Agenda Pantami’s Fresh Mandate: A Legal Win with Far‑Reaching Implications By Fatimah Usman Zagga, Esq 2027: INEC Seeks Release of Outstanding Funds to Ensure Seamless Elections He said personnel from the Brigade, alongside other security agencies and local security groups, mobilised from the headquarters of 21 Battalion and conducted a coordinated search of adjoining forests. The operation resulted in the rescue of 21 victims comprising eight males and 13 females. However, one of the male victims sustained a gunshot wound and was immediately evacuated to a medical facility for treatment. The rescued victims are currently in the custody of the troops pending their handover to the appropriate authorities. The Chief of Army Staff, Lieutenant General Waidi Shaibu, commended the troops and other security personnel, hunters and vigilantes for their courage, cooperation and commitment to the successful operation. He urged members of the public to remain vigilant and provide timely and credible information to security agencies to support ongoing efforts to combat kidnapping and other criminal activities. The rescue comes amid heightened security concerns over the activities of kidnapping gangs along major highways and forest corridors in Kogi and neighbouring states. VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSdssKidnap VictimsKogi ForestsNigeria Police ForceNigerian Army's 6 Brigade Previous articleMilitary Rescues 10 Abducted NYSC Members in Kogi ForestNext articleEFCC Dismisses Over 40 Staff, Prosecutes Others Over Corruption, Financial Malpractice — Olukoyede Prnigeria","title":"Troops Rescue 21 Kidnap Victims in Kogi Forests","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/31/troops-kidnap-victims-kogi/","id":429},{"content_hash":"b042c4f62963cf5e2a506eb3319af9c2455dce8915017a421c927faf32394f81","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:28.876267+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/suspicious-activity-reporting","language":"","text":"Suspicious Activity Reporting: All You Need to Know ByPriscilla Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 29, 2023•5mins ReadWhat is Suspicious Activity Reporting (SAR)?What Businesses are Required to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs)?What are the Record Keeping Requirements for Suspicious Activity Reporting (SAR)?What are the Benefits of Filing Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs)?What are the Consequences of Failure to File Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs)?ConclusionShare this postSuspicious Activity Reporting (SAR) is an important tool in the fight against financial crime and is used to help detect and prevent illegal activity, identify and apprehend criminals, and protect national security. Financial institutions are required by law to report suspicious activity to the appropriate authorities, and failure to do so can result in significant fines and penalties. Here, we explored SAR, its nature and why it is important that businesses adhere to it today. What is Suspicious Activity Reporting (SAR)? Suspicious Activity Reporting (SAR) is also often referred to as Suspicious Transaction Reporting (STR). The term is used to describe the process of reporting activities that are potentially indicative of criminal activity or terrorism to the appropriate authorities for further investigation. Suspicious Activity Reporting (SAR) is typically carried out by financial institutions, such as banks and credit unions, and involves the identification and reporting of suspicious transactions, activities, or behaviours that may be linked to money laundering, terrorism financing, or other criminal activities. The Suspicious Activity Reporting (SAR) process typically involves collecting information about suspicious activity, such as transaction details, account information, and other relevant data. This information is then analysed by trained professionals who determine whether the activity is suspicious and warrants further investigation. If the activity is deemed suspicious, a Suspicious Activity Report (SAR) is filed with the appropriate authorities, such as the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) in the United States and the Nigerian Financial Intelligence Unit (NFIU) in Nigeria. This is usually done in compliance with Anti-Money Laundering (AML) laws and regulations of countries. For more on AML Compliance, read: Ultimate Guide To KYC & AML Compliance. What Businesses are Required to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs)? According to the laws and regulations of different countries, various types of businesses are required to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) if they detect any transactions that may involve money laundering or other illegal activities. Here are some examples of businesses that must file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs): i. Financial Institutions Banks and other financial institutions are required to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) under the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) of the United States. These institutions are also subject to the anti-money laundering (AML) regulations of their regulatory agencies, such as the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN), for banks in Nigeria. ii. Casinos and Card Clubs Casinos and card clubs are required to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) under the Bank Secrecy Act of the United States and must comply with AML regulations. These businesses are regulated by the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) and state gaming commissions. iii. Securities and Futures Firms Securities and futures firms, such as brokerage firms and commodity trading advisors, are required to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) under the BSA and must comply with AML regulations. These firms are regulated by the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) and the Commodity Futures Trading Commission (CFTC) in the United States. iv. Jewellers and Precious Metals Dealers Jewellers and precious metals dealers are required to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) to the Special Control Unit Against Money Laundering (SCUML) in Nigeria if they engage in cash transactions above a certain threshold amount. What are the Record Keeping Requirements for Suspicious Activity Reporting (SAR)? It is a statutory requirement for organisations to preserve their Suspicious Activity Reports (SAR) for a period of time. The mandatory time frame for keeping Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) can vary depending on the jurisdiction and type of organisation filing the report. However, in the United States, under the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA), financial institutions must maintain a Suspicious Activity Report (SAR) and the supporting documentation for a period of five (5) years from the date of filing. It is important to note that the Suspicious Activity Report (SAR) filing and record-keeping requirements may also be subject to additional regulations or guidance from other regulatory bodies or government agencies, depending on the type of organisation and the nature of the suspicious activity being reported. The Special Control Unit Against Money Laundering (SCUML) of the Economic and Financial Crimes Commission (EFCC) in Nigeria is an example of such additional bodies. What are the Benefits of Filing Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs)? Filing Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) can provide several benefits, including: a. Preventing Financial Crimes Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) help banks and other financial institutions identify and report suspicious activities to law enforcement agencies, which can help prevent financial crimes such as money laundering, fraud, and terrorist financing. b. Compliance with Regulatory Requirements Financial institutions are required by law to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) in certain situations, such as when they suspect that a transaction involves illegal activity. Filing Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) helps institutions comply with these regulatory compliance requirements. c. Protection Against Legal Liability Failing to file a Suspicious Activity Report (SAR) when one is required can expose a financial institution to legal liability. By filing Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs), institutions can protect themselves against legal repercussions for failing to report suspicious activity. d. Enhanced Reputation Filing Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) demonstrates a financial institution's commitment to combating financial crime and protecting its customers. This can enhance the institution's reputation and help build trust with customers and stakeholders. e. Collaboration with Law Enforcement Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) can provide valuable information to law enforcement agencies that can help them investigate and prosecute financial crimes. By filing Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs), financial institutions can collaborate with law enforcement agencies to combat financial crime and contribute to the overall safety and security of the financial system. What are the Consequences of Failure to File Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs)? The consequences of failure to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) can be severe and may vary depending on the specific circumstances of the failure. Here are some possible consequences: i. Civil Penalties Financial institutions and their employees who fail to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) as required by law may face civil penalties. The penalties can range from monetary fines to regulatory sanctions. Recommended: Know Your Customer (KYC) Best Practices for Businesses Today ii. Criminal Penalties In certain cases, failure to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) can result in criminal charges against the financial institution and/or its employees. Criminal penalties can include fines and imprisonment. iii. Reputational Damage A failure to file a Suspicious Activity Report (SAR) can lead to reputational damage for the financial institution. This can result in a loss of trust from customers, investors, and regulators, which can have long-term negative impacts on the institution's business. iv. Regulatory Action Regulatory agencies may take action against financial institutions that fail to file SARs, including imposing fines, limiting business activities, or even revoking the institution's license to operate. v. Increased Risk Failing to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) can increase the risk of financial crime and money laundering, which can harm the institution's customers and the wider financial system. vi. Legal Liability Financial institutions and their employees may be held legally liable for damages caused by a failure to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs). This can result in lawsuits and significant financial settlements. In summary, failure to file Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) can have serious consequences, both for the financial institution and its employees, as well as for the wider financial system and society as a whole. It is important for financial institutions to take their reporting obligations seriously and ensure that they comply with all relevant laws and regulations. Conclusion It is important to note that not all businesses are subject to Suspicious Activity Reporting (SAR) requirements. However, many businesses (especially those in the financial services industry) are still subject to AML regulations and other reporting requirements, depending on their industry and the nature of their transactions. Suspicious Activity Reporting (SAR) is a key aspect of Anti-Money Laundering (AML) Compliance. Join 100 + leading global companies in using Youverify automatable compliance solutions for SAR reporting. Book a demo to get started.Related ArticlesAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Priscilla, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 28, 2023What is a Sanctions List?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Emmanuel Agwu•February 14, 2024RegTech's Influence On Regulatory Policy and ReformRead More","title":"Suspicious Activity Reporting: All You Need to Know","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/suspicious-activity-reporting","id":436},{"content_hash":"b7bbd3123f75a56b1bbe1521b95e143eb344fd4c1fa09669f13085a2db8f96ea","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:25.508215+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/kra-pin-checker-verify-kra-pin-online","language":"","text":"KRA PIN Checker: How to Verify a KRA PIN Online (and How Businesses Validate PINs at Onboarding)ByVictoria okereSeptember 19, 2026•5mins ReadKey TakeawaysThe KRA PIN Checker on iTax confirms whether a Personal Identification Number (PIN) was generated by KRA's system and is in Active status. It returns only basic taxpayer details. A Kenyan PIN has 11 characters: a leading letter, nine digits and a closing letter. Individual PINs generally begin with A, and company PINs generally begin with P. A PIN that looks correct, or passes the public checker, does not prove the customer owns it. It also does not prove the taxpayer is tax compliant. Manual checks break down at onboarding scale. Captcha-gated lookups, typing errors and forged documents make automated verification with recorded consent the safer route.","title":"KRA PIN Checker: How to Verify a KRA PIN Online (and How Businesses Validate PINs at Onboarding)","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/kra-pin-checker-verify-kra-pin-online","id":449},{"content_hash":"50f21eb782403cbcbb4923fc064ca25536da8ff84af7f33e3029ac4ccfcf3912","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:21.649353+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/how-to-renew-your-passport-online-in-south-africa","language":"","text":"How To Renew Your Passport Online in South AfricaByEmmanuel AgwuJune 27, 2023•5mins ReadWhy You Should Renew Your Passport Online in South AfricaDocuments You Will Need To Renew Your Passport Online in South AfricaSteps To Take To Renew Your South African Passport OnlineBottom LineShare this postRenewing your passport is a necessary process that allows you to continue your international travel without any hiccups. In South Africa, the Department of Home Affairs has made it convenient for citizens to renew their passports online. While you can use the traditional methods to renew your passport in South Africa, this article will provide you with a detailed guide on how to renew your passport online, ensuring a smooth and hassle-free experience. Why You Should Renew Your Passport Online in South Africa Renewing your passport is an essential task that allows you to continue your international travel smoothly. Renewing your passport online in South Africa can be a preferable option compared to the traditional in-person method for the following reasons. 1. Time-saving and Convenience: Renewing your passport online eliminates the need for multiple visits to Home Affairs offices. With the online platform, you can complete the renewal process from the comfort of your home or office, at any time that suits you. This saves you valuable time and effort, as there is no need to stand in long queues or take time off work to visit a Home Affairs office. 2. User-friendly Interface: The online platform developed by the Department of Home Affairs, known as eHomeAffairs, provides a user-friendly interface that guides you through the renewal process step by step. The system is designed to be intuitive and straightforward, making it accessible to individuals with varying levels of technological expertise. 3. Document Upload and Verification: Renewing your passport online allows you to upload the necessary documents electronically. This eliminates the need to make physical copies or carry multiple documents to a Home Affairs office. The system provides clear guidelines on document specifications, ensuring that you submit the required information accurately. It also facilitates quick verification of the uploaded documents, reducing the chances of errors or delays in the renewal process. 4. Efficient Communication and Tracking: The passport renewal south africa online system offers efficient communication channels between applicants and the Department of Home Affairs. Throughout the process, you will receive regular updates on the status of your application, including notifications regarding document verification, payment confirmation, and passport collection details. This ensures transparency and enables you to track the progress of your renewal application conveniently. 5. Flexible Payment Options: Renewing your passport online allows for secure and convenient online payment methods. The eHomeAffairs platform accepts various payment options, including credit and debit cards, providing flexibility and convenience. This eliminates the need to carry cash or make payment arrangements in person. Read: How to Get a Driver's License in South Africa Documents You Will Need To Renew Your Passport Online in South Africa Certain things must be in place before you attempt to renew your South African passport online. These include certain documents. They are: a. Current valid South African passport You must have your existing passport, which is still valid or has expired within the last six months. b. Original identification document (ID) or Smart ID Card You will need your valid South African green bar-coded ID book or the new Smart ID Card. c. Two recent colour passport-sized photographs You need two identical, colour passport-sized photographs that meet the specific requirements set by the Department of Home Affairs. The photographs should be taken against a plain white background without shadows or reflections. It's important to note that these are the general documents required for passport renewal in South Africa online. However, specific situations or circumstances may require additional documents or supporting evidence. It is advisable to consult the Department of Home Affairs website or contact them directly for any further clarification or specific requirements based on your individual circumstances. Read: Types of Passport in Nigeria Steps To Take To Renew Your South African Passport Online Step 1: Gather the required documents Before starting the online passport renewal process, ensure you have the following documents ready:Current valid South African passportOriginal identification document (ID) or Smart ID CardTwo recent colour passport-sized photographs Step 2: Access the Department of Home Affairs eHomeAffairs website .Visit the official eHomeAffairs website of the Department of Home Affairs (www.dha.gov.za) using a computer or smartphone with an internet connection. This platform offers a user-friendly interface for passport renewals. Step 3: Create an eHomeAffairs account If you are a first-time user, you will need to create an account on the eHomeAffairs website. Click on the \"New Client\" button and provide the required details, including your personal information, contact details, and a valid email address. Create a secure password for your account. Step 4: Login and select \"Passport Application\" Once you have created your account, login using your credentials. On the dashboard, locate the \"Passport Application\" option and click on it to proceed with your passport renewal. Step 5: Complete the online passport application formFill in the required information accurately on the online passport application form. You will need to provide personal details, such as your full name, date of birth, and contact information. Ensure all information matches your current passport and identification document. Step 6: Upload the necessary documents.Scan or take clear photos of your current valid passport, identification document, and passport-sized photographs. Upload these documents in the specified sections of the online application form. Ensure that the scanned copies are legible and meet the specified requirements. Step 7: Choose a convenient collection point.Select a Home Affairs office or South African mission abroad from the available options for passport collection. Choose a location that is convenient for you to collect your renewed passport once it is ready. Step 8: Make the payment onlineProceed to the payment section of the online application. The passport renewal fee can be paid online using a credit or debit card. Ensure that you have a valid payment method available. Step 9: Submit your application.Review all the information provided on the online application form to ensure accuracy. Once you are satisfied, click on the \"Submit\" button to finalize your passport renewal application. Step 10: Track your application.After submitting your application, you will receive an acknowledgment SMS or email with a reference number. Use this reference number to track the progress of your passport renewal online. The Department of Home Affairs provides regular updates on the status of your application. Step 11: Collect your renewed passport:Once your passport renewal is processed, you will receive a notification indicating that it is ready for collection. Visit the selected Home Affairs office or South African mission abroad to collect your new passport. Bring your original identification document and the collection slip or reference number. Bottom Line Renewing your passport online in South Africa has become a straightforward and efficient process thanks to the Department of Home Affairs' eHomeAffairs platform. It offers a range of benefits, including time-saving, convenience, user-friendly interfaces, document upload and verification, efficient communication, and flexible payment options. Embracing online renewal not only streamlines the process for individuals but also contributes to overall efficiency and effectiveness in managing passport services. By following the step-by-step guide provided in this article, you can ensure a smooth and convenient passport renewal experience. Remember to plan ahead and allow sufficient time for the renewal process to avoid any last-minute complications before your travel plans. See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for KYC and AML screening of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today. Related ArticlesView All PostsIdentity DocumentsEmmanuel Agwu•May 26, 2023How to Perform Driver's License Verification OnlineRead MoreIdentity DocumentsEmmanuel Agwu•June 6, 2023How To Renew Your Driver's License In South AfricaRead MoreIdentity DocumentsPrince, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•March 15, 2023What are the Acceptable Employment Verification Documents? Read More","title":"How To Renew Your Passport  Online in South Africa","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/how-to-renew-your-passport-online-in-south-africa","id":469},{"content_hash":"f21e26629bfd6c153d49cc3e220f71fabea1d21b173ee58172bbabc1aa71b94f","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:20.225154+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/13/just-army-debunks-abduction/","language":"","text":"JUST IN: Army Debunks Abduction of Islamic Scholars at Yariâs Residence in Zamfara By Prnigeria - September 13, 2026 Nigerian Army JUST IN: Army Debunks Abduction of Islamic Scholars at Yariâs Residence in Zamfara The Nigerian Army has refuted reports alleging that a group of Islamic scholars was abducted at the residence of former Zamfara State Governor and Senator representing Zamfara West, Abdulâaziz Abubakar Yari, in Talata Mafara Local Government Area of the state. The Army described the reports circulating on various media platforms as misleading, saying no Islamic cleric was abducted at the senatorâs residence. The clarification was contained in a situation report issued on Sunday by the Acting Deputy Director, Army Public Relations, 8 Division, Nigerian Army and Sector 2, Operation FANSAN YAMMA, Lt. Col. Olaniyi Osoba. According to the statement, intelligence reports indicated that some persons were abducted while travelling from the Sububu forest axis in Bakura Local Government Area towards Talata Mafara. Read Also: 2027: Former Gombe Electoral Commissioner Endorses Pantami for Governor NWDC, Kano Task Force Move to Tackle Drug Abuse, Insecurity in North-West Nigeria Has Lost Elder Statesman, Tinubu Mourns Bamanga Tukur It stressed that the incident did not occur at Yariâs residence, contrary to claims circulating online. âThe narrative that the abduction took place at the Senator’s residence in Talata Mafara is entirely false,â the Army stated. The military said troops, working in close coordination with sister security agencies and local intelligence networks, had intensified efforts to locate and rescue the victims. It urged members of the public to disregard sensationalised reports and unverified information capable of causing unnecessary panic or compromising ongoing tactical operations. The Army also appealed to residents to remain calm and vigilant while providing security agencies with timely and actionable intelligence to support efforts to rescue the abducted persons and address the security threat. The clarification comes amid heightened security concerns in parts of Zamfara State, where criminal groups have continued to carry out kidnapping and other violent activities. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSAbductionAbdulaziz YariarmyBanditsFansan YammaIslamic Clericszamfara Previous article2027: Former Gombe Electoral Commissioner Endorses Pantami for Governor Prnigeria","title":"JUST IN: Army Debunks Abduction of Islamic Scholars at Yariâs Residence in Zamfara","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/13/just-army-debunks-abduction/","id":479},{"content_hash":"86bc7ce98b6f22c732fe2de3b20550d548c029504662294e274dec53c2e2f8e3","domain":"son.gov.ng","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:19.117574+00:00","final_url":"https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/21/son-retirees-commend-dg-son-for-payment-of-benefits/","language":"","text":"DG SON and SON Retirees in a Group PhotopreghCross Section of the Retirees at the EventSON Retirees at the MeetingOther Retirees Present SON RETIREES COMMEND DG SON FOR PAYMENT OF BENEFITS Retirees of the Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON) recently visited the Director General and Chief Executive, Dr. Ifeanyi C. Okeke, at the Operational Headquarters in Lagos to express their appreciation for his intervention in facilitating the payment of their retirement benefits. Speaking on behalf of the group, Dr. (Mrs.) Margaret Eshiet, former Director and Codex Contact Point, commended the DG for his leadership and described the gesture as timely and impactful. She noted that the disbursement of benefits had restored dignity to the retirees and demonstrated a high level of empathy and administrative responsibility. In his remarks, Dr. Okeke thanked the retirees for their service and reiterated his commitment to fairness and equity in the treatment of all staff—past and present. He emphasized that the payment of entitlements is not merely a statutory obligation, but a moral duty to honour the sacrifices made by those who contributed to the organisation’s growth. He urged current staff to draw inspiration from the retirees’ dedication and integrity, reminding them that the legacy they leave behind will define how they are remembered. Also speaking at the event, Engr. Richard Adewumi, former Director of Laboratory Services, presented a token of appreciation to the Director General and led a prayer on behalf of the group.","title":"SON Retirees Commend DG SON for Payment of Benefits","url":"https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/21/son-retirees-commend-dg-son-for-payment-of-benefits/","id":486},{"content_hash":"cdb3838cfc0f61978e012941cc1fa4eb22fa043c5d420c65d4ea10ce05bda725","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:17.812748+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/how-to-change-bvn-phone-number","language":"","text":"How To Change BVN Phone Number in NigeriaByEmmanuel AgwuMay 22, 2023•5mins ReadKey TakeawaysChanging your BVN phone number requires an in-person bank visit with a valid ID, your BVN, and your new SIM. You cannot update your BVN phone number online, through a mobile app, or via USSD.The CBN now allows BVN phone number changes only once in a lifetime, making it essential to choose a permanent phone number that is registered in your name and will remain active for the long term.Your 11-digit BVN number cannot be changed, but certain BVN details can be updated, including your phone number, name, or date of birth, subject to CBN rules and supporting documentation.Protect your BVN from fraud by avoiding unofficial BVN update websites and agents, keeping your registered SIM active, and reporting lost SIM cards immediately to reduce the risk of SIM swap and identity fraud. Important: The CBN BVN Phone Number Update Now Once-in-a-Lifetime RuleCan You Change Your BVN Phone Number Online?Can You Change Your BVN Number Itself?BVN Update Process: What You Can and Cannot UpdateIs there a BVN self-service portal or app?What You Need to Change or Update Your BVN Phone NumberHow to Change Your BVN Phone Number: Step by StepHow to Link a New Phone Number to Your BVNHow Long Does It Take to Change or Update a BVN Phone Number?Can I Change My BVN Phone Number at Any Bank?How to Change Your BVN Phone Number at GTBank, First Bank, UBA and Other BanksHow to Change the Name or Date of Birth on Your BVNHow to Check Which Phone Number Is Linked to Your BVNProtect Yourself From BVN FraudBVN Verification for BusinessesShare this post To change your BVN phone number, you must visit a branch of your bank in person with a valid ID, your BVN, and proof of the new number. You cannot change your BVN phone number online, by app, or over the phone. Since 1 May 2026, the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) allows you to change the phone number on your BVN only once in your lifetime, so the number you switch to should be one you intend to keep permanently. check your BVN on Access Bank Important: The CBN BVN Phone Number Update Now Once-in-a-Lifetime Rule This is the single most important thing to know before you start.In a circular issued to banks on 13 March 2026 and effective from 1 May 2026, the CBN restricted BVN phone number updates to once in a lifetime. The rule applies across every bank, fintech, microfinance bank and mobile money operator in Nigeria, and affects all BVN holders. NIBSS recorded BVN enrolment at 68.59 million as of March 2026. What this means for you in practice: If you have never changed your BVN phone number, you have one change available. Use it carefully.Once you use it, the number is locked. Any further change requires exceptional regulatory approval, involving extensive documentation and verification.Choose a number you control personally and intend to keep for years. Do not use a work line, a borrowed phone, or a number tied to a network you plan to leave.Before you change anything, confirm the new SIM is registered in your own name and is active. Why the CBN did this. The linked phone number is used for one-time passwords (OTPs), transaction alerts and account recovery, which makes it the main target in SIM-swap fraud. A fraudster swaps your SIM, requests a BVN phone number change, then resets your banking credentials and drains the account. NIBSS recorded 62,901 fraud cases linked to social engineering schemes in 2023, many involving compromised phone numbers. Capping changes closes that route. Other BVN changes that took effect alongside it. The CBN also directed banks to operate a temporary watchlist, where a BVN linked to suspicious activity can be flagged for up to 24 hours while the bank contacts you to verify a transaction. BVN enrolment is also restricted to individuals aged 18 and above. Can You Change Your BVN Phone Number Online? No. There is no official online portal, mobile app, or USSD code that lets you change your BVN phone number online. Any website or app claiming to do it is not legitimate, and you should not enter your BVN or bank details there.The change must be done in person at a bank branch. This is deliberate, for four reasons: 1. Identity verification: The bank must confirm the person requesting the change is the actual BVN holder, which requires physical ID checks and often biometric confirmation.2. Fraud prevention:An online change route would be exactly the weakness SIM-swap fraudsters exploit. In-person verification removes it.3. Regulatory compliance.Nigerian banks must meet CBN requirements on customer identification and fraud prevention, and in-person verification is how they evidence it.4. Data protection: Your BVN links to every bank account you hold. Requiring physical presence limits unauthorised access to that record.Many people ask how to change a BVN phone number without going to the bank. There is currently no legitimate way to do this. If your bank offers to start the request through its app or customer service line, you will still be required to complete it at a branch. Can You Change Your BVN Number Itself? No, and this is a different question worth separating clearly. People often ask can BVN be changed, or how to change my BVN number, when what they actually need is a phone number update.Your BVN number is permanent. The 11-digit BVN issued when you enrolled cannot be changed, replaced, renewed or reissued. It is your identifier across every bank in Nigeria for life.You cannot have two BVNs. Enrolling more than once is not permitted, and duplicate BVNs are flagged and merged or deactivated. If you believe you have two, report it to your bank rather than using both.What you can change is the information attached to your BVN, such as your phone number, name, or date of birth, subject to the rules and limits below.So, to answer directly: can I change my BVN number? No. Can I change my BVN phone number? Yes, once in a lifetime. BVN Update Process: What You Can and Cannot Update The BVN update process and BVN modification cover several different requests, and they follow different rules. This table sets out what is possible. What you want to updatePossible?HowLimitPhone number on your BVNYesIn person at a bank branchOnce in a lifetime since 1 May 2026Name or spelling correctionYesIn person, with documentary evidenceReviewed case by caseDate of birth correctionYes, restrictedIn person, with birth certificate or age declarationStrictly limited, repeat changes not permittedAddress or contact detailsYesIn person at your bankNo published limitThe 11-digit BVN number itselfNoNot possiblePermanent for lifeHolding a second BVNNoNot permittedDuplicates are merged or deactivated Is there a BVN self-service portal or app? No. There is no NIBSS or CBN BVN portal, app, or website that lets you modify your BVN details yourself. NIBSS operates the NIBSS BVN validation portal infrastructure for banks, not a public modification service. Any app or website offering BVN self-service modification is not legitimate, and entering your BVN there puts your bank accounts at risk.Every BVN modification runs through a licensed bank, in person, with identity verification. If you are told otherwise by a message, social media post, or someone offering to do it for a fee, treat it as fraud.What \"modify your BVN\" means when your bank asks. If a bank prompts you to modify or update your BVN, it usually means the details on your BVN record no longer match the details on your bank account, most often the phone number. Banks require these to match before granting mobile banking access. Resolving it means visiting a branch to align the two records. What You Need to Change or Update Your BVN Phone Number If you are wondering what do I need to change my BVN phone number, bring all of the following to the branch. Missing one item is the most common reason people are turned away and have to return. A valid government-issued ID. An international passport, driver's licence, National ID card (NIN slip or card), or voter's card.Your BVN. Bring your BVN acknowledgement slip if you still have it. If not, you can retrieve your BVN by dialling 5650# from the phone currently linked to it.The new SIM card, active and in your name. Some banks ask for proof of ownership, such as the SIM pack or a printout from your network showing the number is registered to you.Your account details for the bank where you are making the request.The old phone number if you still have access to it, since some banks send a confirmation OTP to the existing number as part of verification. You will complete a BVN update or change request form at the branch, and you may be asked to redo biometric capture, meaning fingerprints and a photograph, to authenticate the change against your BVN record. How to Change Your BVN Phone Number: Step by Step Follow these steps in order to change the phone number on your BVN.Confirm you have not already used your one change. If you have changed your BVN phone number before, ask your bank whether a further change is possible before making the trip.Register and activate the new SIM in your own name. Complete NIN-SIM linkage with your network first, since a number not registered to you will fail verification.Visit a branch of your bank in person. Go to the bank where you hold your primary account. Some banks require the branch where your BVN was originally enrolled, so call ahead to confirm.Request the BVN update or change request form at the customer service or BVN desk.Complete the form accurately, entering your BVN, your current registered number, and the new number exactly.Submit your ID and supporting documents, including proof of ownership of the new number if requested.Complete biometric verification if the bank requires it, usually fingerprints and a photograph.Collect your acknowledgement or reference for the request before leaving, so you can follow up.Confirm the update has gone through after a few working days by checking that OTPs and transaction alerts arrive on the new number. How to Link a New Phone Number to Your BVN Linking a new number to your BVN and changing your BVN phone number are the same process. There is no separate procedure: whether you search how to link my BVN to my new number or how to change it, the bank updates the single phone number field on your BVN record, replacing the old number with the new one. This matters because your BVN holds one phone number, not several. You cannot link two numbers to one BVN, and you cannot add a second number while keeping the first. Linking a new number means replacing the existing one, which is why it counts against the CBN's once-in-a-lifetime limit. To link your new number to your BVN:Register and activate the new SIM in your own name, completing NIN-SIM linkage with your network.Visit your bank branch in person with a valid ID and your BVN.Request the BVN update form and enter the new number you want linked.Complete identity and biometric verification.Confirm after a few working days that OTPs and alerts now arrive on the new number. One point often confused: linking your BVN to a bank account is a different task entirely, and that one can be done remotely. Most banks let you link an existing BVN to an account by USSD, such as GTBank's 73720*BVN#, or through internet banking. That process attaches your BVN to an account. It does not change the phone number stored on the BVN itself, which still requires a branch visit. How Long Does It Take to Change or Update a BVN Phone Number? How long it takes to change a BVN phone number depends on your bank. Most complete the update within a few working days of a correctly submitted request, and some process it within 24 to 48 hours. Timing depends on internal verification and how quickly the change reflects in the NIBSS BVN database. If OTPs and alerts are still going to your old number after about five working days, return to the branch with your acknowledgement slip and ask them to confirm the update was submitted and reflected. Can I Change My BVN Phone Number at Any Bank? Not necessarily. Many people ask can I change my BVN phone number in any bank, and the safest answer is to go to the bank where you hold your primary account, ideally the bank where your BVN was originally enrolled. Because your BVN is shared across all Nigerian banks, an update made by one bank updates the central NIBSS record. However, individual banks set their own internal rules about which branches can process BVN modifications, and some will only handle it where the BVN was enrolled or where you hold an active account. Call your bank's customer service line before travelling to a branch. How to Change Your BVN Phone Number at GTBank, First Bank, UBA and Other Banks Whether you are searching how to change my BVN phone number online GTB, First Bank, or UBA, the process is broadly identical at every Nigerian bank, because the rules are set by the CBN and NIBSS rather than by individual banks. In every case the change is made in person at a branch, using a BVN update form, valid ID, and biometric verification where required. No major Nigerian bank offers a fully online BVN phone number change. What differs between banks is only the practical detail: which branches process BVN modifications, whether they require the branch of original enrolment, and what proof of new-number ownership they ask for. GTBank, for example, directs customers to visit a branch to update a registered phone number. The reliable step is to call your bank's official customer service line first and confirm the branch and documents before you travel. Use the number printed on your debit card or on the bank's official website, never a number from a social media post or WhatsApp message. What If You No Longer Have Access to Your Old Number? If you lost the SIM linked to your BVN, this is now a more serious problem than it used to be, because of the once-in-a-lifetime cap. Your first step is to try to recover the same number from your network operator. Most Nigerian networks will reissue a SIM with your existing number if you visit their office with the ID used to register it. Recovering the original number avoids using up your single BVN change entirely. Only if the number is genuinely unrecoverable should you use your one lifetime change. In that case, take your ID and a police report if the phone was stolen, and explain the situation at your bank branch. Because you will be verified without an OTP to your old number, expect stricter identity checks, including biometrics. How to Change the Name or Date of Birth on Your BVN Yes, you can. How to change your BVN name and how to change your BVN date of birth are a separate process from a phone number change, known as BVN modification, and both are handled in person at a bank branch. For a BVN name change or correction, bring supporting evidence of the correct name, such as a birth certificate, sworn affidavit, marriage certificate for a change of surname, or newspaper publication where required. Banks will not amend a name without documentary proof. For a BVN date of birth correction, banks apply strict limits, because date of birth is a core identity field used in verification. Bring your birth certificate or age declaration and a valid ID. Expect the request to be reviewed rather than processed on the spot, and be aware that repeated date of birth changes are not permitted. In both cases you complete a BVN modification form at the branch and may need to redo biometric capture. Processing usually takes longer than a phone number change, since the request is reviewed against your submitted evidence. How to Check Which Phone Number Is Linked to Your BVN Before changing anything, confirm which number is currently on your BVN record. This answers how to know the number linked to my BVN. Dial *565*0# from the phone number you believe is linked. If the BVN is returned, that number is the one on file. If nothing comes back, that is not the registered number. You can also ask at your bank branch, or check within your bank's mobile app profile section where your registered contact details are displayed. Never use third-party websites or apps that ask for your BVN to check your details, as these are a common phishing route. BVN guides:How to Check BVN on Access BankHow to Check BVN on First BankHow to Check BVN on MTN, Glo, 9mobile and AirtelHow to Check My BVN on GTBank Mobile App. Protect Yourself From BVN Fraud Your BVN is the backbone of your banking identity, and fraudsters know it.Never share your BVN with anyone who contacts you unsolicited. Legitimate banks do not call and ask for it.No one should charge you to change your BVN phone number. The process at your bank does not require paying an agent.Ignore \"BVN update\" links sent by SMS, WhatsApp, or social media. There is no legitimate online BVN modification portal.Report a lost SIM immediately to both your network and your bank, since a SIM swap is the first step in most account takeovers.Keep your registered number active. A number deactivated for long inactivity can be recycled to another user, which creates a serious security exposure on your bank accounts. BVN Verification for Businesses For banks, fintechs and other regulated businesses, the CBN's tightening of BVN rules raises the bar on customer verification. A phone number that no longer belongs to the customer, or a BVN record that does not match the person presenting it, is exactly the gap fraudsters exploit during onboarding. Youverify provides BVN verification and identity verification built for Nigeria and the wider African market, letting compliance teams confirm in real time that a BVN is valid, that it belongs to the customer presenting it, and that the associated details match official records. This supports KYC and AML obligations while keeping onboarding fast. Top 6 Identity Verification Methods for Businesses Book a demo to see how Youverify handles BVN verification at scale. FAQsFrequently Asked Questions1. Can I change my BVN phone number online?No. There is no official online portal, app, or USSD code for changing the phone number linked to your BVN. The change must be made in person at a bank branch with a valid ID, because the bank has to verify your identity and often your biometrics. Any website or app offering an online BVN phone number change is not legitimate. 2. How many times can I change my BVN phone number?Once. Since 1 May 2026, a CBN directive limits BVN phone number updates to once in a lifetime, across all banks, fintechs and mobile money operators in Nigeria. After you use that single change, further updates require exceptional regulatory approval with extensive documentation, so choose a number you intend to keep permanently. 3. Can I change my BVN number itself?No. Your 11-digit BVN is permanent and cannot be changed, replaced or reissued. It identifies you across every bank in Nigeria for life. You also cannot hold two BVNs. What you can update is the information attached to it, such as your phone number, name or date of birth. 4. How do I link my new phone number to my BVN?Linking a new number to your BVN is the same process as changing it. Your BVN stores one phone number only, so linking a new one replaces the existing one and counts against your single lifetime change. Visit your bank branch in person with a valid ID, your BVN, and the new SIM registered in your name, then complete the BVN update form and identity verification. Linking your BVN to a bank account is a separate task that can often be done by USSD or internet banking. 5. Is there a BVN self-service portal or app?No. There is no official NIBSS or CBN self-service portal, app, or website that lets you modify your BVN details. NIBSS runs BVN validation infrastructure for banks, not a public modification service. Every BVN update must go through a licensed bank in person. Any app or site offering BVN self-service modification is not legitimate and should not be given your BVN. 6. What does the BVN update process involve?You complete a BVN update or modification form at a bank branch, submit a valid government-issued ID and supporting documents, and complete biometric verification if the bank requires it. Phone number updates are capped at once in a lifetime since 1 May 2026. Name and date of birth corrections need documentary evidence such as a birth certificate, sworn affidavit or marriage certificate, and are reviewed rather than processed immediately. Your 11-digit BVN number itself can never be changed. 7. What do I need to change my BVN phone number?Bring a valid government-issued ID such as an international passport, driver's licence, National ID or voter's card, your BVN or acknowledgement slip, and the new SIM card registered in your own name. Some banks also ask for proof of ownership of the new number and may require biometric verification at the branch. 8. How long does it take to change a BVN phone number?Most banks complete the update within a few working days, and some within 24 to 48 hours, depending on internal verification and how quickly the change reflects in the NIBSS database. If alerts are still going to your old number after about five working days, return to the branch with your acknowledgement slip. 9. Can I change my BVN phone number at any bank?Go to the bank where you hold your primary account, ideally the one where your BVN was enrolled. Although the update reflects across all banks through the central NIBSS record, individual banks set their own rules about which branches handle BVN modifications. Call your bank's official customer service line to confirm before travelling. 10. How do I know which phone number is linked to my BVN?Dial *5650# from the number you think is linked. If your BVN is returned, that is the registered number. You can also confirm at your bank branch or in your bank's mobile app profile section. Never use third-party websites that ask for your BVN to check details, as these are commonly used for phishing. 11. What if I lost the SIM linked to my BVN?Try to recover the same number from your network operator first, by visiting their office with the ID used to register the SIM. Recovering the original number avoids using your single lifetime BVN change. If the number is genuinely unrecoverable, visit your bank with your ID, and a police report if the phone was stolen, and expect stricter identity checks including biometrics.Related ArticlesView All PostsIdentity DocumentsEmmanuel Agwu•May 26, 2023How to Perform Driver's License Verification OnlineRead MoreIdentity DocumentsEmmanuel Agwu•June 6, 2023How To Renew Your Driver's License In South AfricaRead MoreIdentity DocumentsPrince, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•March 15, 2023What are the Acceptable Employment Verification Documents? Read More","title":"How To Change BVN Phone Number in Nigeria","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/how-to-change-bvn-phone-number","id":491},{"content_hash":"bef5dc542caf144764b32bddc69ea01fa650204ee94a97c89cbd9874fa6c3e06","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:15.929218+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/13/nigeria-benin-customs-move/","language":"","text":"Nigeria, Benin Customs Move to Modernise Seme-Krake Border for Seamless Trade By Prnigeria - September 13, 2026 Comptroller-General of Customs, Adewale Adeniyi, and the Director-General of Benin Republic Customs, Col. Raouf Aboudou Nigeria, Benin Customs Move to Modernise Seme-Krake Border for Seamless Trade The Nigeria Customs Service (NCS) and the Benin Republic Customs Administration have stepped up efforts to facilitate seamless cross-border trade with a joint assessment of the Seme-Krake border towards establishing a modernised One-Stop Border Post. The assessment, conducted on Friday, September 11, 2026, was led by the Comptroller-General of Customs, Adewale Adeniyi, and the Director-General of Benin Republic Customs, Col. Raouf Aboudou. Speaking during the assessment, Adeniyi said the initiative reflected a new level of trust and confidence between Nigeria and Benin Republic, while positioning their Customs administrations to play a leading role in advancing Africa’s trade integration agenda. He said the initiative was anchored on the need for regulators to build the same level of trust that traders had developed in conducting business across national borders. “Today’s assessment visit is the first major step we are taking in the manifestation of that trust,” Adeniyi said. He explained that both countries had developed an interoperable computer system designed to connect their customs processes and facilitate transactions involving traders operating with the Nigerian naira and the West African CFA franc. Read Also: JUST IN: Army Debunks Abduction of Islamic Scholars at Yari’s Residence in Zamfara 2027: Former Gombe Electoral Commissioner Endorses Pantami for Governor NWDC, Kano Task Force Move to Tackle Drug Abuse, Insecurity in North-West According to him, effective connectivity between the systems would make cross-border transactions more seamless and create a foundation for harmonising other trade policies between the two countries. “If we get the connectivity of our system right, we can build every other thing on top of this. We can begin to harmonise our prohibition lists. And that will be the beginning of a more meaningful economic integration between our economies,” he said. The Comptroller-General said the initiative would deepen cooperation between the two Customs administrations and help reduce administrative barriers that could hinder legitimate trade along the corridor. Aboudou described Adeniyi as a pragmatic leader and said the Joint Border Modernisation Initiative was consistent with coordinated risk management, which he identified as one of the core values promoted by the World Customs Organisation. Also speaking, the Customs Area Controller, Seme Area Command, Comptroller Abdullahi Kaila, said the assessment came at a critical time for the sub-region’s busiest trade corridor. Kaila said a fully functional One-Stop Border Post would eliminate procedural bottlenecks, reduce transit times and enhance transparency in cross-border trade. He assured the visiting delegation that the Seme Area Command was prepared to support the initiative and contribute to its successful implementation. The joint assessment is expected to strengthen cooperation between Nigeria and Benin Republic on border management while creating a more efficient and integrated framework for legitimate trade and economic activities along the Seme-Krake corridor. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSBenin Republic Customs AdministrationModernise Seme-Krake BorderNigeria Customs Service Previous articleJUST IN: Army Debunks Abduction of Islamic Scholars at Yari’s Residence in Zamfara Prnigeria","title":"Nigeria, Benin Customs Move to Modernise Seme-Krake Border for Seamless Trade","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/13/nigeria-benin-customs-move/","id":500},{"content_hash":"3c5f7520d4fc2191acc482c36b757bae08e0fa4cccc4dff7ee7317f58aa00217","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:14.166862+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/fintech-fraud-prevention","language":"","text":"The Latest Fraud Prevention Technique in Fintech IndustriesByTemitope LawalJuly 3, 2024•5mins Read5 Techniques for FinTech Fraud Prevention4. Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)5. Regulatory Compliance and StandardsThe Future of Fraud Prevention in FintechPossible Challenges to the Use of Technology for FinTech Fraud PreventionConclusionShare this post The Fintech industry has witnessed explosive growth in recent years. According to a report by Statista, the global Fintech market is expected to reach a market value of $305 billion by 2025. This rapid expansion has been driven by the increasing adoption of digital payments, mobile banking, and online lending platforms.While the benefits of Fintech are undeniable, the industry is also a prime target for cybercriminals. The Association of Certified Fraud Examiners (ACFE) estimates that organizations lose 5% of their revenue to fraud each year, with financial services being particularly vulnerable. As Fintech continues to grow, the industry must stay ahead of fraudsters by implementing advanced Fintech fraud prevention techniques. We shall address 5 ways of preventing fraud in the FinTech industry. 5 Techniques for FinTech Fraud Prevention 1. Machine Learning and Artificial IntelligenceOne of the most significant advancements in fraud prevention is the use of machine learning (ML) and artificial intelligence (AI). These technologies enable Fintech companies to analyze vast amounts of data in real-time, identifying patterns and anomalies that may indicate fraudulent activity. They are: i. Predictive Analytics: By learning from past fraud patterns, ML Algorithm systems can detect suspicious behavior before it results in financial loss. For instance, if a user’s spending habit suddenly changes; an AI system can flag this as potential fraud and trigger further investigation. ii. Real-Time Monitoring: AI-powered systems can monitor transactions in real-time, providing instant alerts when suspicious activity is detected. This allows financial institutions to respond quickly and prevent fraudulent transactions from being processed. iii. Behavioral Biometrics: This technology analyzes users’ behavior, such as typing speed, mouse movements, and touch screen interactions, to create a unique user profile. Any deviation from this profile can indicate potential fraud. For example, if a user’s login pattern suddenly changes, it may be a sign that their account has been compromised. 2. Blockchain TechnologyBlockchain technology, known for its role in cryptocurrencies, also offers significant fraud prevention benefits for Fintech:i. Immutable Ledger: Every transaction on a blockchain is recorded in a public ledger that is immutable, meaning it cannot be altered or deleted. This transparency makes it difficult for fraudsters to manipulate transaction records without being detected.ii. Smart Contracts: These contracts automatically enforce and verify the conditions of a transaction, ensuring that all parties adhere to the agreed-upon terms, thus contributing to fraud prevention.iii. Decentralization: By decentralizing transaction records across a network of computers, blockchain reduces the risk of a single point of failure or attack. This makes it harder for cybercriminals to compromise the system and commit fraud. You may find this post on how blockchain technology has impacted AML compliance informative. 3. Biometric AuthenticationTraditional methods, such as passwords and PINs, are increasingly vulnerable to cyber-attacks. On the other hand, biometric authentication offers a more secure alternative through the use of unique physical characteristics to verify identity. i. Fingerprint Scanning: Many mobile banking apps and digital wallets now use fingerprint scanning to authenticate users. This method is more secure than passwords, as fingerprints are unique to each individual and difficult to replicate. ii. Facial Recognition: Advanced facial recognition technology can accurately verify a user’s identity based on their facial features. This method helps tighten security during login and transaction approval processes. iii. Voice Recognition: Some Fintech applications use voice recognition to authenticate users. This technology analyzes unique vocal patterns and can be used for secure access to accounts and transactions. 4. Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)Multi-factor authentication (MFA) requires users to provide multiple forms of identification before they can have access to their accounts or complete transactions. MFA typically involves a combination of:Something You Know: This may include passwords or PINs.Something You Have: This could be a physical device, such as a smartphone or hardware token that generates a one-time code.Something You Are: This could be a biometric factor, such as a fingerprint or facial recognition.By combining these factors, MFA significantly reduces the risk of unauthorized access, as it is much harder for fraudsters to compromise all required elements. 5. Regulatory Compliance and StandardsRegulatory compliance plays a crucial role in ensuring that Fintech companies implement robust fraud prevention measures. Various regulations and standards guide the industry’s approach to fraud prevention: i. Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS): This standard prevents payment card fraud by outlining security measures for handling cardholder data. ii. General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR): GDPR mandates strict data protection measures for companies handling personal data of EU citizens. Compliance with GDPR helps prevent identity theft and data breaches.iii. Anti-Money Laundering (AML) Regulations: AML regulations require financial institutions to implement measures to detect and prevent money laundering activities. Compliance helps Fintech companies identify and block suspicious transactions.Above all, we have curated a list of financial fraud detection and prevention best practices which you will find very helpful. Related: Are High Security Checks Worth it? The Future of Fraud Prevention in Fintech As the Fintech industry continues to evolve, so too will the techniques and technologies used to prevent fraud. Here are some emerging trends and future directions in fraud prevention: 1. Quantum ComputingWhile still in its nascent stages, quantum computing holds the potential to revolutionize fraud prevention. Quantum computers can process vast amounts of data at unprecedented speeds, enabling real-time detection and prevention of complex fraudulent activities. 2. Quantum CryptographyQuantum cryptography offers a new level of security through principles like quantum key distribution (QKD), which ensures that any attempt to intercept communication would be immediately detectable. This technology could provide Fintech companies with unbreakable encryption methods, significantly enhancing transaction security. 3. Decentralized Identity SolutionsDecentralized identity solutions, powered by blockchain, allow individuals to own and control their digital identities. These solutions reduce the risk of identity theft and fraud by eliminating central points of failure. 4. Self-Sovereign Identity (SSI)SSI enables users to manage their own identity data through a decentralized system. This approach ensures that personal information is not stored in a single, vulnerable location but distributed across a secure network. Fintech companies can leverage SSI to enhance user authentication and reduce the risk of identity fraud. Possible Challenges to the Use of Technology for FinTech Fraud Prevention 1. Balancing Security and User ExperienceFinding the right balance between robust security measures and a seamless user experience can be a significant challenge. While overly stringent security protocols can frustrate users, lax security measures can leave systems vulnerable to fraud.As a solution to this, Fintech companies are increasingly adopting user-friendly security measures that can go on in the background without interrupting user experience. 2. Adapting to Emerging Fraud TechniquesFraudsters continually evolve their tactics, often staying a step ahead of traditional security measures. This necessitates an ongoing effort from Fintech companies to adapt and upgrade their fraud prevention strategies. This way, they can stay ahead of new fraud techniques. Related: 5 Fraud Trends for Commercial Banks in 2025 ConclusionAs Fintech continues to grow and evolve, the battle against fraud remains a top priority. The industry leverages cutting-edge technologies such as AI, blockchain, and biometric authentication as an advantage. As the battle between fraudsters and security experts continues, staying informed about the latest developments in fraud prevention is essential for anyone involved in the Fintech industry.Moreover, fintech fraud can be detected and prevented using compliance solutions. With the Youverify workflow builder, you can automate your compliance processes in just a few steps. Ready to join the fight against fraud and secure your fintech organisation? Join other 100+ companies who use Youverify. Book a demo to see how it works Related ArticlesAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Priscilla, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 28, 2023What is a Sanctions List?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Emmanuel Agwu•February 14, 2024RegTech's Influence On Regulatory Policy and ReformRead More","title":"The Latest Fraud Prevention Technique in Fintech Industries","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/fintech-fraud-prevention","id":510},{"content_hash":"8b730606702a0a00652c706a583540f49f11b00b48a4d707c7758bcaebe5346b","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:12.300311+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/13/frsc-army-strengthen/","language":"","text":"FRSC, Army Strengthen Partnership on Training, Security By Prnigeria - September 13, 2026 Corps Provost of the FRSC, Headquarters Abuja, Corps Commander Livinus S. Audu and the Provost Marshal (NACMP), Major General Mathias Erebulum at the Headquarters of Nigerian Army Corps of Military Police FRSC, Army Strengthen Partnership on Training, Security The Federal Road Safety Corps (FRSC) has sought stronger collaboration with the Nigerian Army Corps of Military Police (NACMP) in training, security, discipline and conflict management as part of efforts to enhance national safety and security. The Corps Provost of the FRSC, Headquarters Abuja, Corps Commander Livinus S. Audu, made the call on Thursday, September 10, 2026, during a courtesy visit to the Headquarters of the Nigerian Army Corps of Military Police. Audu said the visit was aimed at strengthening the existing relationship between the two organisations and establishing a more strategic partnership to advance their complementary roles in promoting national safety and security. He said the FRSC Provost Office had benefited significantly from military training and professional engagements over the years, particularly in discipline, administration and investigations. According to him, the Corps was interested in expanding the relationship through additional training programmes, seminars and workshops with the Army Military Police to improve the professional competence and institutional capacity of its personnel. Read Also: JUST IN: Army Debunks Abduction of Islamic Scholars at Yari’s Residence in Zamfara 2027: Former Gombe Electoral Commissioner Endorses Pantami for Governor NWDC, Kano Task Force Move to Tackle Drug Abuse, Insecurity in North-West Responding, the Provost Marshal (Army), Major General Mathias Erebulu, welcomed the visit and stressed the importance of inter-agency cooperation, intelligence sharing and sustained professional interaction in addressing Nigeria’s complex security and safety challenges. Erebulu said no single service or organisation could effectively tackle the country’s multifaceted security challenges alone, making collaboration and the sharing of complementary capabilities imperative. He described discipline as the bedrock of every organisation and assured the FRSC of the readiness of the Nigerian Army Corps of Military Police to support relevant capacity-building initiatives, subject to necessary approvals. The Army Provost Marshal also noted that both organisations had complementary responsibilities and could benefit from each other’s expertise in areas including traffic management, security, discipline and emerging operational challenges. The engagement ended with both organisations reaffirming their commitment to deepening inter-agency cooperation, strengthening professional capacity and leveraging their respective capabilities in support of national safety and security. The visit was attended by the Chief of Staff, Headquarters NACMP, principal staff officers of the headquarters and officers of the FRSC. The highlight of the engagement was the presentation of a memento by the visiting FRSC delegation, followed by a group photograph. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGS(FRSC)NACMPPartnership on Training & Security Previous articleNigeria, Benin Customs Move to Modernise Seme-Krake Border for Seamless Trade Prnigeria","title":"FRSC, Army Strengthen Partnership on Training, Security","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/13/frsc-army-strengthen/","id":525},{"content_hash":"0ebc6460f29dcd9c4a05a8736131aeb7a0a98b45e328e141e842ffbd19e8cad1","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:10.464125+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/anti-money-laundering-aml-regulations-in-the-uk","language":"","text":"Anti-Money Laundering (AML) Regulations in the UKByEmmanuel AgwuJune 23, 2023•5mins ReadWhat Are Anti-Money Laundering Regulations?Current Major Components of Anti-Money Laundering Measures In The United KingdomWays To Get Compliant With Anti-money Laundering Requirements In The UKWhat Businesses do AML regulations apply to In The UK?AML Sanctions In The United KingdomAchieving AML Regulations with YouverifyShare this postMoney laundering poses a significant threat to the integrity of the global financial system, undermining trust and facilitating criminal activities. In response, governments around the world have implemented stringent Anti-Money Laundering (AML) regulations to combat this illicit practice. This article focuses on AML regulations specifically in the United Kingdom, exploring their purpose, key components, and the obligations they impose on businesses operating within the country. What Are Anti-Money Laundering Regulations? Anti Money Laundering (AML) entails laws, policies, as well as regulations that prevent financial crimes and illegal activity. Global and local regulators worldwide stringently help prevent financial crimes; they also build policies that help to mitigate financial crimes, especially money laundering. To better learn more about anti-money laundering, read this article. Current Major Components of Anti-Money Laundering Measures In The United Kingdom 1. Legislated Laws In The UK The primary or main legislation that governs anti-money laundering and compliance are the; Money Laundering, Terrorist Financing and Transfer of Funds (Information on the Payer) Regulations 2017; included in these regulations is the EU's Fourth Money Laundering Directive (4MLD) and the Funds Transfer Regulation (FTR). These laws direct the obligations of businesses to prevent money laundering and terrorist financing. 2. Authorities That Enforce Anti-money Laundering Regulations The main regulatory bodies that are responsible for enforcing AML and compliance regulations in the UK include the following: Financial Conduct Authority(FCA)Gambling CommissionInformation Commissioner's Office Each of the regulatory bodies listed above has definite oversight over different sectors, such as banks, casinos and data protection. 3. Customer Due Diligence (CDD) UK firms are required to conduct thorough customer due diligence measures in order to verify the identity of their customers and understand their business activities. This entails performing identity checks, assessing the risk associated with customers and monitoring transactions for suspicious activities. 4. Legal Obligation To Report Suspicious Activity Firms in the United Kingdom are legally obligated to report any suspicious activities that may have an allusion to money laundering or terrorist financing to the National Crime Agency. This allows for a collaborative approach between businesses and law enforcement to easily detect and mitigate financial crimes. 5. Compliance Programs: Commercial entities in Uk, are obligated to establish robust AML and compliance programs that are tailored to their unique industry and risk profile. These programs include internal controls, adequate staff training as well as risk assessment and frequent independent audits to ensure adherence to AML regulations. 6. Penalties and Enforcement Non-compliance with anti-money laundering regulations can result in severe penalties, which include fines and criminal prosecution. Regulatory authorities that enforce compliance have the power to investigate businesses that fail to comply and take appropriate enforcement action. Ways To Get Compliant With Anti-money Laundering Requirements In The UK 1. Reporting Although this can be regarded as a basic measure, it can be effective, especially when done promptly. Business entities are obligated or mandated by the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 to report any kind of suspicious activity that they detect. A Suspicious Activity Report (SAR must be handed over to the National Crime Agency (NCA) by a nominated officer deemed appropriate as soon as the suspicion arises. It can also be done online on the go by accessing this online portal. 2. Refining Organisation Structure To Support Compliance Companies can ensure that organisational structures should be well utilised to combat financial crimes like money laundering. Structures may differ and be innovative. Companies can create a separate Anti-money laundering department or an audit department that is well brief on anti-money laundering regulations and can also detect irregularities in financial transactions. No matter the structure adopted, duties and roles should be clear and well-strategised to actually mitigate risks. Companies are required to appoint a nominated officer. Also, FCA-regulated businesses must select a Money Laundering Officer (MLRO). Both officers are expected to perform distinct but important duties. The MLRO is an employee appointed to oversee a company's compliance with AML regulations and to alert the company when they detect a suspicious activity that could be related to money laundering, while the nominated officer is nominated to receive and consider suspicious activity reports (SARs), they are also obligated to make external suspicious activity to the FCA. Asides from relying on official departments and officials with distinct duties related to AML regulations, companies are also required to monitor their employed staff and provide adequate AML training; employees who are exposed to a higher risk of money laundering should be subject to more stringent vetting processes. 3. Customer Due Diligence Checks Customer due diligence, with the popular CDD, refers to a process necessarily adopted by financial institutions and businesses in order to gather data or information about their clients or customers so that they can identify and mitigate risks related to illicit activities like; identity fraud, money laundering, etcetera. Customer Due Diligence in the UK includes record keeping, enhanced due diligence, continuous monitoring, identity verification, risk assessment, etcetera. 4. Anti-Money Laundering Screening Effective and up-to-date systems should be set up in order to complete regular AML screening; the systems should be appropriate to the nature, size and risk level of the companies. Legal and natural individuals should be checked against multiple watchlists like the UK government's financial sanctions list, trade sanctions list, European Commission's list of high-risk third countries and the Treasury’s list of high-risk countries as well as countries to which enhanced due diligence is required. 5. Ardent Record Keeping UK businesses are required to keep records of all CDD measures implemented, including copies of identification documents, transaction records, risk assessments, and any other pertinent documentation. These records should be retained for a specific amount of time (typically five years) and made available to regulatory authorities upon request. What Businesses do AML regulations apply to In The UK? Here is a summary of the businesses that are mandated to oblige AML regulations: 1. Money Service Businesses MSBs, such as money transmitters, providers of currency exchange, and payment processors, are governed by AML laws. They have to submit an application to the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) and abide by the MLR 2017 specifications. 2. Banks and Financial Institutions Both conventional banks and Internet payment service providers are subject to extensive AML requirements, as are banks, credit unions, building societies, and other financial institutions operating in the UK. 3. Casinos and other Gambling Facilities The UK Gambling Commission controls and monitors the adherence to AML requirements by casinos, betting shops, online gambling platforms, and other gambling facilities. For client due diligence, the reporting of suspicious transactions, and continuous monitoring, there are certain standards. 4. Providers of Trust and Company Services AML requirements apply to those who offer trust and company services, such as formation agents, trust administrators, and corporate service providers. They need to register with the FCA or HM Revenue and Customs (HMRC) and follow the MLR 2017 guidelines. 5. Estate Agents AML requirements apply to real estate agents and developers. When dealing in high-value transactions, such as the sale or purchase of real estate, they have a responsibility to exercise customer due diligence. 6. Accountants and Tax Advisers AML requirements apply to those who work in the accounting and tax advisory fields. When offering services pertaining to tax planning, financial advising, or auditing activities, they must adhere to AML obligations. 7. Lawyers and Legal Professionals AML requirements also apply to barristers, solicitors, and other legal professionals. Due diligence must be done, suspect transactions must be reported, and client confidentiality must be upheld. 8. Dealers in high-value products Such as those that deal in precious metals, fine art, or other high-value items, are subject to AML legislation. They have a responsibility to investigate consumers thoroughly and report any questionable transactions. AML Sanctions In The United Kingdom AML sanctions in the UK play an important role in combating and mitigating money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illegal financial activities. These sanctions are imposed by the government and regulatory authorities to restrict the transfer of funds, freeze assets, and deter individuals or entities engaged in illicit activities. The following AML sanctions are applicable in the UK. 1. United Nations Sanctions The UK is committed to implementing and enforcing United Nations (UN) sanctions. These sanctions are imposed by the UN Security Council and are binding on all member states. They aim to address threats to international peace and security, including terrorism, weapons proliferation, and human rights violations. 2. European Union Sanctions Prior to the UK's withdrawal from the European Union, it implemented and enforced EU sanctions. These sanctions targeted individuals, entities, and countries involved in activities such as terrorism, arms proliferation, and human rights abuses. After Brexit, the UK has adopted its own autonomous sanctions framework. 3. UK Autonomous Sanctions The UK now has the power to impose its own autonomous sanctions independent of the EU framework. This allows the UK to design targeted measures against individuals, entities, or countries involved in activities that pose a threat to national security or international stability. 4. Office of Financial Sanctions Implementation (OFSI) The OFSI, which operates under the UK Treasury, is responsible for implementing and enforcing financial sanctions in the UK. They maintain and update the Consolidated List of individuals, entities, and countries subject to financial sanctions. It is the responsibility of businesses and individuals to check this list and comply with any sanctions in place. 5. Sanctions List Checking UK businesses are required to conduct sanctions list checks as part of their customer due diligence processes. This involves screening customers, clients, and other transaction counterparts against the relevant sanctions lists to ensure they are not involved in sanctioned activities. 6. Penalties and Enforcement Non-compliance with AML sanctions can result in significant penalties, including fines and criminal prosecution. The OFSI has the authority to investigate and take enforcement action against businesses and individuals that breach sanctions regulations. Achieving AML Regulations with Youverify It is important to implement anti-money laundering measures across all kinds of businesses. However, financial entities or institutions will always be held to the highest scrutiny. This is why financial institutions need to have departments dedicated to track and fraud and money laundering; all employees should also be educated on the best AML practices. For small, medium and large-scale businesses, it is important that reliable KYC and AML automation tools are leveraged to avoid sanctions and avoidable losses. See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for KYC and AML screening of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today.Related ArticlesAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Priscilla, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 28, 2023What is a Sanctions List?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Emmanuel Agwu•February 14, 2024RegTech's Influence On Regulatory Policy and ReformRead More","title":"Anti-Money Laundering (AML) Regulations in the UK","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/anti-money-laundering-aml-regulations-in-the-uk","id":532},{"content_hash":"4896062eac4b2679d97fb2a2e496fb8cdb65dfb8a1747c8dbf5c5e945b830d67","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:06.504246+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/how-to-check-your-bvn-on-uba","language":"","text":"How to Check Your BVN on UBA ByEmmanuel AgwuMay 24, 2023•5mins ReadWhy Do You Need To Know And Secure Your BVN?What Are The Prerequisites To Check Your BVN On UBA?Step-by-step Guide To Retrieve Your BVN on UBAWhat Other Alternative Method Can You Use To Retrieve Your BVN From UBA?Bottom LineShare this postThe Bank Verification Number (BVN) is a unique identification number issued by the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) to individuals for banking and financial transactions. It serves as a means of verifying and authenticating customers' identities across different banks. Thus, it plays a significant role in preventing fraud and enhancing security. If you're a UBA (United Bank for Africa) account holder and need to check your BVN, this article will provide you with a detailed step-by-step guide how to check bvn on uba and explain how to check bvn using your mobile device. Why Do You Need To Know And Secure Your BVN? As earlier stated, the Bank Verification Number (BNV) is a unique identification system introduced by the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) to enhance security, promote transparency, and curb fraud in the banking industry. It is a 10-digit biometric identification number that serves as a universal identifier for individuals across all banks operating in Nigeria. For those wondering, the answer is that it comprises 10 digits. The BVN captures an individual’s biometric data such as fingerprints and facial features, as well as personal information like name, date of birth, and correct details. This comprehensive identification system ensures that each bank customer has a distinct and verifiable identity, reducing the risk of identity theft, fraudulent activities, and unauthorized access to banking services. The BVN has become a vital requirement for various banking transactions, including opening new accounts, obtaining loans, conducting large transactions, and verifying customer information. By linking an individual’s financial information to their biometrics, the BVN plays a pivotal role in promoting financial inclusion, streamlining banking operations, and safeguarding the integrity of the Nigerian banking system. What Are The Prerequisites To Check Your BVN On UBA? United Bank for Africa (UBA) is a leading financial institution in Africa, offering a wide range of banking and financial services to individuals, businesses, and governments. With its headquarters in Lagos, Nigeria, UBA has established a strong presence across the continent and has expanded its operations to several countries beyond Africa’s borders. Renowned for its commitment to innovation, customer service, and corporate social responsibility, UBA has earned a reputation as a trusted and progressive bank, driving economic growth and financial inclusion across Africa. The prerequisites for checking your BVN on UBA include: 1. UBA Account: You must have an active account with United Bank for Africa (UBA) to check your BVN. The BVN is linked to your UBA account, so you need to be a UBA customer to access this information. If you're wondering how to check my uba bvn number with my phone, having an active account is the first step. 2. Mobile Phone: You will need a mobile phone with network coverage to send an SMS and receive the response containing your BVN. This is especially useful if you're searching how to get my bvn number on my phone. Ensure that your phone has sufficient battery and is functioning properly. 3. SMS Service: Make sure your mobile phone has SMS (Short Message Service) capabilities enabled. Check with your mobile service provider if you are unsure or facing issues with sending or receiving SMS messages. 4. Sufficient Balance or SMS Plan: Ensure that you have enough balance on your mobile phone or an active SMS plan that covers the cost of sending the SMS message to retrieve your BVN. Standard SMS charges may apply, so make sure you are aware of any associated costs. It's important to note that the BVN is specific to individual banks, and while this guide focuses on checking BVN on UBA, it may vary for other banks when using bvn codes for all banks. Therefore, having an account with UBA is a key prerequisite for checking your BVN through the UBA platform. Check out our article here to know what to do if you are banking with another financial institution apart from UBA. Step-by-step Guide To Retrieve Your BVN on UBA Here is a detailed step-by-step approach for how to check BVN on UBA: Step 1: Prepare Your Mobile Phone: Ensure that your mobile phone is charged and has a working SMS application. You'll be using this to send an SMS to UBA's shortcode. Also, confirm that there is network coverage on your phone. Step 2: Compose a New SMS: Open your SMS application and create a new message. You can usually find this option in your phone’s main menu or on the home screen. Step 3: Type the BVN request Message: In the message body, type \"BVN\" followed by a space. Then, enter your 11-digit UBA account number. Make sure to input the account number accurately to receive the correct BVN. it is essential to enter the correct keyword to initiate the BVN retrieval process. As such, double-check the account number to ensure accuracy, as an incorrect account number may lead to an unsuccessful BVN retrieval. Step 4: Send the SMS: Once you have entered your UBA account number in the message, send the SMS to the shortcode \"20123\". This shortcode is specifically designated for BVN inquiries and will ensure that your message reaches the appropriate system for processing. Take note that standard SMS charges may apply, so ensure you have a sufficient balance or an appropriate SMS plan. This shortcode acts as the code for bvn for BVN retrieval. If you're using the MTN network with your UBA account, the process remains the same, similar to how to check bvn on uba mtn. Step 5: Wait for the Response: After sending the SMS, patiently wait for a response. UBA’s system will process your request and send an SMS back to your mobile phone with your BVN. This response typically arrives within a few minutes, but it may take longer during periods of high network activity. In case of any delay, ensure that you have a stable network connection. Step 6: Review and save Your BVN When you receive the response message containing your BVN, carefully review the numbers to ensure their accuracy. The BVN is an essential piece of information, so it is crucial to keep it confidential and secure. Consider saving the BVN in a secure location or storing it digitally for easy reference when needed. Step 7: Safeguard Your BVN: Once you have received your BVN, it is vital to treat this information as confidential. Store it securely and avoid sharing it with unauthorized individuals or responding to unsolicited requests for your BVN. What Other Alternative Method Can You Use To Retrieve Your BVN From UBA? The answer is simple. Visit a UBA Branch. If you prefer a more personal approach or encounter any difficulties with the SMS method, you can visit any UBA branch. At the branch, approach a bank representative and request assistance in checking your BVN. They will guide you through the necessary steps and verify your account details and identity to provide you with your BVN. Pease note, if you are curious about how to check my bvn online, UBA currently does not offer an online BVN retrieval option. Read here for more tips on alternative ways of checking your BVN. Bottom Line Checking your BVN on UBA is a straightforward process that can be accomplished through an SMS or by visiting a UBA branch. Your BVN is an essential piece of information that serves as a unique identifier, promoting secure and authentic banking transactions. By following the step-by-step guide outlined in this article, you can easily access your BVN and ensure the verification of your identity for seamless banking experiences with UBA. Remember to keep your BVN confidential and be cautious of any suspicious requests for this sensitive information. See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for BVN verification of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today. Related ArticlesView All PostsIdentity DocumentsEmmanuel Agwu•May 26, 2023How to Perform Driver's License Verification OnlineRead MoreIdentity DocumentsEmmanuel Agwu•June 6, 2023How To Renew Your Driver's License In South AfricaRead MoreIdentity DocumentsPrince, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•March 15, 2023What are the Acceptable Employment Verification Documents? Read More","title":"How to Check Your BVN on UBA","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/how-to-check-your-bvn-on-uba","id":551},{"content_hash":"16055196389d2bbfb8d28ba67350e940353276b5311f0003298e61a16a583fc6","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:03.643460+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/binance-charged-by-cftc-with-accusations-of-breaking-us-financial-laws","language":"","text":"BREAKING: Binance Charged by CFTC with Accusations of Breaking US Financial LawsByEmmanuel AgwuMarch 28, 2023•5mins ReadBinance Compliance ProblemsShare this postThe Commodities and Futures Trading Commission (CFTC) announced that it has filed a civil enforcement action suit against Binance, the crypto exchange platform, its CEO Changpeng Zhao and three other entities that operate the platform. Filed at the federal high court in Chicago, the suit claims that the entities violated numerous Commodity Exchange Act (CEA) and CFTC regulations. The entities involved are Binance Holdings Limited, Binance Holdings (IE) Limited, and Binance (Services) Holdings Limited (together, Binance). Information from the lawsuit states that Binance has solicited and accepted orders and operated a centralized digital asset trading platform alongside several other corporate vehicles through a willfully opaque common enterprise. The complaint also charges Samuel Lim, Binance’s former chief compliance officer with “willful and substantial assistance” of these violations. With respect to the violations, the agency seeks: Civil monetary penaltiesDisgorgementPermanent trading and registration bansA permanent injunction against further violations of the CFTC and CEA regulations as charged. Commenting on the situation, CFTC chairman Rostin Behnam said: “Today’s enforcement action demonstrates that there is no location, or claimed lack of location, that will prevent the CFTC from protecting American investors. I have been clear that the CFTC will continue to use all of its authority to find and stop misconduct in the volatile and risky digital asset market,” “For years, Binance knew they were violating CFTC rules, working actively to both keep the money flowing and avoid compliance. This should be a warning to anyone in the digital asset world that the CFTC will not tolerate willful avoidance of U.S. law. I applaud the diligent and dedicated work of the CFTC’s Enforcement team in bringing this action, and for their hard work in addressing illegal operations in the digital asset space.” he added Shortly after the lawsuit became public, Binance's CEO tweeted the number \"4\" over from his account, which refers to an earlier goal for 2023 he has pinned to the top of his profile, \"Ignore FUD, fake news, attacks, etc.\" Zhao has rejected the allegations against him. A spokeswoman for Binance mentioned that the lawsuit was;\"unexpected and disappointing,\" stating that Binance has worked \"collaboratively with the CFTC for more than two years.\" She said it has made significant investments to ensure \"we do not have U.S. users active on our platform,\" raising its compliance team to 750 from 100 and spending $80 million to bolster its compliance programs. Gretchen Lowe, CFTC’s Enforcement Division Principal Deputy Director and Chief Counsel added: “Defendants’ alleged willful evasion of U.S. law is at the core of the Commission’s complaint against Binance. The defendants’ own emails and chats reflect that Binance’s compliance efforts have been a sham and Binance deliberately chose – over and over – to place profits over following the law,” “Today’s enforcement action reflects that the CFTC and its Enforcement Division will pursue those digital asset platforms and individuals who flout and actively attempt to circumvent CFTC regulatory requirements. I thank the Enforcement team for their dedication and hard work in bringing this action.” Binance Compliance Problems In the complaint, Binance offered and executed commodity derivatives transactions to and for persons in the US between July 2019 and the present. It also claims that Binance’s compliance program has been ineffective and the company instructed employees and customers to circumvent compliance controls at Zhao’s direction to maximize corporate profits. The complaint charges that for much of the relevant period, Binance did not require its customers to provide any identity-verifying information before trading on the platform, despite the legal duty that entities like Binance functioning as futures commission merchants (FCMs) collect such information, and failed to implement basic compliance procedures designed to prevent and detect terrorist financing and money laundering. According to the complaint: Binance's activities as a futures commission merchant (FCM), failed to collect identity-verifying information from its customers despite legal requirements to do so and did not implement basic compliance procedures to prevent terrorist financing and money laundering. Even after restricting U.S. customers from trading on the platform, Binance allegedly provided instructions to its U.S.-based VIP customers on evading compliance controls. Additionally, the complaint charges Binance with facilitating derivatives transactions without registering with the CFTC, and the entity defendants with failing to properly supervise Binance's activities. One example cited in the complaint is Binance's use of a messaging app with an auto-delete feature to instruct employees on evading controls, apparently to avoid leaving evidence of efforts to retain U.S.-based customers. The complaint also accuses Binance, Zhao, and Lim of intentionally structuring their entities and transactions to evade registration requirements and instructing U.S. customers as well as other customers as to how to evade Binance’s compliance controls in a bid to avoid CFTC regulations.Related ArticlesView All PostsIndustry NewsEmmanuel Agwu•April 19, 2022Looming: Failure to Pass Pending Anti-Money Laundering (AML) & Terrorism Prevention Bills Could See Nigeria Globally BlacklistedRead MoreIndustry NewsEmmanuel Agwu•October 14, 2022Crypto Exchange, Bittrex, Fined $29 Million by the U.S Treasury Department for AML ViolationsRead MoreIndustry NewsEmmanuel Agwu•March 14, 2023Official: Youverify to Launch in South Africa at the MFSA 2023 Event, Bringing Global Compliance Services to LocalsRead More","title":"BREAKING: Binance Charged by CFTC with Accusations of Breaking US Financial Laws","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/binance-charged-by-cftc-with-accusations-of-breaking-us-financial-laws","id":568},{"content_hash":"76f6d873b3bb04fccc27f405dfae84831545090dc253a7695d801ea51f22aae1","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:00.767014+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/20/matawalle-urges-african/","language":"","text":"Matawalle Urges African Defence Chiefs to Unite Against Terrorism, Emerging Security Threats By Prnigeria - September 20, 2026 Minister of State for Defence, Bello Muhammed Matawalle, and some Defence Chiefs in Burundi Matawalle Urges African Defence Chiefs to Unite Against Terrorism, Emerging Security Threats The Minister of State for Defence, Bello Muhammed Matawalle, has called for enhanced continental cooperation, intelligence sharing and interoperability among African armed forces to tackle terrorism, transnational organised crime and emerging security threats. Matawalle made the call while delivering Nigeria’s address at the 18th Ordinary Session of the Specialised Technical Committee on Defence, Safety and Security (STCDSS) and the 21st Ordinary Meeting of African Chiefs of Defence Staff and Heads of Safety and Security in Bujumbura, Burundi. Addressing defence ministers, the African Union Commissioner for Political Affairs, Peace and Security, military chiefs and members of the diplomatic corps, the minister said the STCDSS had become increasingly important amid the complex and interconnected security challenges confronting the continent. He identified terrorism, violent extremism, transnational organised crime, human and arms trafficking, piracy, cyber threats, unconstitutional changes of government and communal conflicts as threats that required collective action. “These threats are not confined by national borders. Consequently, no single country can effectively address them in isolation,” Matawalle stated. He urged stronger collaboration among the African Union, Regional Economic Communities and member states, stressing the need for improved intelligence sharing, joint and multinational operations, and sustained political commitment to the African Peace and Security Architecture (APSA) and the African Standby Force (ASF). According to him, the successes recorded through joint operations demonstrate that coordinated action and intelligence sharing can significantly degrade terrorist and criminal networks. Read Also: Zakari Mohammed: Kwara Must Rise to Full Potential as ADC Candidate Demands Public Debate WSCIJ Hails PRNigeria for Sustaining Young Communication Fellowship Beyond Grant Funding The People Who Made My PRNigeria Fellowship Memorable By Adegbite Anuoluwapo Rafat Matawalle also highlighted the evolving security risks associated with technological advancements, including the deployment of drones, artificial intelligence and cyber capabilities for recruitment, radicalisation, financial crimes and other illicit activities. “Africa must invest in technological innovation, research and development, intelligence capabilities and human capital, while ensuring that technology is employed responsibly and in accordance with international law,” he said. The minister, however, emphasised that military interventions alone could not resolve the continent’s security challenges without addressing their underlying socioeconomic and governance-related causes. He identified poverty, unemployment, weak institutions, inadequate access to education and social services, climate pressures and limited economic opportunities as factors that must be tackled alongside military and security operations. “Our approach to security must therefore be comprehensive. Military and security responses must be complemented by effective governance, youth empowerment, education, economic development, strengthened justice systems and social inclusion,” he added. Reaffirming Nigeria’s commitment to the principle of African solutions to African problems, Matawalle said the country would continue contributing to regional and continental peace support operations, counter-terrorism initiatives and capacity-building programmes. He commended the leadership of the African Union and the STCDSS for their contributions to peace, security and stability across the continent, while wishing delegates fruitful deliberations. The meeting brought together defence ministers, chiefs of defence staff and heads of safety and security from AU member states to deliberate on practical and sustainable responses to Africa’s evolving security challenges. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSAfrican Defence ChiefsBello Muhammed MatawalleMinister DefenceTerrorism Previous articleZakari Mohammed: Kwara Must Rise to Full Potential as ADC Candidate Demands Public Debate Prnigeria","title":"Matawalle Urges African Defence Chiefs to Unite Against Terrorism, Emerging Security Threats","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/20/matawalle-urges-african/","id":582},{"content_hash":"6ef2c56f0feb80499b9e272633ad318ca932d8fc40ad01cf469034e5b03636e2","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:59.505169+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/importance-of-kyc-remediation","language":"","text":"The Importance of KYC RemediationByPrince, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 27, 2023•5mins ReadUnderstanding KYC Remediation and its SignificanceKYC Remediation Process: Steps and Best PracticesBenefits of KYC Remediation for BusinessesKYC Remediation and Legal ComplianceKYC Refresh vs. Remediation: What’s the Difference?Data Remediation Definition: Why Data Quality is Crucial for KYC RemediationAchieving KYC Remediation with YouverifyFinal Thoughts: Staying KYC Compliant is Key to Business SuccessShare this postAs a business owner, you are probably aware of KYC compliance and its importance. KYC, or Know Your Customer, is a process used by businesses to verify the identity of their customers to detect and prevent potential fraudulent activities. However, simply being KYC-compliant is not enough. KYC remediation is also crucial to ensure that your business stays compliant with the latest regulations and best practices. In this article, we will discuss the importance of KYC remediation, its process, benefits, legal compliance, and the difference between KYC refresh and remediation. We will also provide tips on how to stay KYC compliant and how KYC remediation services can help your business. Understanding KYC Remediation and its Significance KYC remediation is the procedure of reviewing and bringing up-to-date customer information and documentation to guarantee compliance with the latest regulations and best practices. Some of the important points to note about KYC remediation include: It helps businesses identify and verify the customer’s identity, assess the risk associated with the customer, and document the findings. It helps you to identify and prevent potential fraudulent activities, such as money laundering, terrorist financing, and identity theft.KYC remediation is significant because it helps your business to maintain its reputation and credibility. KYC remediation also helps you to avoid legal and financial consequences associated with non-compliance. It can result in fines, legal actions, loss of reputation, and even loss of business.KYC Remediation Process: Steps and Best Practices KYC remediation involves several steps and best practices to ensure its effectiveness. The following are the common steps involved in the KYC remediation process: 1. Identify the customers who require remediation This involves reviewing your customer database and identifying the customers whose information and documentation require updating. 2. Collect the necessary information and documentation This involves requesting the necessary information and documentation from the customers, such as identity documents, address proof, and business information. 3. Verify the information and documentation This involves verifying the authenticity of the information and documentation provided by the customers, such as conducting background checks and verifying the identity of the customers. 4. Assess the risk associated with the customers This involves assessing the risk associated with the customers based on their location, business type, and other factors. 5. Document the findings This involves documenting the findings of the remediation process, such as updating the customer database and creating a remediation report. The following are some best practices to ensure the effectiveness of the KYC remediation process: 1. Define a clear KYC remediation policy and procedure This involves defining a clear policy and procedure for KYC remediation that outlines the roles and responsibilities of the stakeholders involved in the process. 2. Use technology to automate the KYC remediation process This involves using technology to automate the KYC remediation process, such as using software to verify the authenticity of the information and documentation provided by the customers. 3. Train your employees on KYC remediation This involves training your employees on KYC remediation to ensure that they understand the importance of the process, the best practices, and the latest regulations. Benefits of KYC Remediation for Businesses KYC remediation offers several benefits for businesses, such as: 1. Compliance with the latest regulations and best practices KYC remediation helps businesses to stay compliant with the latest regulations and best practices, which can help them to avoid legal and financial consequences associated with non-compliance. 2. Prevention of financial crimes KYC remediation helps businesses to prevent financial crimes, such as money laundering, terrorist financing, and identity theft, which can damage their reputation and credibility. 3. Improved customer experience KYC remediation helps businesses to improve the customer experience by ensuring that their information is secure and up-to-date. 4. Increased operational efficiency KYC remediation helps businesses to increase operational efficiency by automating the KYC remediation process and reducing the manual effort required. KYC Remediation and Legal Compliance Legal compliance is a crucial aspect of KYC remediation. Businesses are required to comply with the latest regulations and best practices to avoid legal and financial consequences. The following are the legal consequences of non-compliance with KYC regulations: 1. Fines: Businesses that fail to comply with KYC regulations can face fines imposed by regulatory authorities. 2. Legal actions: Businesses that fail to comply with KYC regulations can face legal actions, such as lawsuits and criminal charges. 3. Loss of reputation: Non-compliance with KYC regulations can damage the reputation and credibility of businesses, which can result in a loss of customers and revenue. KYC Refresh vs. Remediation: What’s the Difference? KYC refresh and remediation are often used interchangeably, but they refer to different processes. KYC refresh involves updating the customer information and documentation periodically, such as annually or bi-annually. It is a proactive approach to ensure that customer information is up-to-date and compliant with the latest regulations and best practices. KYC remediation, on the other hand, involves reviewing and updating the customer information and documentation when there is a trigger event, such as a change in the customer’s profile or a change in the regulatory requirements. It is a reactive approach to ensure that the customer information is compliant with the latest regulations and best practices. Data Remediation Definition: Why Data Quality is Crucial for KYC Remediation Data remediation is the process of identifying and resolving data quality issues to ensure that the data is accurate, complete, and consistent. Data quality is crucial for KYC remediation because it ensures that the information and documentation provided by the customers are authentic and reliable. Poor data quality can result in inaccurate customer information, which can lead to non-compliance and potentially fraudulent activities. Achieving KYC Remediation with Youverify KYC remediation services can help your business to stay compliant with the latest regulations and best practices. Youverify offers the following KYC remediation services: 1. Expertise: Youverify has the expertise and experience to ensure that your business stays compliant with the latest regulations and best practices. 2. Technology: Youverify uses technology to automate the KYC remediation process, which can reduce the manual effort required and increase operational efficiency. 3. Flexibility: Youverify's KYC remediation services offer flexible solutions that can be customized to meet the specific needs of your business. 4. Cost-effective: Youverify's KYC remediation services can be cost-effective compared to hiring an in-house team to perform the remediation process. Final Thoughts: Staying KYC Compliant is Key to Business Success In conclusion, KYC remediation is crucial to ensure that your business stays compliant with the latest regulations and best practices. It helps you to prevent potential fraudulent activities, maintain your reputation and credibility, and avoid legal and financial consequences associated with non-compliance. KYC remediation involves several steps and best practices, such as identifying the customers who require remediation, collecting and verifying the necessary information and documentation, and assessing the risk associated with the customers. Businesses can benefit from KYC remediation by staying compliant with the latest regulations and best practices, preventing financial crimes, improving the customer experience, and increasing operational efficiency. Compliance tips for businesses include defining a clear KYC policy and procedure, using technology to automate the KYC process, training your employees on KYC compliance, and conducting regular audits to ensure compliance. Remember, staying KYC compliant is key to business success! Speak to an expert at Youverify for more information on KYC remediation services and how we can help your business stay compliant. Request a demo today to get started. Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"The Importance of KYC Remediation","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/importance-of-kyc-remediation","id":587},{"content_hash":"d2e41ff959e5e66cb6c9504d5940078e47724856a51b6ea25c6eaf9394b95aa8","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:57.744771+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/20/oyo-osun-customs-intercepts/","language":"","text":"Oyo/Osun Customs Intercepts Arms, Explosives, Cannabis in N4.1bn Smuggling Bust By Prnigeria - September 20, 2026 Oyo/Osun Customs Intercepts Arms, Explosives, Cannabis in N4.1bn Smuggling Bust ccccccc The Acting Customs Area Controller of the Command, Deputy Comptroller Wale Adewole, disclosed this on Thursday, September 17, 2026, during a press briefing at the Command’s Operational Headquarters in Ibadan. Adewole said the seizures formed part of the Command’s sustained anti-smuggling operations aimed at protecting national security, public safety and the nation’s economy. Other security-sensitive items intercepted included 573 Nigeria Police Force crests and 92 yards of police camouflage uniform fabric, raising concerns over the potential use of such materials by criminal elements to impersonate law enforcement personnel. “We will continue to pay particular attention to items that have implications for national security and public safety, while ensuring that legitimate businesses and law-abiding members of the public are not unduly impeded,” Adewole said. Read Also: Zakari Mohammed: Kwara Must Rise to Full Potential as ADC Candidate Demands Public Debate WSCIJ Hails PRNigeria for Sustaining Young Communication Fellowship Beyond Grant Funding The People Who Made My PRNigeria Fellowship Memorable By Adegbite Anuoluwapo Rafat The Command also seized 2,012 bags of 50kg foreign parboiled rice, 550 bags of 25kg foreign parboiled rice, 2,440 wraps of Cannabis sativa, 1,150 used tyres, 17 used vehicles and 450 kegs of Premium Motor Spirit, equivalent to 5,000 litres. Additional seizures comprised 10 containers of corrosive mercury, 105 bales and 28 sacks of used clothing, as well as 48 packs of Tramadol. The Acting Controller stressed the importance of intelligence-driven enforcement and collaboration with sister security agencies in curbing smuggling and preventing the movement of illicit goods across the country. Meanwhile, the Oyo/Osun Area Command generated N65.66 billion in revenue between January and August 2026, representing a 13.88 per cent increase over the N57.66 billion collected during the corresponding period in 2025. Adewole attributed the revenue growth to improved compliance, enhanced stakeholder engagement and trade facilitation initiatives. He reaffirmed the Command’s commitment to sustaining the fight against smuggling, protecting national security and creating an enabling environment for legitimate trade. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSCannabisIntercepts Arms & ExplosivesN4.1bn Smuggling BustNigeria Customs Service Previous articleMatawalle Urges African Defence Chiefs to Unite Against Terrorism, Emerging Security Threats Prnigeria","title":"Oyo/Osun Customs Intercepts Arms, Explosives, Cannabis in N4.1bn Smuggling Bust","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/20/oyo-osun-customs-intercepts/","id":595},{"content_hash":"79bc088ff2bb9b6937a2e6dcf2283786c090c205a7ba589e54d3c28855994aef","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:55.443745+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-pkyc-perpetual-know-your-customer","language":"","text":"What is Perpetual KYC? All About Perpetual Customer Due DiligenceByPriscilla Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 22, 2023•5mins ReadUnderstanding Perpetual KYC (pKYC)What is pKYC?What are the Pillars of pKYC?What are the Benefits or Advantages Of pKYC?Final ThoughtsShare this postPerpetual KYC (more popularly referred to as pKYC) is a child of necessity that was birthed in response to one of the growing concerns that stem from the use of traditional Know Your Customer (KYC) processes. The key components of KYC are customer verification and authentication, customer due diligence and ongoing transaction monitoring. The advent of pKYC was necessitated by the lacuna discovered in conventional KYC. pKYC is therefore an evolution of traditional KYC risk monitoring. Just like KYC, pKYC exists to prevent money laundering and other financial crimes. Understanding Perpetual KYC (pKYC) As it becomes easier day by day to carry out financial transactions over the internet due to advancements in technology, fraudulent financial activities (such as money laundering and terrorist financing) have also been on the rise. This has made it quite necessary for businesses in the regulated industries (especially banks and other financial institutions) to preempt and prevent fraudulent customers who are constantly devising new means of speedily carrying out illegal financial activities without being caught. Aside from the risk of reputational damage to these financial institutions, they also have to abide by the requirement to keep up to date and be compliant with new laws and regulations which are constantly being churned out by their regulatory authorities. Failure to do so would result in fines and penalties which will undoubtedly result in a loss of business profit and revenue. In conventional KYC, risk monitoring is usually conducted at certain fixed periodic cycles or timeframes. Sometimes, between one risk assessment review and another, a lot of damage has been done through fraudulent financial activities. It is for this problem that the evolution from conventional KYC to pKYC became highly expedient. The fast pace of change in the regulatory compliance landscape makes it hard for compliance teams to keep using periodic automated monitoring. Read Also: Anti-Money Laundering (AML) Fines of 2021 - Key Takeaways & Insights to see instances where businesses in regulated industries have incurred financial losses in the form of fines due to noncompliance with regulatory requirements. What is pKYC? pKYC refers to the ongoing process through which financial organisations constantly gather and update customer information in real-time with the aim of using it for risk management. With perpetual KYC, businesses can foresee potential customer risks before they can cause any damage and either opt to cut ties or contain the impact depending on the situation. What are the Pillars of pKYC? One element that lies at the very heart of pKYC is fluctuating risk levels. The risk profile of a customer can take a radical shift at the twinkle of an eye, requiring the prompt attention of the Compliance team and some immediate preventive action. Between one scheduled periodic review of the customer risk profile and another, a customer that was considered a low risk at the point of onboarding could metamorphose into a high-risk customer due to some suspicious transactions or political exposure. Hence the need for perpetual KYC (pKYC). Some of the key components of perpetual KYC include: 1. Precise Customer Profile To effectively conduct pKYC, financial institutions require huge amounts of data. By handling such volumes of data and using them to provide structure and context to the KYC process, financial institutions can build and maintain precise customer profiles for all their customers. With such robust and precise customer profiles in place, it becomes easier to zoom in on and monitor any customer in real-time or near real-time when there is a prompt or trigger of suspicious financial activity. 2. Initiation of Prompt Reviews And Investigations Prompt risk assessment reviews and investigations are vital aspects of pKYC. Instead of the Compliance team spreading itself thin due to lots of work backlog during periodic risk assessments, pKYC enables the initiation of prompt risk investigations and reviews at any time whatsoever, not having to wait for the next cycle of review. This makes financial institutions fully armed and ready for compliance functions in a way that greatly limits risk and prevents fraudulent financial activities. 3. Updated Risk Assessment With pKYC, as soon as there is an event or transaction that triggers a risk assessment or investigation, a review is conducted immediately. This makes it possible for financial institutions to have updated risk assessments for customers as often as necessary, instead of the periodic risk assessments under traditional KYC which often provide obsolete results that end up being remedial rather than preventive. Recommended - What is Periodic KYC Refresh? What are the Benefits or Advantages Of pKYC? Some of the advantages of pKYC include a. It gives a holistic view of a customer's risk profile over time Due to continuous monitoring over the lifespan of the business relationship, a wider and more complete picture of each customer's risk profile is available at all times and not only when periodic risk assessments are conducted. This provides compliance teams with sufficient information to take swift actions and make emergency decisions where some fraudulent financial activities seem to be brewing. b. Prompt and timely assessments of customer risk Under the conventional KYC regime, the risk profiles of customers are assessed and determined first at the point of onboarding. The next time such an assessment might be conducted could be after 2 or 3 years. But with pKYC, risk assessment reviews for a customer are conducted as often as necessary, without waiting for the next scheduled period of risk assessment. c. Flexibility in operation PKYC allows for a shift from manual periodic risk assessment reviews to an event-triggered review option. d. Automatic review of data from multiple sources PKYC relies heavily on data from multiple sources such as government watchlists, sanctions and blacklists. With access to these data sources that are frequently updated, financial institutions can automatically review and analyse information about any customer at any given time. This automatic review makes it easier to assess and update risk profiles. Final Thoughts It has become clear that conventional KYC processes cannot keep up with the demands of fluctuating risk levels and the constantly evolving and speedy methods of financial crimes being deployed. It is therefore through pKYC and its preemptive features that regulatory compliance teams and business organisations can keep pace with the demands in the compliance landscape in our world of super-fast technological change and billions of online financial transactions. Join 100+ leading companies that use Youverify to conduct their KYC exercises and to perform compliance functions and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today.Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"What is Perpetual KYC? All About Perpetual Customer Due Diligence","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-pkyc-perpetual-know-your-customer","id":604},{"content_hash":"d68f8aaaf6e0aa1d54bc161f5d1f2405a85383abdd2f8e04de3d21c82edb5da9","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:53.685295+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/20/fct-police-arrest-major-raid/","language":"","text":"FCT Police Arrest 300 in Major Raid on Crime Flashpoints, Recover Knives, Toy Gun, Cannabis By Prnigeria - September 20, 2026 FCT Police Arrests 300 Criminals FCT Police Arrest 300 in Major Raid on Crime Flashpoints, Recover Knives, Toy Gun, Cannabis The Federal Capital Territory (FCT) Police Command has arrested approximately 300 persons during a coordinated raid on identified crime flashpoints, shanties and uncompleted buildings across the Territory. The operation, conducted on Saturday, September 19, 2026, also led to the recovery of knives, a toy gun, cannabis and other illicit drugs from suspected criminal hideouts. The Command’s Police Public Relations Officer, SP Josephine Adeh, disclosed this in a statement issued on Saturday, adding that the operation was part of ongoing efforts to prevent crime, disrupt criminal activities and enhance the safety and security of residents. According to her, the arrested persons are undergoing thorough screening and profiling to establish their identities and determine possible involvement in criminal activities. She said those found to have committed offences would be charged to court in accordance with the law, while persons with no case to answer would be released after due screening. Read Also: Zakari Mohammed: Kwara Must Rise to Full Potential as ADC Candidate Demands Public Debate WSCIJ Hails PRNigeria for Sustaining Young Communication Fellowship Beyond Grant Funding The People Who Made My PRNigeria Fellowship Memorable By Adegbite Anuoluwapo Rafat The Commissioner of Police, FCT Command, CP Ahmed Muhammed Sanusi, reiterated the Command’s commitment to sustained intelligence-led raids and proactive policing operations, particularly at locations identified as potential hideouts for criminals. Sanusi also cautioned youths and other residents against the use and abuse of illicit drugs, warning that substance abuse could impair judgment, fuel violent and criminal behaviour, and expose individuals and communities to serious security risks. He urged persons struggling with drug use to seek appropriate counselling and rehabilitation support rather than allow substance abuse to lead them into criminal activities. The commissioner further directed police officers participating in such operations to maintain professionalism and operate strictly within the law. He warned officers against using raids and arrests as avenues for extortion, intimidation, harassment or unlawful demands for money, stressing that personnel found engaging in such misconduct would face appropriate disciplinary action. The Command called on residents to continue providing credible and timely information on suspicious activities and criminal hideouts within their communities. Members of the public requiring emergency assistance or wishing to provide security-related information can contact the FCT Police Command on 08032003913. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSArrest 300 in Major RaidCannabisFCT Police CommandRecover KnivesToy Gun Previous articleOyo/Osun Customs Intercepts Arms, Explosives, Cannabis in N4.1bn Smuggling Bust Prnigeria","title":"FCT Police Arrest 300 in Major Raid on Crime Flashpoints, Recover Knives, Toy Gun, Cannabis","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/20/fct-police-arrest-major-raid/","id":611},{"content_hash":"4aae8725617fac17a42ccec7b4cc736af24e48b2e14746e3841a5ec7a0f9516c","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:52.091267+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/kyc-in-crypto","language":"","text":"KYC in Crypto - Why does it Really Matter? ByPriscilla Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 21, 2023•5mins ReadWhat Are Cryptocurrency Exchanges?Is KYC Necessary In Crypto?What Are Some Benefits of KYC In Crypto?What Are The Challenges Of KYC In Crypto?How Does KYC Work In Crypto?ConclusionShare this postCryptocurrency, often referred to simply as crypto is a digital currency (as opposed to physical conventional money) that uses cryptography to secure transactions. There usually exists a central issuing and regulating authority for all physical fiat currencies, e.g. the Federal Reserves for the American Dollar. Cryptocurrency however has no such central issuing or regulating authority. It uses a decentralized system for the issuing of new units and the recording of transactions. With crypto, encryption is used to verify transactions. Since crypto is an asset that can be exchanged for conventional money, it is highly important to know the persons involved in crypto transactions. This is where cryptocurrency exchanges are saddled with the responsibility of conducting Know Your Customer (KYC) exercises for persons who make use of their platforms. KYC entails verifying the identity of customers, conducting some background checks on them and continuously monitoring their transactions to ensure that they are not involved in financial crimes such as money laundering, terrorist financing or trafficking in persons. Conducting KYC protects businesses and limits their exposure to criminal liability when a customer uses their platform for illegal activities. What Are Cryptocurrency Exchanges? A cryptocurrency exchange (sometimes known as Digital Currency Exchange) is an online platform where buyers and sellers of crypto meet to trade crypto. A cryptocurrency exchange is like a marketplace for the trading of crypto. Cryptocurrency exchanges provide an online platform and serve as intermediaries between the buyers and sellers of crypto on the platform provided. Is KYC Necessary In Crypto? Billions of dollars worth of crypto has been traded by millions of persons across the globe since the inception of cryptocurrency. By its nature of providing anonymity for traders, crypto is vulnerable to fraud and can be easily used to illegally move money around and commit serious financial crimes without leaving obvious digital footprints behind. This defeats the goal of Anti-Money Laundering (AML) laws and regulations. It is because of this that governments of different countries and their regulatory agencies have ensured that cryptocurrency exchanges adopt and conduct KYC processes for their customers. In fact, since cryptocurrency exchanges have been defined and categorized as Money Service Businesses (MSBs) by federal regulations, KYC has become mandatory for most crypto exchanges. The large volumes of financial transactions they process have made regulatory agencies put in place rules and regulations to guide the operations of cryptocurrency exchanges. What Are Some Benefits of KYC In Crypto? Some of the benefits of KYC in crypto include: 1. It Helps To Detect And Track Criminal Activities Anonymity is one privilege that crypto traders enjoy a lot. Cryptocurrency provides privacy for all the transactions of its traders. Persons have hidden under the cover of anonymity and privacy in crypto to perpetrate financial crimes. Without KYC in crypto, these financial crimes will not be easy to detect, track and report to the relevant authorities. If such should continue, the crypto industry will be marred by a lot of fraudulent financial activities. 2. It Paints The Cryptocurrency Industry In A Better Light People have had serious reservations about crypto, especially in its early days. One of the major concerns of people was the fact that cryptocurrency exchanges did not have sufficient information about the people that trade on their platforms, hence making the platforms prone to scams and hacks with little or no means of identifying culprits. But with the adoption of KYC in crypto, people have more confidence in the crypto industry, knowing that cryptocurrency exchanges now conduct KYC processes before allowing customers to trade on their platforms. This boosts transparency, builds user trust and paints the crypto industry in a better light. 3. Cryptocurrency Exchanges Are Held To A Higher Standard Of Compliance By ensuring that crypto exchanges comply with KYC requirements, some level of compliance and safety standards are placed on them. This makes cryptocurrency exchanges work towards living up to such expectations to avoid any penalties that may arise due to violation. Without KYC in crypto, cryptocurrency exchanges would be held to almost no standard of compliance at all. 4. Reduced Legal Risk For Cryptocurrency Exchanges Demonstrating their due diligence in KYC and AML practices can help crypto exchanges mitigate the risk of facing legal disputes or regulatory fines. Even when attempts at fraudulent activities have been made on their platforms, their exposure to criminal liability is greatly reduced, as long as they are compliant with KYC and AML regulations. This allows crypto companies to prioritize improving their conversion rates, making transactions more efficient, and adhering to evolving global regulations instead of spending time and resources on court cases, regulatory fines and penalties. 5. Provides More Protection To Crypto Traders The deployment of KYC in crypto has greatly reduced the potential for money laundering and other fraudulent financial activities capable of plaguing cryptocurrency exchanges. The introduction of KYC in crypto has therefore guaranteed more protection for traders on KYC-compliant cryptocurrency exchanges. In the event of any hacks that lead to loss of money, the exchanges can easily track persons involved based on the information and details obtained during KYC processes. What Are The Challenges Of KYC In Crypto? As beneficial as the deployment of KYC in crypto is, cryptocurrency exchanges are faced with certain challenges in the course of implementing KYC processes. Some of these challenges include: 1. Tendency of Violating Data Privacy Laws And Regulations As crypto exchanges collect and verify the information of their customers during onboarding, they need to be extremely careful in how they store, handle and process such information (especially Personally Identifiable Information) so as not to breach or violate existing data protection laws and regulations. 2. Reduced Anonymity In Cryptocurrency A lot of cryptocurrency traders have expressed their unease at the introduction of KYC in crypto. Most users believe the implementation of KYC in crypto has stripped the digital currency of its major feature of anonymity, which was what made it attractive to several users. These concerns have made some crypto enthusiasts shy away from the crypto industry. 3. The Complex And Cumbersome Nature Of KYC The different stages and components of KYC are not easy tasks that can be walked over. Crypto exchanges are having to brace up and look for ways to ensure they are not overwhelmed by the often complex and cumbersome requirements of KYC. Those who have adopted automation and the use of KYC solutions can easily surmount this challenge. For more on KYC Automation, read: Digital Customer Onboarding Process: Why You should Automate your Customer Onboarding Process How Does KYC Work In Crypto? Just like KYC conducted by banks and other financial institutions, the process for KYC in crypto can be broken down as follows: i. Cryptocurrency exchanges collect personally identifiable information (PII) of their customers such as name, date of birth, address and ID number at the point of the customer's registration with the crypto exchange. ii. The crypto exchanges request valid means of identification from the customers, especially government-issued IDs such as National Identity Cards, Driver's Licences or International Passport. iii. The cryptocurrency exchanges then compare the personally identifiable information (PII) collected during registration with the means of identification submitted by the customers. This is to confirm and verify the identities of customers. Recommended: Identity Verification Questions - Frequently Asked Questions On Identity Verification iv. The crypto exchanges conduct searches in official databases of Politically Exposed Persons, watchlists and sanctioned individuals to determine the individual risk profiles of customers and the likelihood of their involvement in financial crimes. v. The cryptocurrency exchanges continuously monitor the transactions of customers for suspicious and unusual transactions to report to the necessary regulatory authorities and help law enforcement agencies to track criminal financial activities. Conclusion As much as ensuring anonymity and protecting the privacy of crypto traders is at the core of cryptocurrency, verifying customers' identity, conducting due diligence and monitoring customers' transactions continually are crucial KYC requirements that must also be considered. When the requirements of KYC in crypto are adopted and upheld by cryptocurrency exchanges, their processes become more standard and safe. People would have more confidence in a crypto exchange that conducts KYC than in one that does not. See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for KYC and AML screening of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today.Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"KYC in Crypto - Why does it Really Matter?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/kyc-in-crypto","id":619},{"content_hash":"4634ba019c27bca1f6aa0948d26f1408b400d8a64124bd735bc6fadf9d360d24","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:48.192875+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/how-to-get-a-proof-of-address","language":"","text":"How To Get A Proof of AddressByLola, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 17, 2023•5mins ReadWhat is Proof Of Address?Why A Proof Of Address Is Necessary For Businesses?Achieving Know Your Customer (KYC) Address Verification with YouverifyShare this postProof of address might sound somewhat familiar already, especially in association with financial institutions such as banks, when opening a new market. The proof of address is a requisite part of the KYC (Know your customer) process. KYC is a regulatory requirement that financial institutions and other businesses must comply with to prevent money laundering, fraud, and other illicit activities. What is Proof Of Address? The term “proof of address” is somewhat explanatory. It is evident that the stated address of an individual is real. It verifies the address of an individual. Proof of address documents are usually required in KYC processes. These include utility bills, bank statements, government-issued documents, and letters from recognized entities. Proof of address is a document or set of documents that demonstrates an individual's current residential address. It is used to confirm the individual's identity and verify that they live at their provided address. It is important to know that the documents must be recent and have a current address on them. Some businesses may require specific types of proof of address documents, depending on their internal policies and regulatory requirements. For example, a bank may require a recent utility bill or bank statement as proof of address when opening a new account. Proof of residency is used in conjunction with other forms of identification that connote the KYC process, like a passport or a driver's license. The documents, together, confirm the identity of their customers and comply with regulatory requirements. Why A Proof Of Address Is Necessary For Businesses? Proof of address or proof of residency is an important component of KYC, just like every other component. KYC, of course, refers to knowing your customer, which is a verification process required of banks and other regulated entities. The proof of address documents is used to verify the identity and addresses of customers and assess the potential risks associated with them. This is done as a precaution for illicit activities like money laundering. Financial institutions and other regulated entities are required to perform ongoing monitoring of their customers to ensure that they remain in compliance with laws and regulations. KYC involves collecting and verifying personal information about a customer, such as their name, address, date of birth, and identification documents like passport or driver's license. However, there are automated ways to do that now. In South America, KYC regulations and practices can vary from country to country. However, many countries in the region have implemented similar KYC standards to those in the USA, particularly in the banking and financial sectors. For example, in Brazil, financial institutions are required to verify the identity of their customers through a variety of methods, such as electronic verification, physical documents, or in-person interviews. Similarly, in Argentina, financial institutions are required to verify the identity of their customers using a variety of methods, such as electronic verification, physical documents, or in-person interviews. Proof of address is required by businesses to verify the identity and address of their customers, clients, or suppliers. This is an important security measure that helps prevent fraud and ensures that the business is dealing with legitimate individuals or entities. This is one of the reasons why proof of residency will always be necessary for businesses. Let's explore a number of reasons why proof of address is necessary for a business : a. Compliance with regulations: Many businesses are required by law to verify the identity and address of their customers or clients to comply with anti-money laundering (AML) and know-your-customer (KYC) regulations. b. Prevention of fraud: Requiring a proof of address helps prevent fraud by ensuring that the business is dealing with a real person or entity and not a fictitious one. c. Contact information: Businesses need to have accurate contact information for their customers, clients, or suppliers to ensure that they can communicate with them when necessary. d. Credit checks: Some businesses may need to perform credit checks or other background checks on their customers, clients, or suppliers to assess their financial risk. ProofProof of address can help confirm their identity and assess their creditworthiness. e. Delivery and shipment: Some businesses may require proof of address to ensure that their products or services are delivered to the correct address. This is especially important for online businesses that ship products to customers. f. Tax purposes: For businesses that deal with vendors or clients overseas, proof of address may be necessary for tax purposes. This helps the business to ensure that they are complying with international tax laws and regulations. g. Insurance requirements: Some insurance policies may require proof of address to determine coverage eligibility or to process claims. This is particularly true for home insurance policies, where the address of the insured property is a crucial piece of information. h. Rental agreements: Landlords and leases may require proof of address from prospective tenants to verify their identity and assess their ability to pay rent. This helps to protect the landlord from potential rental scams or non-payment of rent. How To Get A Proof of Address Many individuals wonder how to get proof of address or how to get proof of residency when preparing for compliance processes. There are several alternatives that businesses can use to obtain proof of address documents from customers for KYC purposes. Here is what counts as proof of residency to meet regulatory obligations. 1. Utility Bills A common option when asked how to get proof of address, as it directly shows residency. Request the customer to provide a recent utility bill in their name, such as a water, electricity, or gas bill. This should have the customer's name and address mentioned on it. 2. Bank Statements Widely accepted; many ask, “is a bank statement proof of address?” Yes, it often is, provided it is recent. Request the customer to provide a recent bank statement that shows their current address. The statement should not be more than three months old. 3. A Government-Issued ID Some IDs also double as proof of residency when they contain the holder’s address. Government-issued IDs like passports, driving licenses, etc., also contain the address of the holder. 4. Rental Agreement Request the customer to provide a copy of their rental agreement or lease. This should have the customer's name and address mentioned on it. 5. Postal Mail Request the customer to provide a recent letter or package they received at their address. 6. Digital Verification With the advent of technology, digital verification services are also available in the market, which helps to validate the customers' addresses by cross-verifying their provided data with their digital footprint. 7. Credit Report If the customer has a good credit history, they may have a credit report that includes their current address. 8. Tax Documents Request the customer to provide a recent tax document, such as a tax return or assessment notice. This should have the customer's name and address mentioned on it. 9. Employer Verification If the customer is employed, their employer may be able to provide a letter verifying their address. 10. Online Verification Some companies may use online address verification services that check the customer's address against a database of addresses or use geolocation to verify their location. 11. Face-to-Face Verification Businesses may also choose to verify the customer's address in person by conducting a face-to-face meeting or inspection.Each of these provides businesses with options on how to get proof of address securely while ensuring compliance. Achieving Know Your Customer (KYC) Address Verification with Youverify Youverify has a range of software services that provide identity verification services, including address verification. The flagship product of Youverify, YV OS, is made to assist companies that handle sensitive data or must adhere to rules. Included in them are KYC and AML, two crucial compliances that could result in severe penalties from regulatory authorities. The YV OS, most notably, reduces the time and labour costs associated with performing physical identification verification yourself, saving your firm money. Accuracy is also guaranteed due to our elaborate agent network that manually verifies the provided address. See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for KYC and AML screening of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. book a demo today. Related ArticlesView All PostsAddress VerificationLola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•May 3, 2023What is Address Verification Service (AVS)?Read MoreAddress VerificationEmmanuel Agwu•June 15, 2023Digital Address VerificationRead MoreAddress VerificationEmmanuel Agwu•August 1, 2022REVEALED: Financial Institutions Need To Conduct Physical Address Verification Or Face Up To N20 Million Fine - Here's WhyRead More","title":"How To Get A Proof of Address","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/how-to-get-a-proof-of-address","id":635},{"content_hash":"6fb5ac2a68f69f9e08c56d83e1e94a03d9dc290500984e5f8da46535a060afbd","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:46.619381+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/10/01/ophk-troops-kill-terrorists/","language":"","text":"OPHK Troops Kill 16 Terrorists, Recover Firearms in Adamawa By Prnigeria - October 1, 2026 OPHK Troops Kill Terrorists OPHK Troops Kill 16 Terrorists, Recover Firearms in Adamawa Troops of the Joint Task Force, North East, Operation HADIN KAI (OPHK), have neutralised at least 16 terrorists and destroyed another terrorist concentration during separate operations in Adamawa State. The operations, conducted between September 30 and October 1, 2026, also led to the recovery of several arms and ammunition, while troops repelled coordinated attacks on military formations in Garkida and Garaha. The Theatre Command, in a statement issued on Thursday by the Acting Military Information Officer, Headquarters JTF North East, Capt. Mohammed Goni, said the first major encounter occurred in the Boima general area of Gombi Local Government Area. According to the statement, troops conducted a coordinated ambush against a terrorist enclave in the area, resulting in the neutralisation of 16 terrorists. It said troops subsequently exploited the engagement site and recovered three AK-47 rifles, two pump-action rifles, one Dane gun, six AK-47 magazines and a belt of pump-action cartridges. The statement added that the Air Component of OPHK also carried out a precision air strike after an Intelligence, Surveillance and Reconnaissance platform detected about 16 terrorists gathered alongside 15 motorcycles beneath a tree canopy in Sector 4. It said the target was identified and acquired before the air interdiction was conducted at about 5:30am on October 1. Read Also: Navy Rescues Two Kidnap Victims, Recovers Stolen Engines in Akwa Ibom Nigeria Enters Age of Prosperity: FULL TEXT of President Tinubu’s 66th Independence Day Anniversary Speech Emir of Zazzau Backs NAHCON’s Reform Drive, Calls for Sustained Excellence in Hajj Administration “Subsequent Battle Damage Assessment confirmed the successful destruction of the target, while few surviving elements scattered in disarray,” the statement said. Meanwhile, troops deployed to 226 Battalion in Garkida and 232 Battalion in Garaha repelled coordinated attacks launched by insurgents against their positions. The military said the troops responded with overwhelming firepower, forcing the attackers to abort their mission and withdraw. However, three soldiers were killed during the fierce engagements, while wounded personnel were evacuated and are receiving medical treatment in stable condition. The Theatre Command said follow-up exploitation and mop-up operations were ongoing across the affected areas. It attributed the latest operational successes partly to the ongoing restructuring of Headquarters Theatre Command, which it said was aimed at strengthening joint integration, improving operational synergy and ensuring greater inclusiveness of relevant stakeholders in the counter-terrorism and counter-insurgency campaign. The command said the renewed approach was already producing operational gains through improved coordination among land, air and other supporting components. It reaffirmed its commitment to sustaining offensive operations against terrorists and leveraging intelligence, technology and joint capabilities to deny hostile groups freedom of action across the North-East. The Theatre Command also called on members of the public to remain vigilant and provide timely and credible information to security agencies as military operations continue across the region. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSKill 16 TerroristsOperation Hadin KaiRecover Firearms Previous articlePHOTO: Troops Rescue 5 Abducted Women, Recover Rustled Livestock in Katsina, Sokoto Prnigeria","title":"OPHK Troops Kill 16 Terrorists, Recover Firearms in Adamawa","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/10/01/ophk-troops-kill-terrorists/","id":644},{"content_hash":"898f0b90862d9a177f398afd8f5fa7816e7906136c61fb9a28704f7633091461","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:44.469133+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/kyc-aml-compliance-for-user-onboarding","language":"","text":"Step-by-Step Guide to KYC/AML Compliance For Onboarding in the USByTemitope LawalJune 16, 2024•5mins ReadWhat are KYC AML Compliance Regulations in User OnboardingWho Needs to Comply with KYC/AML Regulations?What is the Role of FinCEN in KYC/AML Compliance?Step-by-Step Guide to KYC/AML Compliance in User Onboarding in the United StatesBest Practices for KYC/AML Compliance for User OnboardingConclusionShare this post Today, user onboarding is all about speed and convenience. Customers expect a frictionless sign-up process. But for businesses in the financial sector, striking a balance between user onboarding and regulatory compliance can seem difficult. Know Your Customer (KYC) and Anti-Money Laundering (AML) regulations ensure it is not a playground for money launderers and terrorist financiers.Money laundering, estimated at €715 billion to €1.87 trillion annually (2-5% of global GDP), poses a significant threat to the global economy, according to the UNODC.The US is a prime target for these illicit activities. To combat this threat, KYC AML regulations play a crucial role in safeguarding the financial system and protecting legitimate businesses.This article will equip US businesses requiring digital identification services with the knowledge and tools for KYC AML compliance when onboarding their new customers and employees. We'll go into the regulations, explore the role of key players, and provide a step-by-step approach to achieve a compliant and user-friendly onboarding process. What are KYC AML Compliance Regulations in User Onboarding KYC AML stands for Know Your Customer/Anti-Money Laundering. These regulations are designed to prevent financial crime by requiring businesses to verify the identities of their customers and monitor their transactions for suspicious activity.The fight against financial crimes like money laundering and terrorism financing is a global effort. The US has established a powerful framework through the Money Laundering Control Act of 1986 and its participation in the Financial Action Task Force (FATF). These regulations aim to prevent criminals from using the financial system for illicit activities. A guide to KYC AML compliance will help you understand how to implement these regulations in user onboarding. Who Needs to Comply with KYC/AML Regulations?A wide range of financial institutions in the US are subject to KYC anti-money laundering regulations. Here are some key players who need to be compliant:• Banks: This includes traditional banks, credit unions, and online banking platforms.• Money Service Businesses (MSBs): These businesses provide services like money transfers, check cashing, and currency exchange.• Insurance Companies: Insurance providers are required to implement KYC AML procedures to prevent fraudulent insurance claims.• Brokers/Dealers in Securities: These entities dealing with securities like stocks and bonds need to comply with KYC AML regulations.• Domestic and Foreign Financial Institutions with US Operations: Even foreign financial institutions with a presence in the US must adhere to KYC/AML rules. What is the Role of FinCEN in KYC/AML Compliance?The Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), a bureau under the US Department of the Treasury, is the main regulatory body overseeing AML compliance. FinCEN plays an essential role in the following ways:• Issuing Regulations: They establish and enforce Anti-Money Laundering Counter-Terrorism Financing (AML/CFT) regulations for financial institutions.• Collecting Data: FinCEN collects Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) and Currency Transaction Reports (CTRs) filed by financial institutions, acting as a repository of financial intelligence for law enforcement.• Enforcing Compliance: FinCEN investigates and penalizes institutions found to be non-compliant with AML regulations.Further reading: A Comprehensive Guide to AML Compliance Reporting Step-by-Step Guide to KYC/AML Compliance in User Onboarding in the United StatesNow that we understand the context, let's dig into the practical steps for achieving KYC AML compliance in your user onboarding process: 1. Establish Policies and Procedures:• Develop a risk-based AML program tailored to your specific business model and the risks associated with your customer base and industry.• The program should outline your approach to customer identification, verification, risk assessment, transaction monitoring, and reporting.• Consider seeking guidance from legal professionals or AML compliance consultants to ensure your program aligns with the latest regulations. 2. Customer Identification Program (CIP):Implement a Customer Identification Program (CIP) to collect essential information from your customers. This includes:Full NameDate of BirthAddressGovernment-issued ID number (e.g., Social Security Number) 3. Document Verification:Verify the collected customer information with reliable documentation. This may involve obtaining and verifying:Government-issued photo IDs (driver's license, passport)Documents confirming your addressAlso, consider using digital verification providers like Youverify to streamline this process. Youverify utilizes advanced document verification technology and secure data connections to ensure the authenticity of submitted documents, saving you time and resources. 4. Risk Assessment:Customer type (individual, corporation)Geographic location (higher risk jurisdictions)Source of fundsTransaction history 5. Ongoing Monitoring:Continuously monitor customer transactions for suspicious activities that could indicate money laundering or terrorist financing. Red flags to watch for include:Transactions exceeding expected activity levels and lacking a demonstrable economic justificationTransactions involving high-risk countries or individualsUnusual activity patterns compared to established customer behaviour 6. Reporting:File Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) with FinCEN when you suspect illegal activity. SARs are crucial for law enforcement agencies to investigate potential financial crimes.Additionally, certain cash transactions exceeding a specific threshold require filing Currency Transaction Reports (CTRs) with FinCEN.Following the above KYC?AML compliance for user onboarding can be easier when organizations make use of the best digital customer onboarding software. Best Practices for KYC/AML Compliance for User Onboarding 1. Streamlining the KYC process is key to maintaining a user-friendly onboarding experience that fosters high conversion rates while adhering to regulations. Here are some additional tips:2. Simplify the Process: Aim for a user-friendly onboarding experience. Leverage digital solutions like Youverify to automate document verification and minimize friction for your customers.3. Stay Updated: The regulatory outlook is constantly developing. Regularly review and update your AML program to ensure compliance with the latest FinCEN guidelines.4. Invest in Technology: Consider utilizing digital verification providers like Youverify. Youverify offers a suite of KYC/AML tools that streamline customer onboarding, enhance data security, and free up your resources to focus on core business activities. Youverify's solutions are built with compliance in mind, ensuring your adherence to KYC AML regulations. ConclusionKYC/AML compliance might seem frightening at first, but with the right approach, it doesn't have to be a hindrance. By following the steps outlined above and embracing technological advancements like Youverify's digital verification solutions, you can achieve a flawless onboarding process that meets regulatory requirements and provides a positive user experience. A compliant and secure onboarding process is about safeguarding your business, protecting your customers, and building a foundation for sustainable growth.Ready to simplify your KYC/AML compliance and simplify your user onboarding process? Visit Youverify today! We offer a range of digital verification solutions custom-fit to meet the specific needs of your business. Let Youverify help you achieve effortless compliance and focus on what matters most to growing your business. Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"Step-by-Step Guide to KYC/AML Compliance For Onboarding in the US","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/kyc-aml-compliance-for-user-onboarding","id":652},{"content_hash":"f02484b33bc20ebc9b8e7e54c74946666c4119eb6cb2c0b56bce2414b7156433","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:42.620510+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/30/emir-zazzau-backs-nahcon/","language":"","text":"Emir of Zazzau Backs NAHCONâs Reform Drive, Calls for Sustained Excellence in Hajj Administration By Prnigeria - September 30, 2026 NAHCON Emir of Zazzau Backs NAHCONâs Reform Drive, Calls for Sustained Excellence in Hajj Administration In a significant endorsement of ongoing efforts to strengthen Nigeria’s Hajj administration, the Emir of Zazzau, His Royal Highness Ambassador Ahmed Nuhu Bamalli, has pledged the support of the traditional institution to the National Hajj Commission of Nigeria (NAHCON), while urging the commission to sustain its reform agenda and operational efficiency in the management of Hajj affairs. The revered royal father made the commitment during a courtesy visit to the Chairman and Chief Executive Officer of NAHCON, Ambassador Ismail Abba Yusuf, at the Commission’s headquarters in Abuja. The visit underscores the growing confidence of critical stakeholders in NAHCON’s ongoing transformation efforts and highlights the pivotal role traditional institutions continue to play in fostering public trust, pilgrim education, and community engagement across the country. Speaking during the meeting, the Emir commended NAHCON’s leadership for its determination to improve service delivery despite the enormous complexities associated with coordinating one of the world’s largest annual religious gatherings. He noted that the successful management of Hajj operations requires foresight, transparency, discipline, and meticulous planning, particularly in a country with one of the largest pilgrim contingents in Africa. Describing Hajj as a sacred obligation that deserves the highest standards of care and organization, the Emir emphasized that the welfare, safety, and spiritual fulfilment of Nigerian pilgrims must remain at the heart of every policy and operational decision. Read Also: Gombe PDP Suspends Mailantarki, Garry over Alleged Anti-Party Activities after Governorship Primary Nigeria @66: FG Pushes for Growth, Jobs, Improved Living Standards Military Records 367 Attacks, Kills 52 Terrorists in September “We are here to offer our prayers, counsel, and support to NAHCON. We also urge the Commission to sustain its drive toward structural efficiency, early preparations, and transparent communication to overcome recurring operational challenges,” the Emir stated. He further assured that the Zazzau Emirate and the wider traditional institution would continue to support initiatives aimed at sensitizing intending pilgrims and promoting compliance with Hajj guidelines, thereby contributing to a smoother and more successful pilgrimage experience. Responding, Ambassador Ismail Abba Yusuf expressed profound appreciation for the visit, describing it as both historic and encouraging for the Commission’s management and staff. He noted that the endorsement and counsel of respected traditional leaders provide valuable support for the Commission’s efforts to continually improve service delivery and deepen stakeholder engagement. The NAHCON Chairman briefed the Emir on a number of strategic initiatives being implemented by the Commission, including policy reforms, digital innovations, and partnerships designed to enhance operational efficiency, reduce costs, improve accommodation and feeding arrangements, and ensure a more seamless airlift process for pilgrims. Ambassador Yusuf reaffirmed the Commission’s commitment to building a modern, responsive, and pilgrim-centred Hajj administration system capable of meeting global best practices and the expectations of Nigerian pilgrims. He assured the Emir that the observations and recommendations made during the visit would receive due consideration as the Commission intensifies preparations for future Hajj operations. The visit is widely seen as a demonstration of the strong synergy between traditional leadership and public institutions in advancing reforms that directly impact the welfare of citizens, while reinforcing confidence in NAHCON’s mission to deliver a world-class Hajj experience for the Nigerian Muslim Ummah.This version adopts a stronger public relations angle by emphasizing stakeholder confidence, institutional endorsement, reform momentum, and the strategic significance of traditional leadership support. VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSEmir of ZazzauHajjHis Royal Highness Ambassador Ahmed Nuhu BamalliNAHCON Previous articleGombe PDP Suspends Mailantarki, Garry over Alleged Anti-Party Activities after Governorship Primary Prnigeria","title":"Emir of Zazzau Backs NAHCONâs Reform Drive, Calls for Sustained Excellence in Hajj Administration","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/30/emir-zazzau-backs-nahcon/","id":661},{"content_hash":"193d804e0c88a7c8bc4d2df9c637b40c836f97c00c5e18562f4173d33b14742f","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:40.635791+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/identification-verification-authentication","language":"","text":"Identification vs Verification vs Authentication: What's the Difference?ByEmmanuel Agwu (Edited by Favour Praise)March 15, 2023•5mins ReadKey TakeawaysIdentification, verification, and authentication each serve a distinct role in the identity verification process, answering \"Who are you?\", \"Can you prove it?\", and \"Can you access this system?\" respectively.Identity verification combines document verification, biometric verification, and other trusted methods to confirm a user's identity, reduce identity fraud, and support KYC and AML compliance.Authentication protects verified users by requiring passwords, one-time passwords (OTPs), biometric authentication, or multi-factor authentication (MFA) before granting access to systems or accounts.Using identification, verification, and authentication together helps businesses strengthen customer onboarding, prevent fraud, protect sensitive information, and deliver secure digital identity management.","title":"Identification vs Verification vs Authentication: What's the Difference?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/identification-verification-authentication","id":672},{"content_hash":"73b9f3c3d54d7707f0c7d041e4a1805f9f361b854a49147767f62081eed8c3af","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:38.677709+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/10/01/independence-text-president/","language":"","text":"Nigeria Enters Age of Prosperity: FULL TEXT of President Tinubuâs 66th Independence Day Anniversary Speech By Prnigeria - October 1, 2026 President Bola Ahmed Tinubu FULL TEXT: President Tinubuâs 66th Independence Day Anniversary Speech INDEPENDENCE DAY ADDRESS TO THE NATION BY HIS EXCELLENCY, PRESIDENT BOLA AHMED TINUBU, GCFR, 1ST OCTOBER 2026 My fellow Nigerians, Sixty-six years ago today, our flag was raised, for the first time, over a free and sovereign nation. Since then, we have journeyed together as one nation, bound by our collective trust in the great promise of Nigeria: that a country of our size, our bountiful resources, our cultural diversity and, above all, our extraordinary people can build a giant in Africa; that in this blessed and fertile land we can build a united, strong and prosperous nation; a land of regional authority and global consequence. Our journey has not always been easy. In truth, it has generally been too hard for too many. We have known moments of great hope and periods of profound disappointment. We have endured war, military rule, economic crises, insecurity and political upheaval. Yet through it all, Nigeria, with the indomitable spirit of its people, has endured. It has endured through the efforts of the farmer who rises before dawn to work his land. The trader who opens her shop each morning, hopeful for a productive day. The teacher who believes in the children entrusted to her care and the young entrepreneur who refuses to surrender ambition to circumstance. We are grateful for the men and women of our Armed Forces and security services who place themselves in harmâs way so that the rest of us may live in peace. Through every difficulty, generations of Nigerians have continued to believe in one thing: that this country of ours will be better. This faith, which sustains us all, is the inheritance of independence. Our founding fathers did not struggle merely for a flag, an anthem or a place among the nations of the world. Independence carried a larger promise: that Nigerians would have the freedom to shape their own destiny and build a nation capable of providing opportunity, dignity and a better life for its people. Sixty-six years later, Almighty God has made it our responsibility to deliver that promise. Today, as we celebrate our independence, we must speak honestly about the journey so far, the choices we made, and the country we are determined to build from here. For too long, the promise of Nigeria was undermined by choices that postponed difficult decisions and allowed deep economic distortions to grow. By 2023, poverty was rising, and hope was nearly gone. The countryâs situation was darker than ever. We had no choice but to act. Nigeria was like a sick patient who receives the terrible news that he has cancer. His doctor explains that the treatment will be difficult and painful, but that it offers a strong prospect of recovery. The patient has a choice. He can begin treatment, endure its discomfort and fight the disease. Or he can ask only for morphine, dull the pain and leave the cancer to spread. For too long, Nigeriaâs leaders chose morphine while praying for a miracle that never came. They focused on symptoms while allowing the disease to take hold deep within the fabric of our society. We spent enormous sums sustaining inefficient arrangements that were never intended to last. We hid from difficult truths and passed the consequences from one generation to the next. When this Administration assumed office, we resolved to do things differently. We chose to excise the cancer. The reforms that followed were difficult. The side effects were real. Yet, we must never confuse the medicine with the disease. Our reforms did not create the weaknesses in our economy. They confronted them. Now, as certain influential but regressive voices would have us abandon the treatment and return ourselves to the abuse of addictive subsidies, we must resist their siren song. We must remember why we began this journey and how far we have already come. Three and a half years later, the evidence that Nigeriaâs economic outlook has improved is undeniable. Our economy has grown by over 4 per cent this year. Both oil and non-oil sectors have contributed to the renewed period of stable growth. Oil theft is down. Inflation has fallen substantially from its peak. Our foreign reserves have been rebuilt, our foreign exchange market has stabilised, and in 2025 this country recorded its highest revenue from non-oil exports in its history, exceeding $6 billion. This is real money being made by real Nigerian businesses. These are not idle claims. International observers, journalists, NGOs and multilateral institutions can see the change. Each has concluded that our reforms have strengthened Nigeriaâs economic stability and resilience. The private sector has long since delivered its verdict, and foreign direct investment continues to rise each year. My fellow Nigerians, we have reached a turning point. The emergency treatment is over. The foundation has been repaired. The central economic task before us has changed. For three years, our overriding purpose was to correct our nationâs course. Now, our purpose is simple: shared and widespread prosperity. When I speak of prosperity, I do not speak merely of a larger economy, abstract numbers or better statistics. I mean something much more personal. I mean a Nigeria in which the farmer can cultivate his land safely, produce more at lower cost, and earn a decent return for his labour. A Nigeria in which factories have reliable power, businesses can obtain credit, and young people can find productive work. A Nigeria in which food and transportation are affordable, education is within reach, and hard-working families can look towards the future with confidence. This is the promise we must now fulfil. Our priority is to bring down the cost of living. We will achieve this by lowering the cost of producing and moving the things Nigerians consume. Read Also: Navy Rescues Two Kidnap Victims, Recovers Stolen Engines in Akwa Ibom FULL TEXT: President Tinubuâs 66th Independence Day Anniversary Speech Emir of Zazzau Backs NAHCONâs Reform Drive, Calls for Sustained Excellence in Hajj Administration We have the land and people to feed ourselves. My government is therefore focused on expanding mechanised irrigation and dry-season farming, improving access to seeds and fertiliser, holistically increasing mechanisation, and investing in storage and transportation. We are building and completing the roads, railways and ports that connect farms and factories to markets. Our logic is simple. When a farmer produces more cheaply, when fewer crops are lost between the farm and the market, when a manufacturer spends less on electricity, when a truck reaches its destination faster, and when the business environment fosters fair competition, all those savings will ultimately find their way into the price of goods in the market. But prosperity requires more than cheaper goods. It requires productive work. Nigeria is a young country. Millions of young Nigerians enter adulthood every year with talent, energy and ambition. Our responsibility is to ensure that this great demographic strength becomes an engine of production rather than a source of despair. We are therefore placing jobs, enterprise, and industrial growth at the heart of our governmentâs policies. We will use our gas to power new industries. We will support businesses that work to bring factories back to life in our great industrial centres. We will expand digital connectivity into communities that have waited too long to participate in the modern economy. We will invest in the skills employers demand and support Nigerian businesses with the infrastructure and finance they need to grow. I want to see more Nigerians making things. I want to see more Nigerian farms feeding our cities and supplying our factories. I want to see Nigerian businesses selling Nigerian goods to the whole world. I want young Nigerians building unicorns and creating opportunities for others here at home. I am also conscious that millions of our fellow citizens cannot wait for tomorrow. Some Nigerian families still struggle today for the next meal, the next school fee, the next medical bill, or simply enough money to get to work. Their circumstances did not begin with the reforms of the last three years. Their struggle is the accumulated consequence of decades of low productivity, inadequate infrastructure, insufficient opportunity and institutions that too often failed those who needed them most. We cannot erase in four years what accumulated over generations. But we can change its course. We can build an economy that steadily lifts people out of poverty while ensuring that those who remain vulnerable are not abandoned along the way. That is why we are strengthening direct support for the poorest households and improving the National Social Register so that assistance reaches those who genuinely need it. It is why the Nigerian Education Loan Fund is ensuring that the child of a low-income family need not surrender the dream of higher education simply because his parents cannot afford the fees. It is why CREDICORP is giving working Nigerians access to consumer credit, allowing people to acquire vehicles, solar systems, digital devices and other essential assets without first having to accumulate years of savings. That is why, working with our states and local governments, we will continue to strengthen primary healthcare, basic education, and the essential public services poorer Nigerians depend on most. Since 2023, we have paid salaries and pensions on time and in full. We have reformed the national pension program to benefit retirees and the vulnerable. These programmes are not substitutes for prosperity. They are a bridge to aid our nationâs citizens on their path towards it. Our objective is not to manage poverty more efficiently. We will defeat it. It will take time. It will require discipline. It will require sustained growth year after year and the creation of millions of productive opportunities across our country. But for the first time in decades, we embark on this task from a position of strength; with an economy whose fundamental direction has been corrected. We confronted the difficult choices we faced and have laid the foundations for lasting prosperity. Rather than fail the promise of a better future for our children, the time has come for us to build that future. The age of reform has done its work. Now begins the age of prosperity. An age in which the promise of this great nation must finally become the lived experience of Nigerians from all walks of life. We have travelled through difficult years together. We have made hard decisions together. And now, still together, we must build the country those decisions have made possible. I believe deeply in what lies ahead. I believe in the millions of ordinary men and women whose work, courage and determination have always been the true strength of our country. The road ahead will still demand much from us. But we now travel it with firm footing, a clear direction, and renewed hope in our collective future. Nigeria has corrected its course. We have passed through our own Red Sea. This is not the time to look back. Let us go forward together, with faith in ourselves, faith in our country, and faith that the sacrifices we have made will yield their reward. The Promised Land before us is a Nigeria of abundance and opportunity; a nation where prosperity is broadly shared, where every child can dream beyond the circumstances of his birth, and where our countryâs immense promise is finally reflected in the lives of our people. Our destination is in sight. Our foundations are strong. Our direction is clear. So let us go forward. No looking back. Happy Independence Day, my fellow Nigerians. God bless you all, and God bless the Federal Republic of Nigeria. Bola Ahmed Tinubu, GCFR President and Commander-in-Chief of the Armed Forces of the Federal Republic of Nigeria By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSIndependenceNigeriaTinubu Previous articleEmir of Zazzau Backs NAHCONâs Reform Drive, Calls for Sustained Excellence in Hajj AdministrationNext articleNavy Rescues Two Kidnap Victims, Recovers Stolen Engines in Akwa Ibom Prnigeria","title":"Nigeria Enters Age of Prosperity:  FULL TEXT of  President Tinubuâs 66th Independence Day Anniversary Speech","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/10/01/independence-text-president/","id":684},{"content_hash":"c93e8b608280a459af500bcd205788da2d3b8095b546c3aad098d6f1397222d6","domain":"son.gov.ng","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:37.788898+00:00","final_url":"https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/17/son-university-of-ilorin-collaborate-to-promote-standards-and-innovation/","language":"","text":"SON, UNIVERSITY OF ILORIN COLLABORATE TO PROMOTE STANDARDS AND INNOVATION The Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON) has reaffirmed its commitment to institutional collaboration as it welcomed the Vice Chancellor of the University of Ilorin, Professor Wahab Olasupo Egbewole, SAN, on a strategic engagement visit its laboratory complex in Ogba ,on the 9th of April. Receiving the Vice Chancellor and officials of the University of Ilorin, the Director General/Chief Executive of SON, Dr. Ifeanyi Chukwunonso Okeke, who was represented by the Head, Mechanical/Quality Control, SON Laboratory Services, Engr. Abayomi Danmola, emphasized the importance of partnerships between regulatory agencies and academic institutions. He noted that such collaborations are essential to fostering innovation, promoting quality education, and supporting the development of national standards. In his remarks, Professor Wahab Egbewole expressed gratitude to SON for receiving the university’s delegation on short notice. He stated that the university is focused on enhancing learning experiences and is confident that this objective will be better achieved through collaboration with SON. The Vice Chancellor further emphasized that meaningful knowledge is gained when all processes and procedures are well structured. He expressed confidence that with access to SON’s fully equipped laboratory facilities, students’ knowledge will be significantly enhanced, and the standard of learning outcomes will be greatly improved. The visit marks a significant step toward building long-term relationships that will drive sustainable national development through shared expertise and institutional synergy.","title":"SON University of Ilorin Collaborate to Promote Standards and Innovation","url":"https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/17/son-university-of-ilorin-collaborate-to-promote-standards-and-innovation/","id":689},{"content_hash":"9138574d9006472a88e135a8992c8f04ca3996ea394be72ba09fad51a70881fd","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:36.117286+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/employment-verification-documents","language":"","text":"What are the Acceptable Employment Verification Documents? ByPrince, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 15, 2023•5mins ReadWhat Are Employment Verification Documents?List of Critical Documents for ComplianceDifferences between List A and List B of employment verification documentsWhat are the Benefits of Employment Verification Documents?Effects of Non-Compliance With Employment Verification DocumentsBest Practices for Employers Navigating Compliance With Employment Verification DocumentsFAQs on Employment Verification DocumentsFinal ThoughtsShare this postAs an employer, it is important to be aware of the laws and regulations surrounding employment verification documents and how they can affect your business. Employment verification documents are essential for any business to remain compliant, yet they can be complex. This guide provides a comprehensive overview of the different types of documents needed, how to obtain them, and how they must be stored. We also explained the advantages and disadvantages of using an electronic system for verification. What Are Employment Verification Documents?Employment verification documents are any form of document or type that can demonstrate an individual's eligibility for employment. Generally, these types of documents provide proof of legal work authorization, such as a Social Security card, a driver's license, or other government-issued photo identification. These are often listed under I-9 acceptable documents, which outline what forms can be legally presented during the verification process.In the United States, employers must request certain documents from employees to comply with federal immigration laws. This process is formally managed using the I-9 form documents, which are designed to confirm both identity and work authorization.Employers need to understand that not all documents obtained from their employees will provide full compliance with federal employment verification requirements. Depending on the type of document provided by the employee, an employer may be required to verify additional information—such as an employee’s Social Security number—to remain compliant.Most employers use I-9 documents to verify employment eligibility and complete the necessary verification process. The I-9 form requires an employee to present valid form of identification documents, such as a driver’s license or passport, to prove their identity and legal right to work in the United States. This process allows employers to ensure their compliance with U.S. immigration laws while also helping them identify and eliminate fraudulent applicants from their hiring process. List of Critical Documents for Compliance When it comes to meeting the standards of employment verification documents, there are a few critical documents you must have in place: 1. Employment Verification Form Used to verify an employee’s identity, this form is often filled out in the employee's presence and signed by them as well as a company representative. 2. Income Verification Forms These forms verify that an individual earns a specific amount of money while employed with your business. They contain information such as the employee’s name, address, salary, and length of employment. 3. W-4 FormAs a way to ensure tax compliance, the W-4 form determines how much income tax should be withheld from an employee’s paycheck and allows you to report information on the worker to the IRS. 4. I-9 Form This form is used to confirm a new hire's identity and authorize them for employment within the United States. This form requires employees to submit i-9 documentation that verifies their identity and work eligibility such as Social Security numbers, driver’s licenses or passport numbers, or other appropriate documents as listed on the I-9 form instructions page. With updates like the I-9 form 2024, employers must stay informed about current requirements. 5. Tax Credits Employer Forms Employers need all applicable forms that enable their workers to claim applicable credits for certain types of wages earned such as wages for tips received or unemployment wage credits, etc. 6. Applicable State Forms Depending on where the business is located, employers may also be responsible for completing state forms such as those related to state withholding taxes or state benefits programs like unemployment insurance or workers' compensation coverage forms. Having all of these documents in order is critical in ensuring you stay compliant with relevant tax laws and regulations so take Recommended - What Information Can be Released For Employment Verification? Differences between List A and List B of employment verification documents Employers must use two distinct sets of documents when verifying employee identity and work authorization: List A and List B. Deciding which list to use requires careful consideration, but making the wrong choice could lead to violations of federal immigration regulations. When verifying an employee's identity, employers must present a document from List A, the larger of the two lists. These forms of identification prove both identity and employment eligibility. The most common documents used in this category are passports and driver's licenses.List B documents prove an employee's identity but not work eligibility. Employers who choose to use this list must also administer additional i-9 form documents that verify an individual's employment authorization. Common forms used in this process include Social Security cards and birth certificates. Employers should always err on the side of caution when selecting documents from either list - choosing the wrong document could result in serious penalties, including fines imposed by the federal government or lawsuits initiated by individuals claiming discrimination or unfair treatment. To avoid any potential issues, it is best to consult an expert who can provide guidance on which documents to use for each situation. What are the Benefits of Employment Verification Documents? Employers who take the time to thoroughly review and verify all employment documents during the hiring process reap several benefits that protect both the company and its employees. Below are just a few of the advantages gained by properly verifying employment documents: a. Improved Accuracy in Records Having accurate records on hand makes it much easier to compare past performance reviews and screenings, mitigating potential risks associated with hiring candidates with misrepresented or falsified records. b. Confirmation of Eligibility to Work Confirming that a potential employee is eligible to work by properly verifying documentation allows employers to avoid hefty penalties associated with hiring illegal workers or those with false documentation. c. Openness in Communication By verifying all employment documents, employers can create an open channel of communication between them and their employees, which is key to establishing trust and cooperation in the workplace. d. Reduced Turnover Rate Having proper records and valid authorizations for all employees adds an extra layer of assurance for both employers and employees alike, resulting in better job satisfaction and a decreased turnover rate. A comprehensive document verification process may seem daunting, but it is essential for any employer who wants to ensure full legal compliance as well as healthy working relationships with their team. With Youverify solutions, document verification can be simplified for you to ensure full legal compliance with applicable laws about employment verification documents. Effects of Non-Compliance With Employment Verification DocumentsWhen employers fail to comply with employment authorization documents (EADs) and other applicable government regulations, they risk costly penalties. For instance, an employer who does not request the required additional EAD forms or copies of identification documents may be subject to a civil penalty of up to $2,156 for each instance of non-compliance. Additionally, a willful failure to comply with EAD requirements carries a fine of up to $21,563 per violation. In some cases, employers may even face criminal charges. Depending on the circumstances, the employer can be held liable for fines ranging from $375 to $16,000 and possible imprisonment of up to six months. Non-compliance can also result in suspension or debarment from participating in federal contracting or subcontracting activities for up to one year and can have an extremely negative impact on an employer’s reputation in the industry. To avoid these hefty financial and legal ramifications, employers must make sure their hiring practices are compliant with all applicable laws and regulations concerning employment verification documents. Best Practices for Employers Navigating Compliance With Employment Verification Documents Employers are responsible for ensuring compliance with employment verification document standards, including the I-9 Form. To ensure your business complies with all regulations, there are several best practices to follow when dealing with these types of documents. 1. Train Your Staff Make sure that your employees are aware of the requirements for completing and maintaining employment documents. Have a designated point person who is responsible for regularly updating personnel on the changing regulations. Also, provide periodic refresher courses so that all staff members have a consistent understanding of relevant regulations. 2. Accurate Record Keeping Ensure that you maintain up-to-date records of new hires’ documents and copies of all paperwork related to current employees. Create an organized filing system to make information easy to access when needed. You should also stay informed on any changes in government laws and regulations related to employment verification document standards and make sure existing personnel files meet current guidelines. 3. Stay Organized Staying organized is also key when dealing with employment verification documents. Make sure that you have a timeline for reviewing, approving, and filing all employee documents so that information does not get lost in the shuffle. Additionally, create checklists for each step in the process to ensure nothing slips through the cracks or is forgotten. FAQs on Employment Verification Documents Employment verification documents are a crucial element of any compliance program, helping employers confirm the identity, eligibility status, and associated payroll records of their employees. However, many employers are unsure of what these documents entail and how to accurately complete them.To get a better understanding of how to navigate compliance with employment verification documents, we have compiled answers to some of the most commonly asked questions. What Documents do I need to Verify Employment? The federal government requires employers to collect and retain Form I-9 (Employment Eligibility Verification) from all newly hired employees. This is used to verify the identity and lawfulness of employment for all new hires within the U.S., regardless of citizenship or national origin. Additionally, certain states have additional requirements for additional forms, depending on where you are located. What do I do if an Employee does not have Identification? The employee is required to present forms of documentation that establishes his/her identity and eligibility for employment in the United States as outlined by Form I-9. If the employee does not have identification, he/she must provide alternative forms that combine to establish his/her identity and eligibility for work in the United States instead. How Long do I need to keep Employment Verification Documents? Generally speaking, employers are responsible for maintaining Form I-9s on file for three years after the date of hire or one year after termination, whichever is later. However, it is important to check with your state's laws specifically as they may require longer records retention periods. What is Form I-9, and why is it important? Form I-9 is used by employers to verify an employee’s identity and eligibility to work in the U.S. It ensures accurate identity verification and authentic proof of employment documents during the hiring process. What documents can I use as proof of employment? Acceptable employment verification documents include a U.S. passport, Permanent Resident Card, or a driver’s license with a Social Security card. These satisfy the Form I-9 requirements for proof of employment documents. How does employment verification prevent fraud? The I-9 employment verification process helps detect fake identities by validating official records and confirming document authenticity. This strengthens the overall fraud verification process and maintains workforce compliance. Final Thoughts In summary, employers need to be aware of their obligations when it comes to compliance with employment verification documents and ensure that the necessary i-9 documentation for each employee are on file. While the process can be arduous, the benefits of having the documents accurately completed and stored securely cannot be ignored. By understanding the risks associated with non-compliance and by having clear, up-to-date policies in place to guide you and your employees, you can navigate the compliance process with confidence and have a clear understanding of what your obligations both as an employer and an employee are. Youverify offers reliable solutions to compliance with employment verification documents. Request a demo today to get started. Related ArticlesView All PostsIdentity DocumentsEmmanuel Agwu•May 26, 2023How to Perform Driver's License Verification OnlineRead MoreIdentity DocumentsEmmanuel Agwu•June 6, 2023How To Renew Your Driver's License In South AfricaRead MoreIdentity DocumentsEmmanuel Agwu•April 6, 2022How to check if my National Identity card is readyRead More","title":"What are the Acceptable Employment Verification Documents?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/employment-verification-documents","id":700},{"content_hash":"48162be0707ed3c438ea606c379e5a4a8f8d0ed567f18d2c102bedd23e0397f5","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:34.994667+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/10/01/navy-rescues-two-kidnap/","language":"","text":"Navy Rescues Two Kidnap Victims, Recovers Stolen Engines in Akwa Ibom By Prnigeria - October 1, 2026 Navy Rescues Two Kidnap Victims, Recovers Stolen Engines in Akwa Ibom The Nigerian Navy has rescued two kidnapped victims and recovered several stolen outboard engines during an operation against sea robbers and kidnappers in Ibeno, Akwa Ibom State. The operation, conducted on September 30, followed intelligence reports that suspected criminals were targeting fishermen, seizing their boats and stealing outboard engines. The Navy said the Nigerian Navy Ship JUBILEE Maritime Quick Response Team, Ibeno, swiftly mobilised to the area after receiving credible information about the criminal activities. During the operation, the naval team encountered two boats suspected to be operated by the criminals. The occupants reportedly fled into adjoining creeks when they sighted the naval personnel, triggering a pursuit. The suspects abandoned one of the boats, which contained several stolen outboard engines and the two kidnapped victims. The naval team rescued the victims and escorted them to safety, while the recovered engines were secured for verification and further action. Read Also: FULL TEXT: President Tinubu’s 66th Independence Day Anniversary Speech Emir of Zazzau Backs NAHCON’s Reform Drive, Calls for Sustained Excellence in Hajj Administration Gombe PDP Suspends Mailantarki, Garry over Alleged Anti-Party Activities after Governorship Primary The Navy said efforts were ongoing to track down and apprehend the fleeing suspects. The Director of Naval Information, Navy Captain Abiodun Folorunsho, said the operation demonstrated the Navy’s commitment to protecting lives and securing legitimate maritime activities across the country’s waterways. He said the service would sustain proactive patrols, search-and-rescue operations and intelligence-driven interventions to prevent criminal activities and respond promptly to distress situations. Folorunsho also stressed the importance of credible intelligence and community support in tackling maritime crimes, urging coastal communities, fishermen and other maritime stakeholders to provide timely information to security agencies. He said the Navy would continue to deploy its ships, bases, surveillance systems and maritime quick response teams to safeguard Nigeria’s maritime domain and deny criminal elements freedom of action. The operation comes amid ongoing efforts by the Navy to protect coastal communities and legitimate maritime activities from sea robbery, kidnapping and the theft of fishing equipment. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSNigerian NavyNigerian Navy Ship JUBILEE Maritime Quick Previous articleFULL TEXT: President Tinubu’s 66th Independence Day Anniversary Speech Prnigeria Xing","title":"Navy Rescues Two Kidnap Victims, Recovers Stolen Engines in Akwa Ibom","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/10/01/navy-rescues-two-kidnap/","id":709},{"content_hash":"177ab0902ae95600454aea51397b6a72371be4bbfa1ce77ea3dc49db1390d7fb","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:32.930008+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/merchant-onboarding-process-in-south-africa","language":"","text":"Merchant Onboarding Process in South AfricaByTemitope LawalMarch 14, 2023•5mins ReadWhat is Merchant Onboarding Process?Who is a Merchant Service Provider?What are Merchant Onboarding Solutions?What is Merchant Onboarding Risk?What is the Best Payment Gateway in South Africa- Top Payment Gateways in South AfricaWhich Payment Method is commonly used in South Africa?What is Merchant Onboarding Process Flow?What is Digital Merchant Onboarding- How to Automate your Merchant Onboarding ProcessFrequently Asked Questions on Merchant Onboarding Process in South AfricaDigitize your Merchant Onboarding ProcessShare this post Merchant onboarding process in South Africa is one of the key components of the modern payment gateway. A merchant is required to submit information about its business for review, a process known as underwriting before this merchant can accept electronic payments. As the world continues to move towards a digital future, modern payment gateway and merchant onboarding services are becoming an increasingly important part of the South African economy. In fact, according to Statistica.com, 41% of e-commerce transactions in South Africa were completed using a credit card as of January 2022. This underscores the growing trend of modern payment gateways preferred by South Africans. However, the process can be complex and time-consuming, involving a range of merchant onboarding risk assessments and compliance procedures. In this blog post, we'll provide a comprehensive overview of the merchant onboarding process in South Africa, highlighting the merchant onboarding process flow and offering insights into how businesses can optimize their approach to minimize risk and maximize success. What is Merchant Onboarding Process? The merchant onboarding process is the process whereby payment service providers or payment processors in South Africa bring new merchants onto their payment platforms or marketplace, enabling them to accept payments. These payment processors allow for transactions to happen between merchants and consumer banks. Who is a Merchant Service Provider? A Merchant Service Provider (MSP) is a financial services company that facilitates electronic payment transactions for merchants. They are also known as payment processors or payment service providers. MSPs in South Africa provide merchants with the necessary infrastructure and services to accept various forms of electronic payments, including credit cards, debit cards, and other electronic payment methods during the merchant onboarding process. What are Merchant Onboarding Solutions? Merchant onboarding solutions refer to the processes and tools that enable payment processors to onboard or sign up new merchants onto their payment processing platforms or systems. These solutions help payment service providers streamline the merchant onboarding process, which can otherwise be time-consuming and complicated. Merchant onboarding solutions typically involve a combination of technologies, including application programming interfaces (APIs), software applications, and other digital tools. These solutions enable merchants to submit their information and documentation electronically, which is then verified and processed by the payment processor. As a payment processor, one of the best practices for a seamless merchant onboarding process is using professional merchant onboarding services to onboard new merchants. These merchant onboarding services help to automate your merchant onboarding process and ensure compliance with regulatory requirements. What is Merchant Onboarding Risk? Merchant onboarding risk refers to the potential financial, legal, and reputational risks that a financial institution or payment processor may face when onboarding a new merchant or business onto their platform. When a financial institution or payment processor agrees to provide payment processing services to a new merchant, they assume certain risks. These risks can include fraud, money laundering, regulatory violations, or reputational harm if the merchant engages in activities that are illegal or unethical. To mitigate merchant onboarding risk, payment processors typically implement thorough due diligence procedures, such as background checks, credit checks, and risk assessments, to evaluate the potential risks associated with a merchant. They may also require merchants to provide additional documentation or security deposits to reduce the risk of financial losses. What is the Best Payment Gateway in South Africa- Top Payment Gateways in South Africa The common payment processors otherwise called payment gateways in South Africa include:Payfast Yoco – Best for card paymentsOzow – Best for Instant EFTsZapper – Best for QR codes paymentsSnapScanMobicredPayflexPeach PaymentsPaygatePayGeniusNetcashPayUPaypal2checkoutStripe South Africa In South Africa, the Payments Association of South Africa (PASA) manages a National Payment System (NPS) comprising of over 18 different payment systems which customers and businesses can choose from. These range from low-value card transactions to high-value bond exchange payments. The South African Reserve Bank (SARB) oversees the NPS and is responsible for guaranteeing the safety, efficiency, and settlement of all transactions made by South Africans in the SAMOS system. To protect the stability of the country’s financial sector, the SARB further implements measures to reduce systemic risk within the NPS. Which Payment Method is commonly used in South Africa? There are several payment methods payment processors provide for merchants during the merchant onboarding process in South Africa. They include: CashChecks.Debit cards.Credit cards.Mobile payments.Electronic bank transfersDigital Wallets Among these payment options provided by payment processors, the most common payment option used in South Africa is credit cards. What is Merchant Onboarding Process Flow? The merchant onboarding process flow typically involves several steps that enable businesses or payment processors to effectively onboard new merchants onto their payment processing platforms. The specific steps involved may vary depending on the business and the payment processing platform they use, but generally, the process includes the following: Step 1: Application: A new merchant will apply to accept card payment using a Merchant Account on the payment service provider’s platform he has chosen. The application typically includes providing personal and business information, such as legal name, business name, tax ID, contact information, and banking details. Step 2: Card ReaderThe merchant pays the payment service provider the fees for the chosen Card Reader. Step 3: Due Diligence:The merchant due diligence process is an important step in the merchant onboarding process. It includes: i. Merchant Identity Verification: To comply with AML and KYC requirements for FICA verification, the payment service provider will perform due diligence to verify the merchant's identity, business operations, and financial stability. This may involve conducting a credit check, checking for any criminal history, and verifying the merchant's bank account. Upon completion of these identity verifications, the payment service provider will notify the merchant as to whether or not the application has been approved or declined. If the merchant’s application is approved, the payment service provider will arrange for the Card Reader or any other payment method to be delivered to the merchant’s trading location. ii. Merchant’s Document Verification: There are some FICA requirements for the merchant onboarding process in South Africa. Merchants must provide all FICA documentation! The payment service provider will undergo a verification process on all submitted FICA documents. Merchants must provide the service provider with Fica documents from time to time, as required. Step 4: Integration: Merchants will be required to download the payment service provider app and connect the Card Reader to their Compatible Device.The payment service provider provides the merchant with technical integration information and tools to connect to their payment gateway. This includes APIs, SDKs, and other technical documentation to help the merchant integrate their website or application with the payment service provider's platform. Step 5: Activation: Once the merchant has completed the application, due diligence, integration, and compliance requirements, the payment service provider activates the account and the merchant can start accepting payments. The payment service provider may also provide additional services, such as fraud detection and chargeback management, to help the merchant manage their payment transactions. By agreeing to the terms and conditions of payment processors, merchants automatically authorize payment processors to perform credit and regulatory checks on them and their businesses and/or any other principal, partner, proprietor, or owner of your business. All merchant onboarding process in South Africa may not follow the same exact process, however, there are key steps that must be taken like the FICA document verification. The above are the common merchant onboarding processes payment processors follow in South Africa. What is Digital Merchant Onboarding- How to Automate your Merchant Onboarding Process The digital merchant onboarding process typically involves the use of digital tools and platforms that allow merchants to submit their information and documents online, and for the payment processor or acquiring bank to review and approve the application in a timely manner. This can help reduce the time and cost associated with traditional paper-based onboarding processes. Effective digital merchant onboarding is important for ensuring that merchants are legitimate and comply with regulatory requirements, and for reducing the risk of fraud and chargebacks. It can also help improve the customer experience by enabling merchants to quickly start accepting electronic payments. Today, there are several digital merchant onboarding process in South Africa for payment processors. Frequently Asked Questions on Merchant Onboarding Process in South Africa 1. Can my Card Reader work on another merchant’s account? The Card Reader that is delivered to you is linked to the Merchant Account that you created and will not work with any other Merchant Accounts. 2. When can I start processing transactions on my card reader as a merchant? You can commence processing Transactions from the date on which you have activated the Card Reader but the payout for these transactions will not be completed by the payment service provider until and unless your FICA Documentation has been received, reviewed, approved and electronically stored by the payment processor. 3. Can I transfer my merchant account to another business? Kindly be aware that if you dispose of your business in any manner or there is any alteration in the Authorized Account Holder, principal, partner, proprietor, or owner of your business, you will be required to notify your payment processor in writing about this change and submit all relevant FICA Documentation again. Failure to do so will result in the suspension of your access to the Services, and you will have to complete the merchant onboarding process once more to receive the Services provided by your payment processor. Digitize your Merchant Onboarding Process Take the first step towards modernizing your business by digitizing your merchant onboarding process. By leveraging technology to streamline your workflows, you can improve efficiency, reduce costs, and provide a better experience for your merchants. Don't let manual processes hold you back - make the switch to digital onboarding today and stay ahead of the curve! Join 100+ leading companies to automate your merchant onboarding process with the Youverify workflow automation tool. Try our product to get started. Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"Merchant Onboarding Process in South Africa","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/merchant-onboarding-process-in-south-africa","id":722},{"content_hash":"a465f0c33bafc565250222c18c9523056e038ec4d148f812d6a9c1bedb1d2963","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:30.434386+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-liveness-detection","language":"","text":"Liveness Detection: Everything Businesses Need to KnowByLola, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 14, 2023•5mins ReadUnderstanding Liveness DetectionLiveness Detection Vs Facial RecognitionLiveness Detection TechniquesHow Liveness Detection Enhance KYC Processes?Share this postLiveness Detection has become a critical aspect of securing systems and preventing malicious cyber attacks. Liveness detection is a critical aspect of biometric authentication systems that ensure the authenticity of the biometric data presented during the authentication process. Biometric authentication systems use biometric data, such as fingerprints, facial recognition, iris recognition, or voice recognition, to identify and authenticate individuals. Liveness detection techniques are used in these systems to prevent spoofing attacks, where an imposter presents a fake biometric sample to gain unauthorised access. Understanding Liveness Detection Liveness detection is a method used in biometric authentication systems to ensure that the biometric sample being presented is from a live and present person rather than a fake or fraudulent representation. Liveness detection techniques are used to prevent various forms of attacks, such as presentation attacks or spoofing attacks, where an imposter attempts to gain access to a system by presenting a fake biometric sample, such as a photo, video or mask. The process can be performed using various methods such as facial recognition, fingerprint scanning, voice recognition, iris recognition, or other forms of biometric authentication. The goal of liveness detection is to accurately distinguish between a live person and a fake biometric sample in order to ensure the security and reliability of the authentication system. The procedure is literally an upgrade on token authentication. Liveness Detection Vs Facial Recognition Although Liveness Detection is often confused with facial recognition, it is a distinct process, even though both processes are quite related and are used in biometric authentication systems. Facial recognition refers to the process of identifying an individual based on their facial features. Liveness detection is a biometric authentication process that ensures that the individual being authenticated is actually present rather than a photograph or video. The authentication process is meant to detect signs of life using a variety of techniques like; eye movement, changes in skin tone, and changes in facial expression. The essence of liveness detection is to prevent fraud in biometric authentication systems in banking and payment systems. Liveness detection is a method used to verify that the biometric features being captured are from a live person and not from a spoofing attack, such as a photograph or a mask. This is important for biometric authentication systems to prevent fraud. Related: What is Threatware and How to Protect Against It Liveness Detection Techniques Here are some common techniques used for liveness detection: 1. Facial Movement Analysis This technique looks for movements in the face, such as blinking or smiling, to ensure that the person is alive and not a photograph or mask. 2. 3D Mapping This technique uses a depth-sensing camera to create a 3D map of the face and ensure that it has the necessary depth and contours that would not be present in a 2D image. 3. Thermal Imaging This technique uses a thermal camera to detect heat emissions from the face, which can indicate whether the face is real or a replica. 4. Pulse Detection This technique measures the pulse rate or blood flow in the face to determine whether the person is alive or not. 5. Behavioural Analysis This technique analyses the person's behaviour, such as their eye movements or response to prompts, to detect whether they are a live person or a fake. 6. Fingerprint Scanning Another liveness detection method is fingerprint scanning, where the system verifies the presence of a live person by detecting blood flow in the fingers. The system uses infrared light to detect blood flow and can distinguish between a live finger and a fake fingerprint. 7. Voice Recognition Voice recognition is also used as a liveness detection method, where the system verifies the presence of a live person by analysing the voice patterns. For example, the system can prompt the user to say a specific phrase or word and analyse the voice patterns to ensure that the biometric sample is from a live and present person. 8. Iris Recognition Iris recognition is another liveness detection method, where the system verifies the presence of a live person by detecting eye movements. For example, the system can prompt the user to follow a specific pattern with their eyes, such as looking at different points on the screen. The system then analyzes the eye movements and verifies that the biometric data is from a live and present person. Recommended - What is Identity Spoofing? How Liveness Detection Enhance KYC Processes? Liveness detection is an important technique used in Know Your Customer (KYC) processes to ensure the authenticity of the biometric data presented during identity verification. KYC is a regulatory requirement in many countries, which mandates financial institutions to verify the identity of their customers to prevent money laundering and terrorist financing. In KYC processes, biometric authentication systems are used to verify the identity of customers, such as their facial recognition, voice recognition, or fingerprint scanning. Liveness detection is used in these systems to prevent presentation attacks, where an imposter presents a fake biometric sample to gain access to a financial institution's services. Remote KYC processes using biometric authentication systems have several advantages, such as faster onboarding times and improved customer convenience. However, these processes also pose risks, such as the possibility of presentation attacks, where an imposter could gain access to a financial institution's services by presenting a fake biometric sample. Liveness detection techniques help mitigate these risks and ensure the authenticity of the biometric data presented during identity verification. In addition to the use of liveness detection in KYC processes, it is also used in other industries, such as healthcare and law enforcement, where biometric authentication systems are used for identity verification. The use of liveness detection techniques in these industries has become increasingly important as biometric authentication systems become more widely adopted. There are different ways that liveness detection can enhance KYC, which include: 1. Identity Verification KYC requires customers to provide official documents to verify their identity. This can include a government-issued ID, passport, or driver's licence. This process of identity verification ensures that the person presenting themselves is not using a fake ID or impersonating someone else. 2. Biometric Verification KYC can also include biometric verification, such as facial recognition or fingerprint scanning. These technologies can help detect if the person presenting themselves is using a fake ID or attempting to impersonate someone else. 3. Real-time Verification With the help of advanced technologies, KYC can be performed in real-time, making it easier to detect any discrepancies or inconsistencies in the information provided by the customer. 4. Risk Assessment KYC can also help businesses assess the risk associated with a customer's identity. By analysing the customer's history and behaviour, businesses can identify any suspicious activity or patterns, enabling them to take necessary action. 5. Reducing Manual Intervention With KYC, businesses can automate the process of identity verification, which reduces the need for manual intervention. This, in turn, makes the process faster and more efficient, reducing the chances of errors or mistakes. 6. Enhanced Compliance KYC is an essential requirement for many businesses to comply with regulatory standards. By ensuring that all customers are reasonably verified in accordance with the risk they pose, businesses can comply with laws and regulations related to anti-money laundering, fraud prevention, and data privacy. This is referred to as enhanced due diligence. 7. Real-time Monitoring With KYC, businesses can monitor customer behaviour in real-time, enabling them to detect any suspicious activity immediately. This can include monitoring for unusual transaction patterns, changes in customer behaviour, or other red flags. 8. Enhanced Customer Experience and Security KYC can also enhance the customer experience by simplifying the process of onboarding new customers. By automating the compliance process and reducing the need for manual intervention, businesses can make the onboarding process faster and more efficient. KYC can also enhance the security of a business by reducing the chances of fraudulent activity. By verifying the identity of each customer, businesses can reduce the chances of fraudsters gaining access to their systems or stealing sensitive information. KYC can enhance live detection by verifying the identity of the person presenting themselves and assessing the risk associated with their identity. This process can help businesses prevent fraud, reduce risk, and enhance customer trust. Join 100+ global industry leaders using Youverify for liveness detection screening of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today. Related ArticlesIdentity Verification Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•May 31, 2023What Is A Neo Bank?Read MoreIdentity Verification Temitope Lawal•June 18, 2024How to Protect Your Business from Identity Fraud in the USRead MoreIdentity Verification Hakeem Akiode•March 20, 2024What is a Qualified Electronic Signature (QES)?Read More","title":"Liveness Detection: Everything Businesses Need to Know","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-liveness-detection","id":736},{"content_hash":"ac9e5ea591e86ed377b065ceed9b1cfaffc2cb25f50251619dcf8a7a9307f433","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:27.527417+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-identity-spoofing","language":"","text":"What is Identity Spoofing?ByLola, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 9, 2023•5mins ReadUnderstanding Identity SpoofingWhat Is The Difference Between Identity Theft And Spoofing?How To Identify Identity SpoofingHow To Prevent Identity SpoofingProtecting Your Business From Identity SpoofingBottom LineShare this postIdentity spoofing is a cybersecurity threat that has been on the rise over the years, even with rookie cybercriminals. Basically, identity spoofing happens when a cybercriminal adopts another person's or organization's identity and utilizes it to conduct fraud. Spoof attacks use password assaults and credential capture procedures to steal credentials from customers or clients. Understanding Identity Spoofing Identity spoofing refers to a technique that can be used to mimic or falsify the identity of an individual or an organization so that they can gain access to sensitive data or information, commit fraud, or carry out illegal and mischievous activities. These spoof attacks can also be known as identity impersonation, masquerading, or forging. ID spoofing usually involves using a false identity to gain access to information or data that are restricted from the public. Identity spoofing can be achieved through the following methods: email phishing scams, social engineering (which involves capitalizing on human emotions), and the use of sophisticated software tools that can also be adapted to create realistic-looking false identities. However, email spoofing is much more commonly adopted. The email is used to convince the recipient to reveal sensitive information, which may be embedded with a malicious link or could contain a virus. With email spoofing, the attacker sends an email that appears to be sent from a legitimate source, but of course, a fake email address would be used. IP spoofing is another type of identity spoofing in which a hacker sends network communication from a phoney IP address. This can be used to execute a denial-of-service attack by saturating a target server with traffic while avoiding detection by security systems. The use of bogus websites, which are made to look like authentic websites in an effort to fool people into entering their login credentials or other sensitive information, is another kind of identity spoofing. This type of spoofing attack, known as phishing, can be quite successful if the perpetrator can make a plausible phoney website. What Is The Difference Between Identity Theft And Spoofing? The key difference between identity theft and spoofing is that identity theft involves stealing personal information, while spoofing involves impersonating someone else. However, spoofing can be a precursor to identity theft, as the information gathered through spoofing can be used to commit identity theft or other types of spoofing attacks. \"Identity theft\" and “ID spoofing” are two terms that are often used interchangeably, but they specifically refer to different types of cybercrime. While both involve the unauthorized use of someone else's personal information, there are some key differences between them. Identity Theft: Identity theft refers to a type of cybercrime where someone steals another person's personal information, such as their name, date of birth, credit card number, bank account details, or any other personal data or material that has to do with unique identification. The cybercriminal then uses this information to commit fraud or other criminal activities. Identity theft can lead to serious consequences for the victim, including financial loss, a damaged credit score, and reputational harm. Spoofing: With spoofing, the malicious individual impersonates another person or entity online. This could involve creating fake email addresses, websites, or social media profiles that appear to be legitimate. The purpose of spoofing is often to trick people into providing sensitive information, such as login credentials or financial details, or to spread malware. Recommended - Top 10 Best Identity Verification Software Solutions in 2023 How To Identify Identity Spoofing Identity spoofing can be pretty tricky to identify, especially when an individual is a novice at adhering to cybersecurity. However, there are pretty smart general ways to identify a spoofing scam. 1. Check the sender's email address: Scammers usually make use of email addresses that look similar to legitimate ones but are still not the real email address, so check the sender's email address carefully. Look out for spelling errors or unusual characters. 2. Check the domain name: If the email appears to be from a company, check the domain name of the sender's email address to make sure it matches the company's official domain name. 3. Look for suspicious or unusual content: Be wary of emails that contain urgent or threatening language or ask for personal information, such as passwords or Social Security numbers. It can also include spelling errors or grammatical errors. How To Prevent Identity Spoofing Although identity spoofing cannot be permanently limited, there are some precautions you can take to limit ID spoofing and malicious spam. 1. Use strong passwords: Use complex and unique passwords for all your accounts and change these passwords regularly. Make a strong password by making it at least eight characters, using special characters, lowercase alphabets, capital alphabets, and even spaces. 2. Use two-factor authentication: Use two-factor authentication wherever possible. This adds an extra layer of security to your accounts by requiring a second form of verification, such as a code sent to your phone or a question about your personal life that no one else knows. It could even be an embarrassing detail of your life that you have refused to tell anyone. 3. Be careful of suspicious emails and links: Don't click on links or download attachments from emails or messages from unknown sources. These could be phishing attempts to steal your login credentials. 4. Keep your software up to date: Make sure you are using the latest version of your software, as updates often include security patches. Software that is not up-to-date is often vulnerable to malicious activities from cybercriminals. 5. Use reputable security software: Install reputable antivirus and anti-malware software on your devices to detect and prevent identity spoofing attempts. 6. Be careful with your personal information: Do not share your personal information, such as your social security number or bank account details, unless it's necessary and you trust the recipient. It is necessary that individuals know that. 7. Check your accounts regularly: Monitor your bank and credit card accounts regularly for suspicious activity, and report any unauthorized charges or withdrawals immediately. You never know who has gotten hold of sensitive information concerning your bank accounts. It is important to take these precautions to protect sensitive data about your business, or else your business will remain vulnerable to identity spoofing and even more cyber irregularities. Protecting Your Business From Identity Spoofing Here are some of the best ways to protect your business from identity spoofing attacks: a. Always Verify Email Addresses: A very common method of identity spoofing is email spoofing; this is why it is important to verify email addresses before you send out sensitive information or monetary funds. Intelligent AIs can help business owners verify an email address and alert users if an email address is associated with any malicious or fraudulent activity. b. Adopt Two-Factor Authentication: Two-factor authentication is an additional layer of security that requires users to provide two forms of authentication before accessing an account. KYC tools and measures can help provide secure ways to authenticate users and prevent identity spoofing as well. c. Check For Inconsistencies: Automated KYC tools like Youverify can help prevent identity spoofing by checking for inconsistencies in the information provided by individuals and organizations. For instance, if a person provides a different address or a phone number that was listed in public records, then it could be a sign of identity spoofing. d. Train Employees: Employees should be educated efficiently on the importance of KYC and on how to recognize the signs of identity spoofing, which are crucial in preventing fraud. Employees should be able to verify identity documents and recognize phishing scams and know how to report suspicious activity efficiently. e. Use Identity Verification Services: A very efficient way to prevent spoofing is to use identity verification services that can verify the identity of individuals as well as organizations. These services typically use a combination of document verification, biometric data, and other methods to ensure that the person or organization is who they claim to be. Youverify is an identity verification service that can be trusted, and it is a Reg100 company. It uses automated software to perform a range of identification services that aid KYC. Bottom Line KYC tools are an important component of preventing identity spoofing. By using identity verification services, verifying email addresses, implementing two-factor authentication, checking for inconsistencies, and training employees, organizations can help prevent identity spoofing and protect themselves and their customers from fraud. To see how Youverify can help protect your business from spoofing attacks, book a demo today.Related ArticlesIdentity Verification Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•May 31, 2023What Is A Neo Bank?Read MoreIdentity Verification Temitope Lawal•June 18, 2024How to Protect Your Business from Identity Fraud in the USRead MoreIdentity Verification Hakeem Akiode•March 20, 2024What is a Qualified Electronic Signature (QES)?Read More","title":"What is Identity Spoofing?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-identity-spoofing","id":750},{"content_hash":"0461a689b2488cc5206ea7b850f53e179563a547e80590bbd48c52eda0471738","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:22.410593+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/merchant-onboarding","language":"","text":"Merchant Onboarding: Meaning, How it Works, and Best PracticesByTemitope LawalMarch 9, 2023•5mins ReadWhat is Merchant Onboarding - Merchant Onboarding MeaningMerchant KYC: KYC in Merchant Onboarding ProcessWhat are the Advantages of KYC Compliance in Merchant OnboardingMerchant onboarding: How does Merchant Onboarding work?Merchant Onboarding Best PracticesDocuments for Merchaning Onboarding ProcessWhy you should Automate your Merchant Onboarding ProcessGet Ahead of the Merchant Onboarding GameShare this post Merchant onboarding is an important KYC process for payment service providers because it only means one thing- more money for PSP or payment processors. However, the process of onboarding merchants should be done while observing the required KYC compliance to avoid fraudulent merchants. In 2018, the U.S. Federal Reserve conducted a study which revealed that 174.2 billion non-cash payments were made, amounting to an enormous $97.04 trillion. Unfortunately, this provides a vast platform for criminals attempting to scam, commit fraud, and launder money; these criminals are becoming increasingly crafty with their tactics This is why we have thought it well to provide you with a complete guide on the best practices for merchant onboarding. Here, you will know the meaning of merchant onboarding and how it works. Have a good read! What is Merchant Onboarding - Merchant Onboarding Meaning Merchant onboarding refers to the process of bringing new merchants or businesses onto a payment platform or network. This process involves gathering and verifying merchant information, setting up payment processing systems, and ensuring compliance with relevant regulations and standards. The merchant onboarding process is a critical component of payment processing systems, as it determines the quality of merchants and the safety and security of payment transactions. A robust onboarding process can help prevent fraud and ensure that the payment platform is in compliance with various laws and regulations. Merchant KYC: KYC in Merchant Onboarding Process KYC- Know-Your-Customer is an important aspect of the merchant onboarding process. As a payment processor or payment service provider (PSP), having more merchants who will bring in more transactions is the goal, however, you should be concerned with the quality of the merchants you are onboarding. As the global payment industry is rapidly growing, so is the number of fraudsters and online cybercrimes. KYC in the merchant onboarding process will help ensure that as a PSP, you are able to identify your merchants for who they really are and assess the risks associated with caring out transactions with them. In the merchant onboarding process, KYC (Know Your Customer) is a set of procedures and regulations that payment processors and financial institutions must adhere to when bringing new merchants onto their platforms. The purpose of KYC is to verify the identity of the merchant and assess their risk level, including the potential for fraud, money laundering, or other illegal activities. What are the Advantages of KYC Compliance in Merchant Onboarding The advantages of KYC (Know Your Customer) in the merchant onboarding process are numerous, including 1. Compliance: KYC helps PSPs comply with relevant laws and regulations, such as anti-money laundering laws and regulations related to payment card data security (PCI DSS). 2. Fraud prevention: KYC measures can help prevent fraud resulting from false identities or online scams. By verifying the identity of the merchant, payment processors can reduce the risk of fraudulent activities and protect their customers. 3. Risk assessment: KYC enables payment processors to conduct an adequate risk assessment of the merchant, taking into account factors such as their financial history and ownership structure. This information can help payment processors determine the appropriate level of due diligence needed and mitigate risks.4. Trust-building: KYC can help build trust between payment service providers and merchants by demonstrating that the PSP is committed to providing a secure payment environment. This can lead to increased loyalty and repeat business. 5. Reputation: A robust KYC process in merchant onboarding can help payment processors maintain their reputation in the market by demonstrating their commitment to compliance and customer safety. Overall, KYC is essential in the merchant onboarding process as it helps payment service providers protect their customers, comply with relevant laws and regulations, and build trust with merchants. Merchant onboarding: How does Merchant Onboarding work? The merchant onboarding process flow can vary depending on the payment processor or financial institution or country, There are specific requirements for the merchant onboarding process in South Africa, but generally includes the following steps: Prescreening/ Application stageMerchant KYC/identity verificationMerchant history checkBusiness and operational model analysisWeb content analysisInformation security complianceCredit risk underwritingGo Live and Continual Support1. Prescreening / Application stage: This initial stage of merchant onboarding is carried out by the PSP or payment processor who gathers basic information about the new merchant such as the type of business or location.Upon receiving a merchant application, the payment processor may conduct a quick check to ensure that the application appears legitimate. Although this process is not comprehensive, it can be helpful in identifying and filtering out potential scammers or fraudsters. 2. Merchant KYC/Identity Verification: To initiate the underwriting process, the payment processor conducts a KYC check where small and medium-sized businesses (SMBs) are required to provide various information for verification purposes. This information can include proof of identity and address, bank account details, and business setup documents, among others. Although this process can be time-consuming, it has been simplified due to automation and digitization. Rather than submitting physical copies of numerous documents, most payment processors now offer API-based integrations allowing for direct online application submission. Much of the data can be automatically checked against online databases, resulting in a quicker process. Additionally, any required documentation or clarification can be easily provided online. 3. Merchant History Check: During this stage, the payment service provider (PSP) will examine both your personal and business track record. Merchants’ personal credit history, and your business's financial documents, such as tax returns, are scrutinised to ensure there are no irregularities or red flags. This is necessary to set a benchmark in case your merchant application is approved. For instance, if you run an eCommerce store, and your sales increase significantly, this could raise suspicion and trigger further investigation. The PSP aims to prevent fraud and ensure its clients are trustworthy. 4. Business and Operational Analysis: A business and operational model analysis may be conducted based on the perceived risk of your business, after conducting due diligence. The specific requirements for this analysis can differ among payment service providers (PSPs) or acquiring banks and are typically reserved for high-risk merchants or to assess the long-term financial viability of a merchant's business model. Additionally, a web content analysis may be performed to review a merchant's online presence and content for legitimacy. 5. Web Content Analysis: The web content analysis stage is a part of the underwriting process in merchant onboarding. In this stage, the payment processor may analyze the merchant’s website design, user experience, product offerings, marketing tactics and overall online presence. This is done to assess the risk of fraud, chargebacks and other financial losses. 6. Information Security Compliance: After being conditionally approved, the merchant is moved into the operational phase, where it is crucial to ensure that all transactions adhere to the latest network security standards. Whether you are receiving payments through credit cards, contactless options, or exclusively online methods, your company must fully comply with all requirements. Based on the web content analysis, the payment processor can determine the merchant's eligibility for a payment processing account and set appropriate transaction limits and chargeback thresholds. The PSP may require the merchant to make changes to their website if need be as it is essential for merchants to ensure their website and online content are compliant with relevant regulations and reflect their business practices accurately. 7. Credit Risk Underwriting: In merchant onboarding, credit risk underwriting includes payment service providers confirming that there are no credit risks and that the merchant meets the eligibility requirements for the services offered. 8. Go Live and Offer Continual Support: After ensuring compliance, the merchant may begin accepting sales, signifying the completion of the merchant onboarding process and enabling you to process payments. The payment processor or financial institution should ensure to provide ongoing support to the merchant, including technical support, compliance guidance, and risk monitoring. Overall, the merchant onboarding process can be complex and involve multiple steps. However, a thorough and well-designed process can help payment processors and financial institutions mitigate risk and provide a secure payment environment for their customers. Merchant Onboarding Best PracticesFollow these best practices for a seamless merchant onboarding process: 1. Collect all necessary documentation upfront: Before initiating the merchant onboarding process, ensure that you have collected all the necessary documentation, such as government-issued IDs, business licenses, tax identification numbers, financial statements, and bank account information. Having all the necessary information upfront can save time and prevent delays in the onboarding process. 2. Communicate clearly and regularly: Communication is key during the merchant onboarding process. Keep your merchants informed about the progress of their application, any issues that arise, and what they can expect moving forward. Be sure to provide clear instructions and answer any questions they may have. 3. Streamline the process: Minimize the amount of paperwork and steps required to onboard a merchant. Use electronic forms, automate verification processes, and reduce the number of manual touchpoints wherever possible. This will help to expedite the onboarding process and reduce the likelihood of errors. 4. Ensure security and compliance: Ensure that your merchant onboarding process is compliant with all relevant regulations and security standards, such as PCI-DSS. Conduct regular audits and risk assessments to identify and address any potential vulnerabilities. 5. Provide ongoing support: Once a merchant is onboarded, provide ongoing support to help them navigate the payment process. This can include providing training, troubleshooting assistance, and regular check-ins to ensure they are satisfied with the service.By following these best practices, you can ensure a smooth and efficient merchant onboarding process that meets the needs of your business and your customers. Documents for Merchaning Onboarding Process To simplify the merchant onboarding process for a new PSP, you can initiate the collection of essential documents by requesting them ahead of time. Although this is not a comprehensive list, some typical documents you will require are:Proof of identity for all individuals involved in your company (such as a passport or driver’s license)Proof of addressRegistration documents based on the type of business you run (incorporation documents/certificates, LLP agreements, orRegistration certificates, to name a few)Income tax returnsSalary slipsBank statements Why you should Automate your Merchant Onboarding Process Do you know could spend hours trying to prevent onboarding harmful merchants and still miss an important aspect of your merchant onboarding process? Missing a vital aspect can cause you as a payment provider and your business a lot. However, there is a solution to that. Smart payment service providers now automate their merchant onboarding process. Instead of performing your onboarding process manually, a process subject to many errors, you can automate it using professionals. Get Ahead of the Merchant Onboarding Game Get ahead of the game by implementing digital solutions as a payment service provider. Acquire more merchants without waiting around for traditional compliance systems. Youverify Compliance Workflow automation tool lets you combine 300+ data sources into a single workflow and make automated decisions with accuracy.Join several 100+ companies that digitise their customer onboarding experience.Ready for a digital journey with your merchants? Book a Free Demo with an Expert Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"Merchant Onboarding: Meaning, How it Works, and Best Practices","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/merchant-onboarding","id":772}],"index":{"N":70,"avgdl":1136.3285714285714,"docLens":{"1":914,"17":264,"35":919,"51":783,"68":597,"71":266,"88":958,"96":567,"107":22453,"108":1057,"119":991,"134":980,"151":884,"167":855,"174":498,"181":767,"197":973,"210":1010,"225":1311,"240":911,"253":866,"267":964,"286":1264,"301":520,"309":1048,"317":902,"324":851,"333":834,"340":507,"353":859,"360":1138,"368":3005,"383":1176,"393":800,"403":595,"408":840,"415":217,"423":1089,"429":308,"436":1004,"449":104,"469":870,"479":288,"486":155,"491":2547,"500":406,"510":955,"525":361,"532":1272,"551":877,"568":592,"582":468,"587":881,"595":336,"604":791,"611":372,"619":973,"635":944,"644":418,"652":861,"661":468,"672":99,"684":1337,"689":161,"700":1387,"709":334,"722":1248,"736":977,"750":991,"772":1325},"terms":{"10":{"df":19,"postings":[[1,1],[35,2],[96,1],[107,3],[174,3],[286,1],[309,1],[353,1],[368,2],[403,1],[415,5],[429,1],[469,1],[491,1],[525,1],[551,1],[595,1],[635,1],[750,1]]},"11":{"df":10,"postings":[[107,5],[174,1],[197,1],[286,1],[309,1],[449,1],[469,1],[491,1],[500,1],[635,1]]},"12":{"df":6,"postings":[[35,2],[107,3],[309,1],[368,1],[408,1],[429,2]]},"13":{"df":11,"postings":[[35,1],[107,5],[225,1],[368,2],[408,1],[429,1],[479,1],[491,1],[500,1],[525,1],[595,1]]},"14":{"df":21,"postings":[[17,2],[51,2],[107,7],[119,1],[151,1],[167,1],[181,1],[197,1],[210,1],[253,1],[267,1],[301,2],[309,20],[393,1],[408,1],[436,1],[510,1],[532,1],[568,2],[722,1],[736,1]]},"15":{"df":16,"postings":[[35,2],[68,1],[107,3],[174,2],[309,10],[360,1],[368,1],[408